      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>MY DEAR NELLIE</head>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>I Ud I - 1 i• :</p>
        <p>During the last few months hats have been trim- med very largely with flowers and feathers. The usual way of arranging the former in hats is with one upright and slightly to one side,. and a long one curling over the brim at either side, or else following the line of brim and meeting to fall over the hair at the back. The new toques are enor- mously wide, with brim upturned end trimmed in the centre with a knot of velvet, a large buckle in diamond or steel, and two or three ostrich plumes foaming over on each other. Velvet is much used for hats and toques, and much of the effect is given by the careful curve of the brim, which some clever milliners know how to impart. The white felt hats are very carefully arranged as to curve of brim. Their success depends almost entirely up- on this particular.</p>
        <p>Now we shall soon be thinking of the necessity of muffs, for the cold weather will soon be with us. The Ladies' Pictorial has some specimens. I think I might give you a suggestion, the only one I have at present of the style of muffs. There appsars to be a unanimous opinion in favonr of both boas and muffs being composed chiefly of furry animals. Some of the muffs look like nests, outside which the original owners had left their tails. There are as many as five sticking out of some of them. On the front of some of the muffs are the original owners' heads, which make a very effective ornament. One I saw yesterday had a fox's head for an ornament.</p>
        <p>Coats are now g cut in fanciful designs around the hips. I ought to tell you that sealskin is to be very much worn this winter. I eaw a most tempting coat in this luxurious fur, with a high collitr of Russian sable, lined with markt velvet. The fronts, furnished with revers of sable, opened over a ve.t of scarlet velvet. The short coat will be seen, later On, in various furs, such as broadtail, turned back with ermine, and finished with enormous buttons. Some of the short coats dip down in a rounded point in front. o:;hers are cut away at the waist in curved lines, that give rather a jaunty air to the costume. A vest of full lace front is often visible down the front. Many of the new, tight-fitting waistcoats are embroidered in a pale colour, or white or sulphur-chain-stitch is used for this embroidery, which is sometimes in g,,!cl or whife or crei.in. With fur jackets the halt. avtc•iat is uocarionally in miniver or pure whi,e tor.</p>
        <p>Amongst new materials that I have had my attention drawn to is a new felt, which is called cloth-felt, and, really, it is difficult to decide at a glance whether the characteristics of the cloth or the fa:, prevail. It is soft and pliable, and twists into the innst delicious toques, but at the same time, itnvit;g more euhatanee than cloth, and a depth in its apparance ; it is handsomer than the tex irre of any woollen fabric. Beaver, too, is being used, and when we see it, as we shall, in the company of large tulle rosettes and a bordering of fur, we shall very likely be amazed, though it is a: all times somewhat foolish to allow the freaks in which milliners indulge to cause us any surpriue. The time has now arrived when we shall take up with extreme approbation such winter eoilettes as those bordei ed with fur. They are to be seen a great deal now in many modistes' rooms. The arrangement of the for in one case suggests a capuchin hood, and provides cuffs and the lining of the storm collar. It is a little new again to use the bell-shaped cuff with the turnover of fur that seems to suggest the return of those wide angel sleeves which at one time were extremely modish, and extremely cold, too, though they pretended to be quite otherwise. The way in which the wind contrives to whistle up a wide bell-shaped cost sleeve has only once to be ex- perienced to be thoroughly disliked. There are, strangely enough in the present-day fashions, no violent changes of any sort, and the beat dressmakers are designing their autum gowns on much the same lines as they adapted in the spring. There are several new skirt models, but the long, close, graceful tunic will be quite as fashionable, apparently, as it was in the spring. It is differently trimmed and cut in different shapes, but it follows the same lines. The most elaborate gowns, such as evening, ceiling, or reception toilettes, are made on this model, and are so fashioned that they trail several inches on the ground. The tunic is often long behind and cut in many graceful scallops; or, again, it may fall in several long, much•trimmed vandyke points—a particularly graceful form. A novelty is a short, tight overskirt that ends above the knees. This is cut the same length front and back and with a straight edge. it is not particularly graceful, but has a smart appearance on a certain kind of cloth gown.</p>
        <p>I think the chief alterations upon which fe.shion has been asserting herself have to do se, cur coats and capes, thougn, to be sure, in , part of the year there was a tea tatpive 4!;tcmpt made to introduce the three-quarter lergh Lito favour again. That attempt is now much more strongly demonstrated. I always feel it my duty when I write about three•cparter coats to emphasise my opinion that in L-mdon this model never becomes very popular, and, indeed, there is no reason why it should. It is extremely smart when well made and well worn, but under other conditions it spells dowdine2s of the most pro- nounced type. •</p>
