      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>AS 14 Et MY: FOR MCDOUGAL L'S PATTINT SELF-RAISING FLOUR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>WILLIAM R. (From a pl</p>
        <p>LETCHFC D. iotograph.)</p>
        <p>If you once try Scott's Emulsion you will not wonder at the faith shown in this preparation by others. Scott's Emulsion is not a secret nostrum. The public is told frankly wbat is in it, and why it is superior to other forms of cod-liver oil. Medical practice cannot get along without i cod-liver oil, any more than it can without opium or quinine. The great superiority of Scott's Emulsion is in its perfect manipulation, and in the superior quality and blending of its ingredients. Scott's Emulsion contains cod-liver oil, hypophosphites of lime and soda, and glycerine, blended in such a way that it is perfectly palatable and the oil is easily absorbed into the blood without digestive effort. Among the hundreds of letters received from those who have been convinced of the efficacy of Scott's Em- ulsion is the following, from a gentleman who had been troubled with a cough for four years:- 33, Morden Street, Lowiskam,S.E. "February 3rd. 1899.</p>
        <p>"Gentlemen,—! am pleased to tell you, for the information of all sufferers from coughs, etc., that my cough, which has troubled me for the last four years, is nearly gone, after takinf three bottles of</p>
        <p>7:At that dreadful fear was quiTivir ilispelled by Scott's Emulsl-:,-7:</p>
        <p>your valuable Scott's Emulsion of cod-liver oil. I had at one time feared that my cough's ultimate issue would be consumption, yet am thankful to say that fear is now past. I have recommended Scott's Emulsion to several of my friends, to whom it has proved equally beneficial.—l beg to remain, gentlemen, yours very faithfully, " (S,gned) WILLIAM R. LETCHFORD. The people are becinning to understand .that con- sumption and other wasting diseases develop largely because the system is neglected when it first shows wasting tendencies. The wide range of usefulness of Scutt's Emulsion is due to the fact that it helps nature to build up the bcdy so that it can resist evil influences. Scott's Emulsion is a specific for throat and lung affections, and is the easiest form of the most potent nourishing pro- perties of food. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence 0 cover postage to Scott and Bowne, Limited, Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E.G., and mentioning this paper. All Chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
