      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The betbrothal of Queen Wilbelmina of Holland was again announced last week. Rumour has already betrothed this young woman to half the unmarried princes in Europe. So one or two more or less, u ill not raake much difference.</p>
        <p>The very pretty custom of engraving quaint lines and mottoes inside wedding rings, has some in again, and few lovers now fail to indulge in an inscription of some kind. The custom of wearing rings of this description on the third finger of the left hand, dates back to the time'cif the Egyptians from whom the Greeks borrowed the custom, and handed it on to the Romans. Iron was at one time generally used for wedding rings, and among the very poor rush specimens were quite general wear.</p>
        <p>One of the most curious of old wedding rings is Martin Luther's, a most elaborate affair, containing re- 1 presentations of all the articles used at tbe Crucifixion. The ladder, the cross, the rope, the nails, the hammer, the spear, and the thorns, are all shown on its surface. Imitation gems grow yearly more fashionable, and when one thinks of the anxiety one feels when wearing valuable stones, and the grief one experiences at the loss of some ornament, it is scarcely a matter for wonder that things have taken this turn. One item however is all important. The imitation must be of the very best, and in good taste, otherwise it becomes vulgar. Individuals of the pre-historic type hold up their hands in holy horror at the idea of hockey being patronised by women, but the fact remains, morever her Majesty is pleased to regard the game as a fit and proper one for our sex. Regarding golf, this is still indulged in by many women in the " smart " set. Mrs. Asquith for example is a great golfite. She at one time occasioned much amusement by playing without a hat, but prob- ably she found this a somewhat chilly arran-ement, anyway it is one she has now relegated to the limbo of the past. Many lovers of b;rds will have read with pleasure the new army order, which provides that for the future officers of the Royal Horse Irtillery, Hussars, and Rifle Regiments, are to provide themselves with plumes of ostrich feathers, in place of the osprey plume, now in use. This is a notable result of the complaints which have been made by the Press, that the beautiful white) heron was being gradually exterminated for the adorn- ment of mankind, and still more of wornenkind. It is to be honed that the action which has been taken by the War Office—probably with the personal approval of the Queen—may serve to impress upon the minds of women that it is they who are responsible for the merciless slaughter of these birds. Every smart bodice boasts a bolero ; real or simulated this autumn.and almost every toilette toasts a trimming of lace, in some shape or other. It may take the form of a vest, full, pleated, or plain, of a jabot dawn the front of a vest or bodice, or a collar laid round the shoulders. It may appear about the collar of a fur cape of the ends of a fur muff, and provided it is good invariably forms an ornamentation far in advance of any other, as regards effect and good taste. Of white fur there is quite an epidemic. The women exhibiting a white fox boa with a multitude of tails banging therefrom, are quite as numerous as wen these a short time back, exhibiting boas of ostrich feathers. Muffs to match look like nests, outside which the original owners had left their tails. There are as many as six or seven sticking out of acme of them. This white fur is not generally becoming however,and should be chosen only by those women whose complexions are beyond reproach. When addreFsing the large gathering of women a the Albert Hall last week, Dr. Creighton, touching upon the subject of " Husbands," came in the course of his address to the serious question of what a wife should do when her lord and master comes home sulky. The worst thing he said was to ,be passionate, and he set before his hearers this ideal that "the more sulky the man.tlae more cheerful the wife must be." There can be no doubt that the BiE hop's advice is secnd, but there is a good deal of human nature in wives, and the majority of them will say that his lordship is asking a bard thing. But if they cannot reach the Bishop of London's standard, ehey can at any rate be tactful, and probably the second best thing, if not the best, is to follow the advice tendered with uncomplimentary frankneas by an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
