      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>gm.</head>
        <p>gm.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Speeches on the War.</p>
        <p>A tea and smoking concert were held in the War- rington Liberal Club on Saturday evening in con- nection with the Bowling Club, when the annual distribution of prizes took place. About 39 sat down to tea, and the subsequent proceedings were presided over by Mr. A. 11. Crosfield (president). Amongst those present were Alderman Roberts Councillors Plumpton, Tinnion. F R. Roberts.' Peacocke and Mr T Pemberton (hon eecretary). The recipients of prizes were : Ist, marble clock Mr James T Locker ; 2nd, carvers in case, Mr 11 Daintitb, 3rd, tea eervice,Nlr J Sankey; 4th, dinner service, Mr W Rostron; sth, barometer, Mr A Green- halgli; 6th, cruet, Mr T Critchley ; 7th, Gladetene bag, Mr J Brimelow; Bth, umbrella, Mr F Wilkinson; 9th, copper kettle, Mr NI Knowle-; 10th, hall dozen knives and forks, Mr W Monks; 11th, clock, Mr .1 Parr; 12th, biscuit jar, Mr J Orford. Councillor PLUMPTON said he had very great pleasure in introducing their esteemed friend Mr. Crosfield. (Applause.) He was ever ready to come to any of these occasions and they were all very pleased to see him. (Hear, hear.) Mr. CROSFIRLD having taken the chair, said the first toast was that of "The Queen and the Royal Family." He said it was a toast that needed no commendation to an audience of English people. The Queen had presided over the destinies of our great country and empire during perhaps the most momentous epoch that had ever been in the whole history of civilisation. (Applause.) Mr. a. H. CROSFIELD prior to distributing the prizes, said : It has so often been my pleeeent duty to congratulate priz winners at bowls that I begin to feel in somewhat the same position as those gentlemen at Academy banquets, who have to res- pond year alter tear to the same familiar toasts, and if therefore, following their exalted example, I treat you to a rechauffe of toe remarks you have heard before, you must please make some allowance. If the remarks are not new, the subject is congenial te us ; we have in fact a bias towards it—(laughter)—and to judge from their success, so have a good many Warringtonians. We were very proud when, a couple of years since. the Cemetery Hotel carried off the premier henours in the Championship Competition of the Lancashire and Cheshire Counties Association. (Hear, hear.) That was one feather in our caps. add now Councillor Evans's team has added another. (Cheers.) lam quite sure I may offer him and them on your behalf as on my own hearty congratulations. (Cheers.) Some neopie I know shake their heads over all these games. Tney say we shall lose in the race of international competitioae I prefer to take the Duke of Wellington's opinion of the value of national sports. He said, that the battle of Waterloo was won on the play grounds of Eton ; we , may extend that play. ground. The Duke's figurative remark includes all English manly exercises and out- door pastimes. (Hear, hear.) After all few people need to work more than twelve hours a day, and if a por- tion of leisure hours is devoted to games, the people are all the better for it, both in character and in working capacity. Years ago there was a picture in" Punch" of a man who had been reading all the daily papers on some political question, half of them written one way and the other half the other way. In the end you see him sitting there torn with hopeless indecision. (Laugh- ter.) Well at any rate this is one of those questions on which none of us need have doubt or misgiving, eo let us wish all success to these games, in which Warrington has always played a very respectable part. May our bowling, cricket, and football clubs, athletic sports, gymnastics, oh and golf !—I must not forget golf— (laughter), and all the rest of them go on and prosper. (Hear, hear.) But if these things occupy our lighter moments, it is difficult for Englishmen to meet tegether just now without their thoughts and conversatien turn- ing to the all ab;orbing topic of the day, and in a politi- cal club like this you will not expect me to apologise for saying a few words upon it—the apology might be expected indeed if I did not. Some two or three weeks since I was talking to one of the strongest Liberals in 'these parts, and not unknown in Warrington. He has been to Johannesburg and I believe he is a very gaol judge of the comdition of things that has recently existed in the Cape in regard to the English, Boers, Kaffir,' and blacks. What he said about the diplomatic controversy then in progress simply came to this: that unless England clearly asserts herself in this matter the situa- tion in South Africa will be in:olerable. (Cheers.) I am sure, gentlemen, that was your view, and the view of the large majority without distinction of party. The only doubt one had was whether diplomatic intercourse had not rather resembled a game of blind man's buff— (hear, hear)—whether the two sides had come so nearly together that we might have deserted ourselves and got what we wanted without war. And that made some people doubtful whether in certain influential quarters, war at any price was not rather the object. Mr. Asquith says not, and for anything we know to the con- trary not only the Government, but every one in it have done their beet either to secure equal treatment and just government for Boers and English alike in the Transvaal just as obtains in Cape Colony, or to give the Uitlanders the means, by la five years' franchise, of working out their own salva- tion. President Kruger refused the five years' franchise when it was finally proposed in our despatch, I beieve of the Bth September, unless conditions were added, which, as you know, it was impossible for us to enter- tain. That may have been due to suspicicn, for which, unfortunately, recent history had given the Boers only too much cause; in that case it had been our previous duty to do all we could to allay that unfortunate but natural suspicion and apprehension. Was that done? We shall hear more on that point when Parliament meets- What the Boers and the people of the Orange Free State obviously think, is, that our object has been to get hold of their land, and that perhaps explains this desperate attempt to defy us. But in these matters President Kruger has had authority,and for all we know he was determined all along to stop short of what we really required. People at any rate, were pondering these questions until that astounding ultimatum came from Pretoria the other day. All one can say about it is this, that if President Kruger had that view at the back of his mind all the time, and intended to base his final action on that view of the matter, a peaceful settle- ment of it was out of the question. (Hear, hear.) The delay in permitting the promised alternative proposals must surely have been due not to their being unpre- pared, but to private negotiations made by those in authority in the hope of ensuring the acceptance of those terms when presented. All that however, is over, and now we all wish God speed to the brave fellows that are going out to the Cape, and our one desire is that the war may not be protracted and that at no dis- tant date complete success will reward the grim deter- mination and indomitable pluck which are the glory of our army. (Applause.) The prizes were then handed to the successful com- petitors. Alderman ROBERTS proposed the toast of "Success to the Liberal Club," and asked permission te trespass out- side the limits of the programme by referring to the war in the Transvaal. He said some of them deplored the fact that we were fighting with white men ; men whose sympathies, natural tendencies and dispositioas and whose upbringing were similar to our own. We would rather have Night someone else. There were some of them of the opinion that if it had not been for that misguided and unfortunate despatch of President Kruger'a on the previous Wednesday war might have been avoided. (Hear, hear.) It was his (the speaker's) birthday on Wednesday and without exception it was the most unhappy birthday, because when he read his newspaper all hope of peace had gone. however much they might deplore the causes which had led to the pre- sent rupture there was only one thing to be done now and that was for all parties in the country to support the Government in bringing this mischief to an end. The time for criticism would come afterwards, and if it could be shown that any possible way of escape had been allowed to slip, then he thought those responsible for it would be responsible for the sad and stricken homes in tbis country and South Africa, and would have to give an account of themselves and hold themselves up to the true verdict of the people in this sad and terrible best- ness. (Applause.) The Liberal party would be ae loyal in this crisis as any fire-eating Jingo could be. It was not a time for criticism and fault finding. Their hearts went out to those young fellows who had gone out to South Africa and who were still going out. They wished them well through it all. They would read their news- papers with great anxiety, and it might be with very great sorrow, and their only hope and prayer was that God would bring it to a very speedy termination. (Hear, hear.) Alluding to the reference of the Chairman that he (Alderman Roberts) was likely to be chief magistrate during the coming year, the Alder- man said tie believed the Liberal party in the town had been very much gratified by the change of front—should tie call it ?—on the part of their friends on the other side, and if there was anything which added to the pleasure he felt it was the manner in which it had ben done. (Hear, hear.) The loyalty of the members of,his own party had been shown must distinctly by everyone of them and the way it was shown by the Conservatives added to the pleasure. He hoped they were beginning a day of better things in regard te municipal politics when men would in the future be elected solely on account of their fitness for the position,and when elected they would receive the reward which every honest man bad a right to expect. (Hear, hear.) He looked upon the office of chief magistrate as the most honourable position a man could occupy. He could confess without shame that it had been the object of his ambition for many years, but he did not think it would have ccme quite so soon as it had. He was very proud to accept the position and to receive it by grace of the Conserva- tive party who had given it, he believed, solely and en- tirely as an acknowledeeseent of hard work done by himself and his friends. (Hear. hear.) He saw that in the neighenutine boron-ea of Wigan they had perman- ently settled this question and removed an element of difficulty by deciding that the Mayoralty should be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>rn=iBI2MMIIMINC'.7III</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 2O, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 2O, 1899.</p>
        <p>offered alternately to either party. That was what he wanted to see brought about in Warrington. They had got something in that direction as far as the aldermen were concerned and he hoped they would repeat it in Warrington in regard to the Mayor. (Hear, hear.) lie had now to propose stlCceSS to the Liberal Club and he did so with all his heart. Whatever his duties and responsibilities might be in the future he should always have a warm place in his heart for the Liberal Club. He had been associa- ted with it since its birth in Market-street some:biug like 25 or 26 years ago %hen he held the responsible and not always very pleasant position of the secretary- ship. He had not forgotten the friends who were asso- ciate with them at that time, and he was very pleased to see, while he respected the prowess of the younger members, that all the prizes did not go to the younger men. (Hear, hear.) He hoped Mr. Matthew Knowles would live to . win many a prize and receive them at the hands of their worthy president Mr. Crosfield. (Applause.) He believed that the Liberal Club performed and furnished a very important element in connection with the Liberal Party of this town. He believed it would be a great misfortune, wtaich was net at all likely to occur, if circumstances made it impos- sible to continue a Liberal Club. Out of that suggestion arose this qeeation. was it doing the work they antici- pated it would do P He thought it was, but perhaps not as much as some would like in certain departmen s Club life was that element which provided the social part of it more particularly and so far as the sccial life of the party was concerned he bedeved the club furnished it very successfully. He was very glad that Alderman Francomb was able to preside at a meeting at the Conservative Club recently, and he was also pleased to see the remark a h made as to the respectability of the Conserveive Club. He (Alderman Roberts) ventured to say that such remarks would apply with equal force and strength to the Liberal Club. (Hear, hear.) He did not know another club where there was less objectionable conduct on the part of the members than in connection with th Liberal Club. He had known of clubs where the element of membership was compesed of the aristocracy and where the eonduct of members individually and collectively would not stand very close inv,stigatian. When they remembered that their club was composed of ab3uc 95 per cent of absolutely bona fide woririn:men, who might have an excuse occasionally for being a little extrava- gant in their pleasures because their duties in their works and callings in life were not of the moat agreeable kind, it was something to be able to refl cc that they I stool SD well in regard to the general conduct of the I place. (Hear, hear.) He defied anyone to name a &amp;rob which had stood more firm, honestly aad consistently for what was proper in conduct and le,haviour. (Ap- plause.) lie had spoken of the social aspect of the club, ' and he would now say something on the educational side. He saw several strange faces, there. There was a ' time when he knew everyone in Warrington, and when everyone knew him. ("They do now.") Every timel he came to a meeting of that kind, he saw an accession of strength in the direction of younger men c ening for- ward. He would ask them not to be satisfied with the social benefit of the club, good and necessary as it was. He tnoueht they should do a little more on the eluca- tional side, ens that the club would not only be a rendez- vous for amusement, entertainment, and physical</p>
        <p>pleasure, but a centre of light and leading in regard to Liberali,m in the district. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the men who had woe prizes would try to win ethers,in the regard and esteem of their fellow men, for work done of a more enduring character in regard to the government of the town and country. Though he might not come there so often i was not because his interest in it was waning. It was more for lack of oppertunity than any- thing else. He was a busy man and had very few opportunities of indulging in bowls or anything of that kind. He did a little cycling and also studied ornith- ology on the quiet. (Laughter.) If his public duties debarred.him from going to the club very often during the next twelve months they must not think that he hal declined to be as vigorous a supporter ot t.e Liberal Cause and club as he had been during the whole of his life. He asked them to drink with him success to the Libgral Club. (Applause.) Councillor Trenrote, who replied, said they had no warmer friend in the Liberal party than Alderman Roberts. He congratulated him upon the high office he , had been called upon to fill as the first citizen of the good old town of Warrington. (Applause.) He did not hesitate to say that the Town Council in making that selection bad naade a very wise one. (Hear, hear.) He knew something of most members of the Town Council, and there was no one who could fill the position with more credit to the town and honour to himself. (Applause.) Proceeding to speak of the South African question he said he might hold very different opinions to many of them. It was a subject of vital importance not only to South Africa but very largely to the best interests of this country. As previous speakers had said, this was no time for criticism. He had had however the greatest difficulty in trying to believe that the policy of England had been conducted in the best spirit, and in the heir interests of the nation. It was true his views bad changed somewhat recently. If the spirit of the ulti- matum was the true spirit of the Boers in this mater, then he was not surprised that things had been pushed to the terrible issue they saw to-day. If President Kruger was prepared to threaten us with an ultimatum. what must have been his conduct to Englishoaen and others under his control? He thought there must be something behind it all of which they knew nothing about. This war was a terrible business; but it was their duty to stand by the Govern- ment and by our soldiers and wish that a speedy end might he made of it. (Hear, hear). Speaking of the club, he said he felt proud to be associa- ted with it. It was doing a great social work, and he was glad to say that they were never in a better or healthier position. Not only was it used for social pleasures, but he had beard many exchange opinions on political matters is that club, and their arguments showed that they must have given some time in study- ing the questions. He believed that they would not have had a Liberal party in the town had it not been for the club. They all knew that they were about to lose the services of their esteemed manager, Mr. Mitchell, who had served them faithfully and well for a long number of years. They acknowledg,ei the value of his work and wished him success in his new sphere of life. (Hear. hear.) They would be appointing a new manager shortly, and whoever they selected he hoped the members of the club would give him every support. (Applause) In conclusion, be thanked Alderman , Roberts for the handsome way be had spoken of the Liberal Club. . . .</p>
        <p>Vocal and Instrumental music was agreeably inter- spersed durinz the evening. The artistes acquitted themselves w2ll and were as follows :—Messrs. Brims- low, Butterfield, Chappell, Whittaker, Stubbs and Locker. Mr. T. R. Ford ably dtscharged the duties of accompanist.</p>
        <p>realizing the grave evils.both physical and moral,arising from the practice of young people frequenting public- houses would respectfully urge the Home Secretary to I promote such legislation as shall prohibit all sale of I alcoholic beverages to young people under 18 years of lage, thug giving the same protection to young people in England as is given in Russia,Norway and Sweden. This mew ins' would also remind the Home Secretary that in Canada and the United States such protection is extended up to the age of 21 years." Mr. JOSEPH MOUNFIELD seconded the resolution, and stated that the taste for intoxicating liquors was often formed when children were young. If we could get young people to do without drink until they were 21 years of age, we should net have so many drunkards. Mr. O'BRIEN seconded the resolution and stated that in America there was certainly not as much drinking amongst women. The resolution was unanimously carried. On the proposition of Mr. Mounfield, the services of Miss Shilston were acknowledged, and the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MERIT REWARDED.</head>
        <p>MERIT REWARDED.</p>
        <p>That ral merit is the outstan ling feature of Chivers' Gold Medal .Nlies is a statement which is capable of easy and abundant proof. The fact that so many thou- sands of consumers continue to buy them year after year speaks volumes for their worth, and is a reward which only attaches to merit. Chivers' Jellies are prepared in a healthy Cambridgeshire village, by up-to-date methods, with the most scrupu- lous cleanliness and care and flavoured with ripe fruit juices. The high-class quality and delicious fruity flavour of these dainties gives im- mense satisfaction all round. Here is a tribute from a Free Church Minister : "We have tried many other kinds of Jelly, but have long decided to specu- late uo more, as we have been disappointed each time. We really fled none like Chivers• " A. Wricle- sale Grocer writes : " I have just beers testing other Jellies which have been sent to me for trial, and have &amp;Tided that no other Jelly can compare with: Chivers' both for texture and flavour." A lady says " Without a fault, simply perfection." Another Lady remarks : " I have not found any so good as yours." Here is tivi testimony of The Lancet "An excellent Jelly poves3lng a flavour of qaite a natural character." Sold by Grocers and Stores in palketi, Fltlf-pints 4-d., Pints 1., glees 81. A free sample will be sent on receipc of-post-card. S. Chill's &amp; Son, Elkton, Cam- bridge. Please mention this paper. A THEATRE DESTROYED BY FIRE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The Theatre Royal and Opera House at M. Helens, which was erected nine years ato at a cost of £13,000, and which was one of the most handsome buildings in the town, was completely d-stroyed by fire early on Fri- day morning. The boards have this week been occupied by W. S. Cody's "Klondike Nugget" Company, and after the performance on Thursday night ever7thing appeared perfectly safe when the building was inspected before being closed. At two o'clock,however,a passer-by noticed flames issuing from the stage end ,and gave an alarm at the Central Fire Brigade Station,only one htindred yards away. Superintendent Lyon and the full strength of the brigade turned out with full appliances, but the filmes, fanned by a strong breeze, spread with terrible rapidity, and in a few minutes the ntire building from end to end was blazing furiously. The roof fell in half- an-hour later with a terrific crash, and the flames shot high in the air, illuminating the town and district in the darkness of the night. Despite every effort by the brigate the handsome theatre, which was one of the most sumptuously furnished in the provinces, was com- pletely gutted. and ve(terday morning nothing but bare walls, enclosing a mass of debris in the auditorium, remained. The members of th ! " Klondike Nugget" Company lost their entire stock of propertieg, which were not insured, while , the whole of the theatre permanent accessories were completely destroyed. The entire loss is roughly estimated at about £20,000. How the fire originated is a complete mystery. The building ha at a vcrr handsome frontage in the main street of the town, with the Central Wesleyan Church on one side and the Holy Cross Catholic Church on the other. The Town Hall and manicipal offices are only one hundred yards away, and directly opposite the theatre is the Gamble Institute, erected at a cost of over £30,000. The destruction of the ! theatre is a great loss to the architectural beauties of the ' town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN. ---- Meeting of the Warrington Associa-</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN. ---- Meeting of the Warrington Associa-</p>
        <p>tion.</p>
        <p>A successful tea and mt•ecing were held in the Liberal Club, Bewsey-street, on Monday evening. After tea the fifth annual meeting was held, and the proceedings were presided over by Mrs. Armi- tage. Amongst those present were:—Miss Shilston, Mrs Henry Roberts (hon. treasurer), Mrs Grimshaw (hon secretary), Mrs Richard Roberts, Mrs William Roberts, Mrs Crampton, Mrs Barker, Mrs C!ay, Mrs Walker, Mrs Hill, Mrs Rathbone, Mrs Mounfield, Mrs Dunn, Mrs Hawthorn, Mrs O'Brien, Alderman Roberts, Messrs W Roberts, Dunn, Mounfield, and O'Brien.</p>
        <p>Mrs. ARMITIGH said she was pleased to give them all a hearty welcome. They had hoped tu have Mrs Garnett present, but she wrote to say that although Mr Garnett was much better—and they were very glad to hear that—she felt so unhinged by his illness that she did not feel able to come and speak. Mrs Jackson Holmes was also unfortunately unable to be present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>-Me SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>-Me SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>ltrs. GRINISHAVI in her annual report said that another year's work had been accomplished and they had cause for satisfaction that they were in a properous condition financeally and numerically. Since the last annual meet- ing the committee had worked on quietly and stead- fastly to premote those causes which were and must ever remain dear to the hearts of Liberal women. They had held nine committee meetings and supported the follow- ing Bills :—Sir Charles Dilke's Shoos Bill, Sir John Lub- bock's Early Closing 8.11, Women's Suffrage, Temper- ance, Slavery in the Zanzibar Protectorate, Pro- tective Legislation for Birds and the Amendment of the Divorce Act of 1857. She was glad to tell them that the Shops Seats Bill had been passed and canoe into operation on the let of January next. (Hear, hear). The annual tea which was held on Oct-ter 9 was:exceedingly well attendel. Upwards of 80 of their members were present and a very able address was given by their good friend Miss Dutton, whose absence that evening she was sure they all regretted. In the same meeth their President, Mrs. Armitage, attended a meeting in Liverpool of the Federal Council. In N member Mrs H. Roberts and I%lre. Walker attended a conference held in Blackburn on the " Labour Ques- tion" and " Women's Suffrage," and were most hospitably entertaiaed by the Blackburn A.ssociation. On the 2ilth November a well attended public meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, when the late Countess Mice Kearney gave a very eloquent lecture on " Why women should be Liberals and take part in politics." ktany of them would remember her clever, earnest address. They would all regret the loss of a very able advocate of the Liberal cause. In December their secretary attended a meet- ing of the Liberal Council held in Manchester and in January they held their annual ball. It was a great success, and their thanks was due to a very able and competent cemmitteeof ladies and gentlemen, some of whom were not connected with their aeseciation and therefore deserved special thanks. In May the annual meetings of the Women's Liberal Federation were held in London, and Mrs. Robert Garnett, one of their vice- presidents, attended as a delegate from the association. rkqv were all very sorry she was not with them that evening to give a report of these meetings, owing to the illness of Mr. Garnett. They sincerely hoped he might soon regain his health and strength. A large party (109) started on August 31st for Overton fills. They were unfortunate this year in the weather. After a pleasant drive there the rain came on and prevented them enjoying a ramble over the hills. Fortunately, however, there was ample accommodation under cover, and by indulging in games, &amp;a., they managed to spend a pleasant time together. She hoped they would endeavour to make the coming year even more successful by getting new members and taking an intelligent interest in the questions of the day. They wanted to spread their principles. Some day some of them would have a vote, and they wanted them to use it wisely and well by voting for the Liberal candi- date. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>FINANCILL STATETLENT</head>
        <p>FINANCILL STATETLENT</p>
        <p>Mrs. H. Ronsars submitted the financial report, from which it appeared that the year was started with a bal- ance in hand of £l9 24. 1i i. The tot al income includ- ing the balance was £46 164. and after paying expenses the balance in hand was £2044. 61. Sh e said the report was ot a very gratifying character an d it would be seen that they had slightly increased their balance in hand.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mrs. ARKITAEFE, seconded by Mrs. WALKER, the reports were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS</p>
        <p>Mrs. ARMITAGE was unanimously re-appointed presi- dent on the -notion of Mrs. H. Roberts, seconded by Mrs Clay. And the following appointments were also made : —Vice•presidents, Mrs Crosfleld, Mrs Robert Garnett, Miss Dutton and Mrs Pierpoint ; hon treasurer, Mrs a Roberts ; hon secretary, Mrs Grimshaw ; Committee: Mrs Jackson Holmes, Mrs Shaw Green, Mra Barker, Mrs Cooke, Mrs Walker, Mrs Rathbone, Mrs ,Clay, Mrs Hill Mrs Mouneeld, and Mrs Wyllie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ADDRESS BY 3fD3S SRILSTON.</head>
        <p>ADDRESS BY 3fD3S SRILSTON.</p>
        <p>MiS9 SHILSTON, in the c)urse of an lir:cresting and in- structive address, spoke in the first place of heredity and environment and their influence for good or bad upon the child life of the country. She said she wishe I particularly to refer to one imp ort int phase of the sub- ject, and that was that so many children, sometimes with a taste for and sometimes with an excessive ten- dency to, the excessive use of alcohol, were so often brought up under circumstances which would inevitably lead them into intemperate habits before they reacned the age of manhood or womanhood. She did not hesitate to say that in Warrington there were many children whose parents, though trying to bring them up to the best of their ability, allowed them through carelessness or thoughtlessness to be accustomed in their earlier days to shots and s ninds of the public:- house, and more than that, even to the taste of drink. Not long since some friends tried to get some idea of the number of children who were allowed to go into pubic. houses. In one place in Lancashire, not far from War- rington, the children were counted corning nut of four publie-houses, and in less than two hours 507 children were counted. They obtained the ages of the children and it WAR found that there were 30 only three years old; 51, four; 65, five ; 84 six ; and sa on up to the age of 13. Only eight were of the age of 13. The greatest number were between the ages of five and six. Sea wished to ask them as mothers and women whether such a state of things could be for the good of the coun- try. Everyone deplored, even those engaged in the liquor traffic, the amount of intemperance in the coun- try. Tney saw how intemperance was specially spread- ing among women, and they asked why? She beiievsd that one of the strongest reasons was because they had been allowed as young children to become accustomed to the taste of drink. They might ask why she objected to children going into a public-house. In the first place she thought everyone would acknowledge that the bar of a public- house was hardly the place for a child. (Hear, hear.) The very atmosphere of the place was objectionable,and then the sights and sounds were not such as any child should see or hear. She realized that the licensed vic- tuallers had an exceedingly difficult task to perform, and she believed they did their best. The licensed victualler was most particular about his own children, and surely they might expect him to help them in the matter. When children came away from a public-honse with a bottle el* jug in many cases they took a sip of the drink. She had noticed that herself, and she had a series of snap-shot photographs taken in certain streets and lanes in Liverpool of children coming away from public-nouses,and in some of them children were shown in the very act of taking stolen sips. It was just that little sip hy a growing child that the foundation was laid of intemperate habits. If they kept a child abso- lutely free from alcohol until it was 21 they would not find much intstriperance after that. That was why they should protect the young lives in their midst. She wished to make a special appeal to the mothers because they had the young child life in their bands. She wished them to shield them not only from the influence of drink, but from evil thoughts. It was incumbent upon them to keep their children in good, pure, holy thoughts. Proceeding to review what had been done in Liverpool by the magistrates with a view to preventing the sale of intoxicants to children under 13 years of age, she said the Committee who approached the magistrateson the subject wrote to them asking them to accede to three requests : (1) to grant no new licenses; (2) to close back-door entrances to cer- tain houses ; (3) to do all in their power to prevent children being served in public-houses. The speaker spoke of the incalculable good which had followed the adoption of these reforms in Liverpool, and said she was glad to know that the action at the magistrates with reference to the sale of drink to children had been taken up is whet towns. In London, nothing could be done in this direction, and imperial legislation was necessary. Such legislation was in force in !tussle, Norway and Sweden with beneficial results. In Canada and the United States no minors were served with drink and she attributed the small percentage of drunkenness amongst women as being due to this law. She wished them to petition the Home Secretary to bring us up to the level of Manchester in this direction and had pleasure ' in moving the following resolution :—" That this meet- ing of the Warrington Women's Liberal Aseociatinn.</p>
      </div>
