      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>GOSPEL MISSION AT PARR</head>
        <p>THE GOSPEL MISSION AT PARR</p>
        <p>HALL</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Thorne, who has conducted a week's gospel mission in the Parr Hall, on Saturday gave a lecture entitled "From the stage to the cross ; or the story of my life." Mr. S. B. Stringer presided, and amongst those present were the Revs. John Yonge, E. T, Re d and J. S. Hughes. Mr. Thorne gave a most interesting account of his life. He said that when he was young his father became connected with various theatrical speculations in different parts of the country and in consequence, the members of his family had free access to the theatres. He (Mr. Thorne) used to go behind the scenes and watch the play there, and he had vivid recollections of some who were great in the theatri- cal world. Once his father produced a piece called" The illustrious stranger" and a number of little boys were re- quired to represent aborigines of some distant land. It was his lot to appear on that occasion and he went through the performance with the proud consciousness that he had taken the first step up the ladder that would lead to the fame he so ardently desired. His father shortly became lessee of several theatres in different parts of the country including one at Warrington and in their journeys to and fro they used to come across various phases of life and that proved instructive to him. His mother used to teach him to go to church and the first church he remembered going to was one at which the service was ritualistic. He had a recollection of priests walking up and down and of his being tremendously interested as he listened to the music till he fell asleep. He was told that he was carried out in disgrace; he bad confounded the ritualistic service with a performance at the theatre. He did not think it was to be wondered at if the same mistake was made to-day. Ritualism was more like a theatrical performance than the worship of Almighty God. (Applause.) In those days they did not think very much of pious people. They thought there was a great deal of hypocrisy amongst people who professed religion. A lady once induced him to go to St.. John's Presbyterian Church at Warrington. He remembered the minister very well. There was a marvellous solemnity about his statement of the gospel. There was one thing that he said which was destined to pro- ' duce a great impresslon upon him (the speaker). He said there were some people who liked the "Family Herald" better than the Bible. After the service he at once went to the house in Bewsey-street where he was staying,got out his Bible and read for hours the epistles ' of St. Paul. It was beyond his power, however, to con- ceive the strange words and he shut the book in despair. Mr. Thorne further related how he was prepared for ' confirmation, and after reading certain parts of the Bible he came to the conclusion that he should leave the stage. He did so, and after for some time being a Sun- day school teacher, he commenced to preach in the streets. Later he NM appointed a travelling secretary and evangelist to the Y.M.C.A. This position, however, he gave up to do the work in which he was now engaged. Although he thought the stage was no place for him, he was not of the opinion that all actors and actresses were the most degraded people in the world. That was not true. Some actors and actresses worked hard and were very kindly people. In spite of their surrounaings they maintained a degree of respectability that might put to blush many people who were in posi- tions more favourable to moral ,and spiritual life. He, however, thought that the right side of the theatre for Caristian people was the outside. (Applause.) A collection was taken on behalf of the Y.M L.A. and a vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Stringer for pre- siding. During the evening, the United Band of Hope choir, under the conductorship of Mr. Walter Brough, sang several hymns. Mr. Thorne conducted a service in the Emmanuel Church, Bold-street. on Sunday morning, and in the afternoon he delivered an address to young men in Parr Hall, entitled" Man without God." He also conducted a gospel meeting in Parr Hall in the evening. On Mon- day two meetings, at 3 p.m. and 7.30 p.m., were held In Wycliffe Church, Bewsey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CRUELTY TO A PONY AT WARRINGTON. ---- Shocking Revelations.—" A Bag of Bones."</head>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A PONY AT WARRINGTON. ---- Shocking Revelations.—" A Bag of Bones."</p>
        <p>On Monday at the Warrington Police Court, Dennis Dolan, 24, Ellen-street, was charged with ill-treating a pony by neglecting to supply it with sufficient food and water. Mr. A. Browns prosecuted on behalf of the R.S.P.C.A., and defendant, who pleaded not guilty was represented by Mr. A. J. Willett. Mr. Browne said that the defendant was the owner of two horses and a pony.and on the 2ad October Inspector Canning having heard of the wretched condition of the pony went to defendant's house in company with P.C. Turner. Defendant was absent,so they went to a stable opposite the house. They found the pony tied to the wall by a piece of string. It was in a deplorable condi- tion and was devouring sawdust and its own dung. They tried to get it out of the stable and it fell down twice. It was practically a bag of bones. Some hay was obtained and the animal in trying to reach it fell down again. They searched the stable but found there was absolutely no food. The pony ravenously ate the bay supplied by the Inspector and the Constable. The following day, Inspector Canning took Mr. Diann, veterinary surgeon to see the animal, but it had disappeared. The Inspector said ha had come to examine the pony, but defendant said he had not got one. He then informed him that he had seen it the previous da.y and that it was the same animal that he had been convicted for illtreating at Newton. Defendant then said Well it is boiled up by now." Jo- specter Canning concluded that it had gone to the knacker's and on making isquiries found it at Mr. Toole's where it had been left at 9 o'clock the night before. It was in the yard gnawing a wooden box. After it had been destroyed, Mr. Dixon made a post- morteni examination and found that the stomach was empty of solid food. The animal weighed 1581bs, where- as it should have weighed 360 lbs. It was therefore, not half the weight it should have been. It was clear that it had been starved to death. The Inspector subse- quently saw the defendant and told him that he had seen the pony and that Mr. Dixon said it had been starved to death. Defendant said he told the inspector a lie before. He did not want to g, t into trouble and asked him to let him off. Inspector Canning asked him why be load starved the pony and he said ha had given it chaff and potato peelings to eat. Defendant had kept the animal in an open space without food or water, and it was only put in the stable when defendant beard that the inspector was coming. He had been determined that , the pony shotild be punished, and was no doubt having his revenge for what occurred at Newton. Inspector Canning gave evidence bearing out this statement. He said that the pony had a breast like a chicken's. The bones were protruding through the skin, and he could span its neck with his hands. When Ise brought the hay it came towards him as though it would almost have eaten kino. He was present at the post-mortent examination. There was a little fresh grass in the pony's stomach. There was no fat what- ever, and the men could hardly skin it. It looked like a dead grey hound. Be never knew such a case in his life.</p>
        <p>P.C. Turner corroborated. He said he never raw a case like it before. The pony ate the hay as though it had never had any for twelve months. John Warburton, 6, Ellis-street, said he was in the habit of going along the railway embankment at 13(.,wsey to his work. He passed the place where de- fendant's pony was kopt, two or three times a day. It was kept in a shed without any roof on. There was no bedding whatever. It had been kept there for two months. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Dixon, veterinary stsrgeon, said he saw the pony when it was alive. It was merely a bag of bones. It was from thirteen to thirteen and a half hands in height and was lame in the fore limbs. After it had been des- troyed he made a post•mortent examination. rhere was a little fluid in the stomach as though the animal had had a drink. There was not a patch of solid food, and no fat whatever. There was not enough to" grease a gimlet." It had no muscles. Ic weighed 153 pounds whereas it ought to have weighed at least 3 cwts. Two men could lift it. and they took it in a wheel barrow to be weighed. Mr. A. J; Willett for the defence, said he was in- structed that the pony was not more than ten bands high. An order for its destruction was made at Newton but defendant's son had persuaded him not to destroy it. On the 2nd of October, defendant's son gave the pony a bundle of grass before going to St. Helens. He submicted that if the animal was only ten hands high 158 lbs was a reasonable weight.</p>
        <p>Dennis Dolan, the defen-dant, said that the pony bPlonged to him but he never worked it. It was at the most ten hands high. He had not destroyed it became his son did not want him to do so.—Cross-examined by Mr. Browne : At Newton he promised in open court t destroy the pony, and on that account he was let off with a small fine. fie had found plenty of sawdust</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>* -47,-**A4L*-41,*,*„*"_*,.*:* *</p>
        <p>*</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>*Ta. * .*-7* *-*</head>
        <p>*Ta. * .*-7* *-*</p>
        <p>and straw for bedding, and did not keep it in the open all night. Neither his wife nor the neighbours had asked him to destroy it. The pony was always well attended to.</p>
        <p>Frank Dolan (15), son of the defendant, said that on the Saturday previous to the Inspector's visit he went to Warburton's farm at Daliam and got a small cartload of grass for the pony. He gave it a bundle on the Monday morning. He had heard his mother say she would get his father into a "bother " for not destroying it.</p>
        <p>James Cliffe said he had visited the defendant's stable on many occasions. He had kept horses for some years. Defendant's pony was not more than 10 hands high, and he thought 15131b3. was a proper weight. It would not have weighed more if they had fed it as fat as they could. He saw defendant's son feeding it three or four days be- fore the Inapectn's visit. Cross-examined by Mr. Browne.—He had been fined 33 61 for cruelty Co a horse. The magistrates retired to consider their decision and upon their return, _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Alderman Francomb), said they had very carefully considered the case and were bound to admit that it was one of the worst they had ever heard. There was no excuse whatever, for him not having the pony destroyed, and they had no doubt that the cruelty was proved up to the hilt. Defendant ould be fined £5 and costs or in default of payment would have to go to gacl for two months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>* * AX</p>
        <p>'to</p>
        <p>creased wonderfully and Catholic churches were multi- plying everywhere. The work of the propagation of the faith was being carried on in China and other countries. There were plenty who had souls to be saved as Christ diei for all men. The musics portion of the services was excellently rendered by the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. J. Gibbins. The musical part of the Mass consisted of Farmer's "Kyrie,"" Gloria," "Credo " and W. Knight's " Agnus DQI." Mr. J. Gibbins took the bass solo in the latter. Miss A. Richardson rendered Gounod's " Ave Maria" as an offerory piece. The treble solo "Et lacarnatus" in the "Credo" was creditably sung by Miss C. Hems. After the sermon in the evening Miss Fair- dough, and Messrs. J. Gibbins and G. Newton sang " 0 Sacrum." Miss Belliaa ably presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>BAPTIST CHURCH, LATCHFORD. - The autumnal sermons in connection with the Baptist Church, Latchford, were preached on Sunday morning, afternoon, and evening by the Rev Charles Cole, of Bol- ton. At the afternoon services the choir creditably ten- dered the anthem" Show yourself j)yful," and at the evening service "0, core, let us worship." Mr. Wilkinson presided at the organ. All the services were well attended, and collections were taken in aid of the church funds.</p>
