      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. --- ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. --- ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woolridze was the special speaker at the men's service, held in St. Peters's Church on Sunday after- noon,when he VITO an address. Two solos were credit- ably sung by Mr. Peter Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ALL S AIN rs' MISSION.</head>
        <p>ALL S AIN rs' MISSION.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services in cennection with the above mission were continued on Sunday. There were two celebrations of the Floly Communion, at eight and nine o'clock, followed by the service at eleven o'clock, when the Rev. A. F. Waite, assistant curate of the Parish Church, was the special preacher. In the afternoon a children' service was conducted by the Rev J. H. G. Hollingworth, curate-in-charge, who also delivered an address. The collections were in aid of the new chalice fund. In the evening the Rev. F. E. Powell, vicar of St. James's, preached. The total collec- tions amounted to over WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY. There was a large congregation in Wycliffe Church on Sunday afternoon when an open meeting was held in connection with the P.S.A. Society and the men were allowed to introduce members of the opposite sex. Mr. T. Birchall, presided,and de ivered a brief address. He said it was not a mere platitude when he said it was a pleasure to pay them another visit and te see that those gatherings kept up in interest, and that they still had a large membership. He was glad to see so many present on the side of God and righteousness. Tnere were so many on the other side workiag for evil that it was very welcome indeed to see such a large force on the side of righteousness. In looking back at Warrington as it was forty or fifty years ago he saw many advantages which the people enjoyed and which were not then existent. But he was not quite sure that with all our advantages we were the better for it. We had our Seturday after- noons with plenty of ,time in which to get outdoor amusements. He was afraid these games got rather too muct of their time and he questioned whether some of our young men's brains were not going into their feet With all their advantages he was afraid they did not find any more in the Sunday Schools on a Sunday morn- ing than there were formerly. They wanted enthusiatio young workers to give the best of their lives to the service of the master. The great sin of to day was indifference. They cou!d not get young men to be enthusiastic on the side of God. Christianity that did not take the form of manifesting itself in service was a very poor thing indeed. They mid not b 3 of use to their fellows unless they served them. In the school, in the church, in the club, in the workshop every where round about them, wherever their fellow men gathered, there they had opportunities far service. The speaker proceeded to refer to the deplorab!n condition of the masses of our people and to the vice and profanity which existed. There was one thing in which weeeed receded, that was in the matter of foul language. But was this to be wondered at where children drank in profanity with their mother's milk. They ought to try and do something to improve matters. If there were to be happy homes the men must take their share in making them so.—Mr. A. Sykes, of Huddersfield de- livered the address. At the outset be referred to the value of the P.S.A. movement and gave instances of the good werk it was doing. The P.S.A. movement had brought about the cleansing of impure homes and had done much to stop impure language. The religion of Jesus Christ, he pointed out, was not simply to lift them to a higher, and loftier level but it ought to be the means. of making them an influence for good wherever they might be found. The speaker showed from the lives of such men as Bunyan, Carey, Livine,stone, and others, how the religion of Jesus Chrint had induced them to delicate their lives to the good of their fellow men. The religion of Jesus Christ also came to gladden their lives. It was a power not only for the life that is to come but for the life that now is. The Widnes Orpheus Scciety contributed two selections of music which were greatly. appreciated. The " Hallelujah Chorus " was exceed- ingly well performed. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ. The Rev. John Yonge announced that the membership at the present time stood at 574, which was very satisfactory, coneideting that there were other ag,enc!es in the neighbourhood doing the same work. Prizes were distributed to the members to the number of 350. Prayer was offered by Mr. T. Hawthorn. A collectioe was taken on behalf of the Society's funds. CHURCH OF THE SACRED HEART, LIVERPOOL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ROAD.</head>
        <p>ROAD.</p>
        <p>The anniverssry sermons were preached in the above church on Sunday by the Rey. Father Kopp, S.J., St. Francis, Xevierse In the morning high mass was cele- brated and the Rev. Father Kopp preached a sermon on " The Conversion of England." At the evening service, the subject of the sermon was " The triumph of the Church." The preacher said that they found their doz- trines had a wonderful vitality about them bnause they were the doctrines taught by a church fuLl of vitality and vigour. What was more full (A vitality and vigour than the Catholic Church ? Notwith- standing the persecutions, and the insults of all classes of men, still the Catholic Church asserted ite rights as being the church that was planted by our Lord when be was upon earth. Toe church was full of a hidden strength and vitality. It was a oh. rch that had no visible effect on things terrestrial. The faith had been handed down from Christ's apostles. It was a militant church and an aggressive church, and in spite of seemingly deadly blows would always immerge triumphant. On Penticost Sunday,when the Holy Ghost cams down and filled the hearts of the ApoAlea with wisdom and courage, the church immediately sprung into full possession of its power. On that very first day they were told, four thousand people came and made their allegiance to the Church of God, and so they found that as time went on, the true spirit showed itself, and no matter what assaults had awaited it, or what cruel fate it had had in the past the Catholic Church always emerged triumphant. In the first days of the Church they found that martyrs sealed their fate because they would not give up their belief in its doctrines, but now they found the Chut•eh great in strength and power, great in the vigour of its constitu- tion till the very mien; of Christendom were only too glad to subscribe themselvos as children of the Cathore Church. The church had had many a struggle to go through but they were now leavening the minds of men and women. Looking at the world ite it was now, they found that in America the Catholic population had in-</p>
