        <p>have been universally admitted to be A BOON TO: WOMANKIND. They are the best known :remedy !for Anzmia, Giddiness Fulness and Swelling after Meals, Loss of Appetite, Hysteria, Palpitation of the Heart, Debility, Depression, Weakness, Irregularities and all Female Ailments.</p>
        <p>Boxes, 90., I. 10., 23. 9d and 4s. 6d. May be had from Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors everywhere. Do not be persuaded to accept a substitute. If not in stock ask them to obtain them, or to be hail direct from the Pro- prietor—</p>
        <p>Dn. Dl.vm, 309, Portobello-road, Notting Hill. London.</p>
        <p>THERE is no inconsistency The SvliEg in the ehangPd opinions. Round.</p>
        <p>which are held by so many persons in this country who have been active for peace, and who deprecated the commencement of hostilities until every diplomatic move had been exhausted. The change is in the situation itself ~ and it seems to have caused a swinging round which is annoying to some and puzzling to others, The matter may be put very plainly. Until the Transvaal declared war against England and in— vaded British ground there was some hope of a pacific conclusion. The proposals of the British Government had not been presented, and there was a chance left for wisdom and good feeling to operate. The situation is now altered. It is no use talking of peace. We cannot make terms with an invader, who has no real ground for Ins action, and we begin to doubt whether the negotiations of the past few months have not been a mere covering for ulterior aims, irreconcilable with a pacific conclusion. The Transvaal Government has taken a tremendous plunge,'but it had clearly been ma- turing its plans for hostilities, and it was ready to take the offensive. Any British patriot, under these circumstances, could no longer hold the arguments and the position he used and occupied. He had to swing round. He had to support the Government in its efforts to protect Cape Colony from serious ravages, and to think that after all, behind the veil of negotiations, the Boers had been preparing to dispute the supremacy of British power at the Cape. This is exactly what the two Republics are now attempting to do, and the Orange Free State is quite as belliccise as the . Transvaal State. Indeed, the Afrikander move- ment began in the Free State, and the campaign against British power in &amp;nth Africa has been proceeding in the Free State. This fact accounts for the ill-advised readiness of the Boers of the Free State to make common cause with President KRUGER and his Government. It encouraged re-- rsistance to our moderate demands, and it pro- mised assistance without which the Transvaal could hardly have committed its act of madness in ordering British troops out of Btitish territory. This is the new situation. We begin to see the threads of a conspiracy between the two States to, undermine British influence and power. It had not been apparent before that matters had gone so, far. We were free to disbelieve that our para- mountcy was in peril, and we did disbelieve it. New light has dawned upon us, and it has trans- formed our opinions, though it leaves us free to criticise the conduct of negotiations when the clash of arms has come to an end. But the justi- fication of the " swing round" is as complete as it can be. We have been too credulous, too ready to believe that no ulterior danger thredened, and too eager to think that peace had been possible if we had adopted a less irritating policy. A policy of moderation and goodwill has always everything to commend it when a great Power is negotiating with a smaller State. The question we now ask ourselves is, whether it would have had the suc- cess which we ,thought probable. Two years ago it might, when the Boers were not ready to as- sume the offensive. To-day, however, we cannot say more than that, and the country is united, as it ought to be, in repelling an unjust war.</p>
        <p>A GENERAL survey of tho Labour's Four Ideas. present industrial situation</p>
        <p>reveals the fact that labour is working at four ideas. The first idea is that which is called Free Labour, in which the in- dividual workman is left at liberty to make his own bargains, thinking only of himself. The reference is not to be an organisation which pro- claims itself free and acts in a way to convince us that it is a mere antagonistic organisation. There is a large amount of free labour which is not , organised. and that alone is entitled to be re- garded as free. It can take a market-rate of re- muneration, or it can go beneath it. This is the primitive idea of labour, dating from the time be- fore the guilds were formal and before there was competition with slave labour. The idea shows surprising vitality in the rural districts, and in certain occupations. The second idea is that of labour organised into unions. This is a nineteenth- century idea, for the most part, and it is therefore one around which controversy still rages. Its idea is to fix, maintain, and restrict, in order that the workman shall not be without some support in his bargaining. The skilled trades are nearly all organised on this basis, and it cannot be said that the idea has been a failure, though it often involves conflicts and considerable loss of meney in conducting them. The third idea is that of co-partnery, or profit-sharing, which exists in a variety of forms, and is extending until it becomes what we call co-operation, in which there ara no masters in the common sense of the term. Or</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>gotorko, ZufStnesleo, etc.</p>
        <p>faustta fnstruiritnts.</p>
        <p>errlo,</p>
        <p>CHEMISTS HO COMMAND PUBLIC ESTEEM. THREE GOOD QUALITIES YOU MAY DEPEND UPON. (1) THE PERFECT FRESHNESS (2) THE GOOD QUALITY (3) THE FAIR PRICE OF ALL DRUGS SOLD AT BOOTS CASH CHEMISTS.</p>
        <p>Every Branch is under the management of a com- petent man, qualified by examination of the Phar- maceutical Society. The services of a reliable Chemist are thus at the disposal of EVERY cus- tomer whether a Physician's prescription is brought to be dispensed, or the simplest drug is required. For (1) PHYSICIANS PRESCRIPTIONS (2) PURE DRUGS (3) SURGICAL APPLIANCES (4) TOILET ARTICLES (5) HOUSEHOLD REQUISITES BOOTS LjASII CHEMISTS</p>
        <p>take the iced in the towns where they have branches. TOILET ARTICLES.—Many of the branches have a large and elaborate stock of Hair Brushes, Vinai- grettes, Fancy Combs, &amp;c., suitable for presents, in inexpensive or expensive mountings, according to the taste and means ot purchasers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>hopes have bean excited as to the economic and social results of the various forms of this idea. It seems to harmonise with the movement of capital in the direction of companies. The fourth idea is that of Socialism, or the nationally co-operativ3 system, in which tha State is all in all. It is more of a theory than anything else, and most of the attempts to work it out on a small scale have been failures. Yet it has captivations for thousands of persons who feel the burden of the first and second ideas, and have not yet come within the attraction of the third, or are disappointed with qs slow advance. The collision of all four ideas is obvious, and yet they exist side by side, and there is no apparent reason why they should not do so for many years to come. Practical minds will, in the end,prefer the idea which yields the best present results, and the second and the third will hold the field. Workmen are free to form their unions as they please, but they are not free to form co-partnery concerns unless masters are willing to offer terms. It seems to us that the warfare the unions necessitate will become a powerful force in the direction of compelling the treaty of peace which profit-sharing implies. Wages are a part of the protit,but not the whole,and they are often unequally divided. The Socialist aims at a better division of profits, and so he is at one with the co-partnery idea or with the purely co- operative idea. That he fails to see it, that he is unwilling to accept anything less than his full idea and that the profit-sharors and co-operators are strong enemies of Socialism, cannot be denied. Large social changes. however, are of slow growth and practical men ought, in their own interests, to give the preference to the scheme which yields the larger profit to the individual. Profit-sharing appears to do this. It is a mild attempt to realise a kind of Socialism, and we predict for it a more extended scope and usefulness in the new century which is so close at hand.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain has very properly taken a strong line about the expenses of military con- tingents from the colonies. Parsimonious spirits were from the outset disposed to argue that the colonies ought to pay full expenses, just as India had to pay the full cost, of the Indian troops who went on an Imperial mission to Suakin some years ago. Happily this huckstering policy has been discredited. The Adstralian, New Zealand and Canadian forces will be paid by the Imperial Government, and all wants providel for, from the moment they set foot in Capetown. It must be remembered that the (rain is nearly all on our side. Obviously it is most desirable that colonial troops ahould obtain experience of actual warfare. A month on the veld in face of the enemy will be worth a year's peace discipline. If England were in imminent peril the circumstances would be different. We should want all the money we could obtain, and it would be the manifest duty of the colonies to come to our aid without thought of, or desire for, repayment. We believe that the colonies would accept that view, and would put down their men and their money without a word. At present we are not in any peril, but England desires to know the exact military value of the men upon whose capacities the fate of the Empire may at some future day depend, and it is only just that Erie)" nd should pay the cost of the experi- ment. Mr. Chamberlain has seen this clearly enough, and has carried the rest of the Cabinet with him.</p>
        <p>An interesting volume, conveying many lessons, might be written upon movements that have sug- o'ested songs. It is a truism that any movement worth its salt—sometimes not worth it, as the case may be—is certain to be fixed upon by poets, rhymesters and nrisicians for vocal development. The songs of patriotism are known in all the lands of the world —not excepting the "ignorant " Boers. The drinking song touched its apotheosis in Germany, chif fly at Heidelberg. where young men congregate. The song of militarism and the song of peace are to be met, in varying qualities, in England, France, Germany and Italy ; Russia, not being given to unstinted emotionalism of that sort, has produced little that is notable. We have cricket songs and football songs, songs connected with every kind of movement—except, perhaps, a temperance song; that is to say, a good and well- known tune which can be taken up at will by almost any assemblage of Britons. How is it that temperance has not inspired the bard and the musician. The theme is good enough and serious enough. Some will say that it is not sufficiently popular among the right class of people; but that would he a grave slur upon our civilisation, and the explanation must come from elsewhere.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians are be- ginning to realise what an expensive undertaking they put their hands to when they decided to build a new infirmary. The cost at the very lowest, estimate will be £34,000. This,we venture to say the majority of ratepayers will think is a trifle ex- travagant. It is much more than a Union like Warrington can well afford to spend on a building provided expressly for the accommodation of the sick poor. Those who have had an oppor- tunity of looking at the extensive block of build- ings which is now nearing completion must have come to the conclusion that the Guardians have not only been convinced of the truth that "the ' poor we have always with us" but that we may expect a very large addition in the future. If such a large hospital is really required for the sick poor in the Warrington Union then we may safely look forward to an increase in the poor rate unless ' there is a substantial addition to the rateable ' value of the Union. The cost of maintenance alone—independent of the interest and redemption of loans—must be a very serious item. At least a dozen trained nurses will be required as well as an addition to the present staff of officers in connection with the Work- house. The Guardians would have shown better discretion and acted more in the interests of the ratepayers if they had built a smaller hos- pital at the outset and made arrangements for adding wings to it when the necessities of the Union demanded them; or as an alternative scheme, purchased the Warrington Fever Hospital.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.—THE FIRST SUBSCRIP TION CONCERT IN THE PARR HALL, PALMYRA. SQUARE, Os WEDNESDAY, NOVEMBER 8, 1899. UOUNOD'S "FAUST." ARTISTES : .NIARGuEnrrE.—... MISS LILIAN COOMBER. FAUST NR. REGINALD BROPHY. :MEPHISTOPHELES MR. A. S. WINCK WORTH. &amp;EBEL MARTHA. MIS 3 JESSIE KING. VALENTINE WAGN.ER SAIR.5AIR. HARGREAVES HUDSON. CHORUS &amp; ORCHESTRA OF OVER 203 PERFORMERS. CONDUCTOR: NH. FRANK 11. CROSSLEY. Hrh. Organist,: MR. J. B. CALD WELL. Leader of orchestra' MR 12. H. PEARSON. PRICES OF ADMISSION : Reserved Dress Seats, 6s ; Ra- se rved Centre Gallery and Second Seats, Body of Hall 3s; Reserved Side Gallery, 2s ; Unreserved Back Gallery, Is 6d ; Unreserved Body of Hall or Pro,Tenade, Is. Tioni4 OF bUR,CRIPTION FOR OERIEs OF THREE CONCERTS: —Reserved Dress Seats Body of Hall, 153 ; Reserved Centre Gallery and Second Reserved Seats, Bo ,y of HAI!, 7s LU; Side Galleries, reserved, ss. These amounts will entitle any Subscriber to One Ticket for each Concert. Doors open 7-15 p.m. Concert 7-45 p.a. Carriages 10-23 p.m. Ticket-Holders admitted by early door at 7 p.m. THE PLAN OF RESERVED SEATS will be open at Mr. R. H. PEARSON'S, Bold-street, to Subscribers, on Saturday next, OCTOBER 28th and to the general publics on and after the following Wednesday. NOVE'ABER let. A Special Train for Latchford, Tbelwall and Lymm, will leave Arpley Station after the Concert. W. B. ATKIN, Hon. Sec, L. W. REYNOLDS, Hon. Treas</p>
        <p>THE RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL OF RUN- CORN.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM LORDSHIP WATBRWORK.S. The Council invite Tenders for the construction of Water- works for the Lordship of Frodsharn. The wor .5 will c im- prise the Building o' a 13.1 ck Engine House, I;ned Br ek umn Crossiey's 4 HP. " Otto" Gas Engine. 16 inch stroke. 8 double ac,,ion Rana pump 8 inch str .ke, Air Vcssel Valves, Pipes and Connections, 6 inch and 3 inch cast iron Mains witn the necessary Valves, Hydrants, etc. Covered Brick Reservoir of 50.00 Gallons capacity. Th,s "tans and skieclfication may b inspected at my office. 71. tligh-street, Enncorn. on and after MO VDAY, NOVIM- BICE lei', 1899, and conies of the -3pecification Schedule and Form of Tender may 13- obtained on deposit of One Guinea which will be retur ed on receipt of a bona-flde Tender. Sealed Qenders endorsed—Tender for " Froth iarn Lordship Water Works " to be delivered at my office not later than the 17th day of NOVE B &amp;R. 1899. The Council do not biad themselves to accept the lowest or Itny tender. By Order, GEORGE F. ASHTON, Clerk to the said Council. 71, High-street, Runcorn. 18th October, 189 g.</p>
      </div>
