      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WIDNES MAN IN TROUBLE</head>
        <p>A WIDNES MAN IN TROUBLE</p>
        <p>At the N irt hwich Police Court, on Saturday morning before Messrs. J. Weston (in the chair) and i. Bratt, an old man, named Thomas Hunt, a native of Widnes, was summoned for beiog drunk in WP.ton-street on the previous eveninrz.—;).C. Ellison proved the case, saying that the defend int was with a young man who knocked him down two or threri times.—Fined 53. " HARR AND HOUNDS" AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn [Janus held an ordinary run from the headquarters last Saturday, when 13 members turn-d out. Die hare., NI, sr s E Faulkner and S Clarke, were despatcned at 3.45, the pack following about 15 minute. liter. Trail was laid over about eight mites of good coun'ry (including several good jumps over hedges and water) in th-. direction of Sutton, and frclan there to Hal- ton, and tnen home again. All the pack kept in clo., order until the final burst, when Ray drew to the front and won a good race from J Goodall, with Hopkins, a good third. Next Saturday, for the team race— Captain v. Sub.captain—,l medal will be given for the first man home by W Thomas (captain), a mile break being formed at Halton Brook. A good race ii anticipated. MILTON P.S.A. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly in.,eAng was held on Sunday after- noon, in Milton Church, when the President occupie I the chair, and spoke of the difficulties they had to con- tend with in their religious work in Widnes. He said the Sunday labour and the cliff irent phases of religious c:ecds all tended against suczessful organisation and work. Mr. W. R. Thomas sang with good effect the " Dawn of Redemption," and" I love to tell the story." The speaki-ir, Mr. Barrow, gave a very practical and helpful address on " Henceforth I call thee not servants, but friends," and said that it WAS their duty to he friends of Jcsus Christ, for Christ needed them, in order that they might help for ward the great purposes of God. Tne address was well deliverei, and listened to with rapt attention, and at the close received the hearty applause it deserved. ST. EDWARD'S CATROLIC MISSION, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The patronal f,stival in connection with this church was held on Sunday, when there were god congrea- dons. The" Mes.f.e S)lennelle" was impressively ren- dered by the choir under the able conductorship of Mr. Dockery. The pulpit was occupied by a Passioniit Father, Rev. Burtrard, who gave two excellent dis- courses. In the morning he took for his subj ct the sacrifice of Christ for naa.n's salvation,and enlarged upon r,he efficacy of the sacrifice of the mass. At evensong the rev, gentleman dwelt upon the devotion to the Blessed Virgin, seen especially in the first ages of the church, and paid to her by the apostles and even Christ himself. Ele then proceeded to show her virtues and her title to their respect, especi illy appealing to the child- ren of Mary to be faithful in their duties. Before the sermon Mr. Dockery sang a solo in a very effective manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF MR. E. NEWTONT.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF MR. E. NEWTONT.</p>
        <p>Last Monday night, news that Mr. E. Newton had died from a sudden seizure of apoplexy was received with considerable consterha-don by his many frieiids. It appears that Mr. Newton, who was chief clerk and parcels agent at the Runcorn Railway Station, went home after attending his usual duties and was bidding " good ni;,ht " to his housekeeper when he was se zed with the fit. Dr. Delany and Dr. McLennan were at once summoned, but despite all their efforts Mr. Newton did not reeover. In his capacity at the Railway Station Mr. Newton made very many friends, and was respe,:- ted by all who were brought in contact with him. He was also a inembsr of the Ellesmere Lodge of Fr,e- masons, and had worked up to the positien of " S.W." baying held all the intermediate offices. Indeed so highly respected was he am )ngst his brother masons that flex' July would, doubtlese; have seen him installed as Worshipful Master. It is but a short time since the grave closed over the mortal remains of Mrs. Newton, and much sympathy is felt for tie bereaved sons in their double sorrow. MARINERS' MISSION CHURCH HARVEST HOME.</p>
        <p>This f, stival was observed on Sunday, services being held at 3 and 630 p.m. In the afternoon the pu'pit was occupii,cl by tee Rev. C. R. Killick, M.A. (vicar), wh ) preached an appropriate sermon to a larg, congrokati In the evening Mr. John Miller, evangelist, of Widnes, was the preacher. Tao building was crowded to over- flowing, many persons being unable to gain admission. Mr. Miller'gliVo a practical discourse, which was listened to with marked atter:Hon. The building, as in previoas years, was most effectively decorated, many willing hands having been engaged upon the work, and from alt quarters there was geaeral admiration of the good nets displayed. On Monday evening Mr. Joseph Stokes, of Rocksavage, preached a helpful sermon to a large con- gregation. The choir, under the direction of Mr. Price, gave a good account of themselves in the musical portion of the services, and the singing of a number of the most popular harvest hymns contributed in no small digree to the success of the proceedings. The offertories in aid of the mission, were liberal. LOCAL CASES AT THE QUARTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Knutsford Quarter Sessions in the second court before Mr. ates, and other justices, Enoch Rawlinson, for having stolen two pairs of boots the property of Anthony Bradley, at Roncorn, was sentanced ti niter udander months with hard labour.—Samuel Lewis (43), described as an engine driver (who was committed bv the Runcorn Magistrates some time ago), pleaded guilty to stealing a silver watch and chain, a pair of trou.er ..a vest, and a pair of boots, belonging to Louis Muy:sen, at Ashren-on-Mersey ; and also to obtaining by false pre- tences from Maggie Russell at the same town food and lodgings.—Mr. Trevor Lloyd; who prosecuted, said the prisoner bad represented himself as a working-man, and hiving obtained food and lodgings, decamped ciich articles which he found in the hous.s where his rkickery had been successful. He was arrested in Liverpool,when the articles of clothing and the watch and chain were found upon him. He was nothing less than a travelling thief, and in support of that assertion, Mr. Lloyd pro- duced a long statement of his offences.—The Chairman said that having regard to the prisoner's previous con- duct, and to the fact that he had during only 29 tears been convinted 14 times, it was impossible to deal leniently with hira. The sentence of the Court would consequently be six months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CANON WODD ON WAR IN THE TRANSVAAL.</head>
        <p>CANON WODD ON WAR IN THE TRANSVAAL.</p>
        <p>In the course of his sermon on Sunday evening, at the Church. Canon Wood observed that the Slrip'ural accounts of war showed that it was a scourge from GA, for the punishment of evil-doing, as was instanced, among other cases, by the destruction of Jerusalem. Adverting to the present struggle, the preacher said it was far from him to attempt to apportion the blame; only God, who knew the hearts of men, could do that. This nsuch they could do, however, and that was to un- derstand that the underlying power which bred war was man's selfishness, greediness, pride.jealousy, and hatrA. War was to be regarded as the symptom of a dise oe, which was these evil passions acctumulating and spreading, till they broke in what wag not the cause, hut the symptixa of the disease. The greatness of a nation was not its wealth.but the self-denial of the people.their Power of controlling themselves, and if England forgot that her greatness must come to an end. The present dispute was one of those things which came to touch our pride. and to make us remember that after an. wherein E.igland's greatuess lay was that she believed in God. He earnestly asked them to pray that God w )uld overrule it f,r good, and that He who maketh war to cease in all the world might bring go ed out of this evil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>st;WIftftti=NOMISEMB</head>
        <p>st;WIftftti=NOMISEMB</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRI DAY, OCTOBER 20, 1899.</p>
        <p>Tillillrffi MIMI GAS METES'S A/ WIDNES.</p>
        <p>John Hopkins-, for thefts trom two CrpAration gas meters at With.es was on Thursday s--ntencel to 14 days' imprisonm.n.t in each case, The Pohce Superin- tendent reported p,eyi.us c3nviction3 agAin3t the lad, and stated he had been sent to a reforina ory, on one occasion, until the age of 15.—The by sid he was w 17 years, old and offered no re.3.sln for his thefts The Saperintendent asked foe a csiumi'.mlnt in resp.cr ti an unpaid fine of 203. standing against Hopking, an,l the magistrata finally decided to send him to jail for 6 weeks with hard laboor.</p>
        <p>A DESRRTER FOR THE "FRONT."</p>
        <p>A young man named Arthur Williams, went up to l Conable"Wilson at about a quarter to eight on fil,nday night and said he :vas a desertJr frocn the First S)utti Lancashire Regiment, r•tationed at Preston. Whuld the Constable take him in P—Wilson replied that he would, and did so, with the remit that the following morning ht, was remanded, to await identlfication and a miltiry eic irt. —The young fellow said he deserted some time ago but was now desirous of gotn; " to the front." An- other soldier, John Bushell, presQnted him4elf before Me.srs. F. Wiswall and Henry Reynolds at the Police Court on Wednesday morningoaying he was an absentee from the same regiment as WitliaFns. Hew is remanded and eventually handed over to the military authorities</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>tHE RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Owing to the Knur,iford Q _tarter Set slot:is being held on Monday lasr,no summones were made returnable for th 9 Runcorn Petty Sessions of that date.—At the Police Court, however, there was one case. Jilin Warder, a youth, residing with his mother at the Roe and Crown, wt charged before Messrs. A. R. Norman and F. Wis- wall with being drunk and disorderly in ,T3hz--street, Runeorn, on the 14,:h inst.—P.S Gill proved the case, And said the defendant was very drunk and creating a great disturbance which was especially annoying to a dancing class held at the Brunner Gui d Hall.—Defenl- snt was fined is 61 and 83 6.1 costs.—The money was paid. STREET SINGERS BEFORE THE BENCH.</p>
        <p>Ellen Smith and her husband, visitA Runcorn, on l'uLs.lay, with the evident intention of giving the Run- corn public the benefit of their extensive repetoire of "songs." Somehow or other the Runcorn public did not sufficiently appreciate the "vocalists," and the female went soliciting alms from several shop-people. Now in Runcorn there is a lynx eyed constable named Gregory who saw this latter proceeding and subsequently took Mrs. Smith " under-the-clock " where she was formally introduced to Messrs. Wiswall and Reynelds.—These latter gentlemen pointed out the enormity of the off .nce and dismissed Mrs. Smith with an injunction to leave the town at once.—Tho husband, who was in court, then got up, and impressed upon the Bench that he was a cripple through paralysis, and destitute. He craved permission to finish his singing so that he might induce s.,mn music lover (?) to part with sufficient cash to see him over the Bridge.—The Bench said they had nothing to do w tO his singinz, but he must nit b'g. RUNCORN LICENSED VICTUALLERS' ASS ,CIATION.</p>
        <p>Tnis association hold its annual meting at the Railway Hotel, Runcorn, last Friday. Mr. A. Hough pre ided over a good attendance of members.—Mr. E. Grundy (the secretary) presented the annual report, which showed the association was in a very thurisbing condition.—The financial statement presented by Mr. rhornas C. Price was also considered satisfactory.— G ineral regret was expressed that the whole of the licensed victuallers in the town did not take or:vantage of the ben,fits secured by the membership of the associa- tion.—Mr. A. Hough was re-elected prisilident, and the following were elected officers for the ensuing twelve inonths :—Mr. T. Jones, vice-president ; Mr. Thomas C. Price, treasurer ; Mr. H. Grundy- secretary ; Atissrs. T. Andertsn, M. Conway, T. Wnite, R. Jones, W. John- son, and J. T. Winfield, committee. A RUNCORN Ma.' IN TROUBLE AT NORTHWICEI.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs J. Weston (in the chair) and H. Bratt, at the Northwich rolice Court an Stturday morning„ a man warned David Ashiey, 'it Runcorn, was charged with stealing 61. fr am the person of Thomas Hunt, on the previous day.—P.C. Ellison stated that he was on duty in Witton-streat on the previous night when he saw the prisoner and Huat under the iuflu-nce of drink Ha put Hunt into Mr. Babbington's yard whilst he went o summon assistance. He had go: about 20 yards up the street when he heard the old mm shouting for help Re turned bek, and upon throwing his light on the men he sew Hunt lying on the ground, and Ashley was kneeling on his shoulders and rifling his pockets. When witness put his light on the latter he dropped the six- pence.—Prisonet said that loo,h he and Hunt workei and lived together at Lostock. He wal endeavouring to get his companion home when ha fell OR him. He ,mohatically (sealed that he had his hand in Hunt's rocket.—The Chairman said they believA the police- man's evidence, tit sceing that the prisoner was on friendly terms vr:th Hunc they would give him the benefit of the ckubt. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a meeting of the above Order was held in the Greenway•road School. Mr. Thomas Cooks occupied the chair. There was a large attendance of memb•re. An interesting feature of the meeting was that the officers and members of the Weston divi3ien paid a fraternal visit. A very cordial welcome was extended to them by the Chairman (Mr. Thomas Cooke) who invited them to take for the evening the various offices, which they accepted, Mr. iJames Dunbabin taking charge of the meeting. Mr. Cooke referred to the good feeling which had always existed between the two divisions, and he hoped that it would always con- tinue, and he cherished the hope that they would often have an interchange of visits. Mr. D. Bisbrown remineed the meeting that their object was total ab4inence from all intoxicating liquors, and urged them to push forward their propaganda. There was then an interchange of views by the members and a most interest.ne and profitable meeting was spent' It was said that the Runcorn Division, including the cadets, numbered 419. At the close of the meeting Mr. Thomas Heesom moved a vote of thanks to the Weston friends for their visit, which was carried unanimously. The following officers and members took part in the meeting, Messrs James Johnson, Thomas Clarke, Samuel Tomlinson, John Brown, Samuel Dodd, James Moores, W Kirkham, Thomas J Holt, William Hazlehurst, and Thomas White. The meeting then closed. SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST A RUNCORN SCHOOL TEACHER On Saturday, at the Runcern Police Court, before Messrs. F. J. Norman and Walter Timmins, Charles Gornall, assistant master, at the Teinity School, Run- corn, was charged with havikg, on the 6th of October, at Runcorn, obtained by felse pretences, two pairs of boots, the property of Mr. John Tomlin, shoemaker, Re- gent-street.—Evidence way given to the effect that prisener, on the day in qiieetion, gave a note to a boy named Jackson, near to the Mattenra Hall, and told him to take it to" Mr. Canning's shop." Jackson was one of his ewn seholara at the Trinity Schools. He did so, and in answer to the note, he received a parcel which he gave to prisoner.—The letter was as follows:—"Stan- ley Villas, Greenway-road,--Dear Mr. Canning, will you kindly send me a pair of patent leather, and a pair of light-coloured tan boots, say five or small six to chereie from. I will return one back im- mediately together with money,if you will out it inside. f you have no tan send two pair of box calf. My last pair of tan from you have warn exceedingly well and I am therouehly satisfied with them. My messenger will return immediately I have chosen my pair. Hoping you are A.I. In haste, yours sine rely Percy Linsworth." —Mr Percival Unsworth, of Staeley Villas, on oath said he did not write the letter (preduced) and moreover his name was "Percival" and not "Percy." —Mr. J. Tomlin carrying on business under the name of "Canning" Proved supplying the boots. and Jackson proved hand- ing them to the prieoner.—ln answer to the charge Gornall pleaded not guilty, elected el be summarily dealt with, and also gave evidence. He swore that his action was not premeditated. He was appearing in character at a concert and wanted the boots, and com- mitted the offence on the spur of the mnmenr. He was very sorry.—Prisener's father appeared in court and made an appeal for leniency to the magistrates.—The Bench, in view of nothing being known against the prisoner before, bound hiei over in £lO, and his father in £s,to D'itlle up for judgment when c tiled upon,withia the next six months. ALLEGAVONS AGAINST A WIDNES MOTHER.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Police Court, on Fridar. before Alderman W.W. Gossage, Margaret Salters, 19, Midwood-street, was charged on a warrant at the instance of the N for cruelly neglecting and illtreatiog her enildren, John Richard, nine years, Edward, six yea's, and Norals Ann. four years, on the 6:h October and different other dates.—Me. Ku iwles appeared on behalf of the society.—Prisemer pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Knowles said that he would only lak far a remand until Thursday. He would call the husband, who would state that his wife durin,, the last feet years had turned out a veritable drunkard. She spent all the money she could lay her hands on, and would often go on drinking but of t ,vo or three days' dur ition. No food was brought into the house by the woman, and when the husband brought home food she would throw it on to the fire before giving it to the children. She was a perfect terror. On the nncasion of the assault the mother had scratched the poor lad's face, who, on Monday, was in a pitiable plight. She also struck him on the head with an iron stand, and when the father came home at night he hund the lad's head matted with blood. This he thought sufficient to warrant a remand —Thomas John Salters said he was a chemical labourer. During the last four years his wife had simply been a slave to drink. He knew for a fact that she was very severe with the children and not only used her hards, but had res irted to anything she could lay her hands on. He had also paid during the last month £2 in fines for her drunkenness —Prisoner was remanded until Thursday.—On Thursday. before Alderman Sadler and other magistrates at the Widnes Police Court, Margaret Salters, was brought un on remand.—Thomas John Salters, husband of the defendant testified to his wife being utterly abandoned to dr:nk and she was not fit to look after his children.—Evidenee was also given by Inspector Hackett,ef the N S.P.C.O ,Censeable Wresfeld and the boy John Richard —Dr. Hutchieison described the boys injuries. When examined by him there were three wounds on the head, and the la is face was very much scratched as was also his n,ck. There were other bruises on the boy's arms. He should think that one rf the wounds was caused by the kettle-stand (produced) such an missile was caused by the kettle-stang produced sentenced her to 3 inloths without hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RATS FOR CLIFTON TOWNi•RIP</head>
        <p>RATS FOR CLIFTON TOWNi•RIP</p>
        <p>A rate for tile townstaip ot Clifton of I+. 2d. in the £ to realise £365 174. 8 was made before Messrs. F. Wis- wall and H. Rynod on Virt dnesday last.</p>
        <p>A JUVENILE THIEP BIRCHED.</p>
        <p>Richard Malone, aged 12 ear:, was broeght up at the Runcorn Occasional Court on Wednesday charged with the larceny of 2s. the property of Annie Hayes, on the 1.7-,h of Ootober--Ti.e maeis,.raree present were Me-era. F. Wis wall, and Henry Revnelds.—Annie Hayes a single woalan, said she resided with her parents at 67, Spring Gardens, off Peuningt in-row, Canal-street, Runcorn, a., )ut 2 o'clock on Tuesday afternoon she put 2s. in an o d glove, under the bed in her house for safety. She -went out, and on coining back at quarter to five sent her sister upstairs to get the money,but it was not there. Ble suspected the prisoner of the theft and went in search of him. Subsequently she found him in a c:ip shop h the " Wint " near to the Tneatre. Prisoner's mother was with her at the time. Be was with an) her boy to whom he had given 51 of tie- money stolen, and h had a shilling himself.—ln answer to the Superiaten- dent of Police witness said she su pected the by be.nause he had been into the house twjci before and taken money out of her dress pocket upstairs.—Walter Hayei, aged 12 years, residing at Granville-street, proved the pesseseion of the money by d,fendant.--Constable G. Carrington said he chargel the boy with the theft to which he replied : "It la all right,l to Ift it and bought cigare-es, cakes and chips with it and gave Walter Hayes 51." Witness added Ihai7. first of all the prisoner aceu-ed Hayes of the thett but from fog iriea institutel he found that Hayes was nowh 're near the place at the time.--The boys' mother elected to have him summarily dealt with.—He pleaded guilty, saying he went up, taire and foaled the money under the bed, on ceming down be cleared off across Moore's btickfield.—Tne Bench sentenced prisouer to receive 12 strokes from the birch from Constatle Wilson. THE GRAMAPHONE AT THE SEAMEN'S MISSION:</p>
        <p>The fues,ay orient cuneen at the Mersey Mi,sion to Seamen was presided over by Mr. W. Byfleld, who also got up the whole programme,ani proved highly success- ful. Tie following progromrne Wi9 given :—Pianoforte solo, Miss Garvie ; song, " Tit for Tat," Miss Coward ; c iolin solo (selected). Miss E Jlnes ; humorous sang (selected), Mr Naild ; gramophone songs (the latest) an speeches, Mr Percnard ; recitation, " Ned the Elgine Driver," Miss Turner ; song (seleced), Miss May Bill; quartette (musical instruments) " Old Folks at Home," Mr A and the Misses Joues ; reading, "A new rec for soup for a sick noon," Mr Byfield ; humorous song (selected), Mr ; duet (selected), Misses Hill ; gramaphone selections, Mr Perchard; duet, Miss Coward and Mr Temple, and " I can't change it" by A Sailor (encored). fhe Gramaphone was very highly appre- ciated by all present, and in addition to the programme already given Mr. J. Rutter was prevailed upon to con tribute a song or two. The ••fficieat accompanist was Miss Gorvie, Daring the interval refreshments were provided and liberally partaken of. A most enjoyable entertainment was closed with a hearty vote of thanks to the Cha,r,nan, and aristes proposed by Mr. W. Shaw and seconded by Mr. J ,hespe,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCOR;i4Uin4,</head>
        <p>RUNCOR;i4Uin4,</p>
        <p>[By A CORRHSPONDENT.] Halton water supply again gives general dissatis- faction, and a furtner step has been decided upon by the Halton Parochial Committee so as to ensure an independent analysis. They are rightly det;er- mined to get to know exactly hoc' bad the water is, and,witti a view to &amp;finitely settling that ques- tion; invite the Runcorn Urb ,n District Council to co-operate with them in securiJg a thoroughly in- dependent anal) sis. This the Runcorn Council should do.</p>
        <p>Once again, I raise my voice in appeal for the preservation of that little bit which is left of historic Runcorn—the Stocks. They have stood for years in the Market-square, but are now fast falling to rack and ruin, and will 830E1 be a mere recollec- tion unless attention is given to them. They have partaken in the vicissiti• de of the town, and left to themselves must naturally dacay ; yet, surely, they are worth the rdpairing and preserving.</p>
        <p>The ancient Court Leet at Halton met last Satur- day, apid its memberi spent quice a jovial after- noon, in feasting and ruminating upon the doings of their forefathers, who, when vested with full authority, could hang "one and all "for innumer- able petty crimes. How, also, that authority has dwindled.history clearly defines, and now the meet- ing of" Ye Ancient Court" is but an excuse bra rare good feas,-,. All I can say is that the feast is said to be equally as good as the excuse; and the good old liaitan freeman has an offspring whose feasting and ale-quaffing abilities do him large honour.</p>
        <p>On Friday night, the memblrs of the Runcorn Liberal Club, had laid before them details of the proposed alterations to the club premises,in Regent- street, and passeJ a resolution approving of the action takea by their committee. The details, are however, witheld from the public for a time, await- ing fuller development. But from a general point, of view, the proposed alterations will greatly en- hance the place both sacially and hygenically.</p>
        <p>A fortune awaits anyone whose inventive geniui can devise a scheme for the using up of the " wastes" from tan yards, chemical yards, and such like places. It appears that it is principally these kind of works that are acredited with the sin of contaminating the Manchester Ship Canal waters. The River's Pollution Act, has ferrited that much out, and now all the managers of such works are. more or less, face to face with the problem of how to treat their effluents. The necessity has therefore arisen for a more economic treatment of waves, and it is in the s)lution of this little prob- lem that the undiscovered Klondike lies. Unless some such solution is forthcoming, it may yet be possible to make a high road of the canal—it will be so solid.</p>
        <p>The amount of sewage and other contamination which gets into the canal must be enormous, and an exaci, statement of the case would be interesting to all who live within olfactory distance of its waters, inasmuch, as they would then know exactly what they bad to contend with. if, however, the smell is going to increase pro ratio with the amount of con- tamination—and probably it will do so—where is the whole thing to end. Runcorn should look to these things before the condition of the water gets as bad as it is at Latchford and uowards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MISS JUKES' PUPIL; At UREENWAYROSD SCHOOLROOM,</head>
        <p>MISS JUKES' PUPIL; At UREENWAY- ROSD SCHOOLROOM,</p>
        <p>A high-class entertainment was given at the Green- way-read Schoolroom, on Wednesday evening last, which immensely delighted a crowded audience. The artistes were the exceedingly clever musical pupils of llie Jukes, Brackley-street. Runcorn, and their perforate ices simply and collectively were of a most finished character. Punctually at eight o'clock, the chair was taken by Mrs. %V. B Kay, and she introduced Misses Jenicia and Juke 3as the executents of the pianoforte duet " Overture to Guy !Veneering" (Bishop). an item that was done justice to. Miss M. Kennedy wee next with Gordon Temple's "In mellow autueintide," and gave a fine rendition. Her sweetly flexible voice, however, was better heard in the song" By the Fouetain " which was her contribu- tion for the second part of the programme. The vocal waltzs " Playmates " and" Gipsies " were two tuneful contributions by the children. In the latter the Riney dance by Misses L Shaw, M Weston, J Lee and N Jones was chernvngly effective. Miss Mabel Digges showed herself to bs a thoroughly efficient manipulator of the mandoline in a " Selection of Irish Airs," by Birch. A bewitching little artiste was Miss M Weston, and as soloist in " I love nay baby," and in the recitation of " The Ler ters " her success was pronounced she being encored in each. Selections on tbe mandoline, piano, tamboriee, bow zyther, and triangle given by the whole strength of the company, to use a familiar phrase, showed the whole to advantage as a unique band. Miss L Shaw was perhaps the most accomplished of the juvenile soloists. She gave Buy a Broom," and " I shan't go to School," and met with universal approval, being recalled on both occasions. Botb items were given in co•turne, and concluded with neatly executed dances in one of which the skipping rope figured. The second part was opened by a capital execution of Auber's " Overture le Cheral de Bronze " by the Misses Riley and Jukes. The Misses Kennedy and Janison showed their ability with the bow zyther in a duet " Revevie " (Henry Tolhurst.) This item was not, however, heard to advantage, owing possibly to the deadness of the table upon which the instrument lay. The trio "Three little Quakaresses" Misses Shaw, Jones and Weston, was a quaint and pleasing it m, which was vociferously recalled. The juveniles taking part in the vocal waltzes were : Girls, W Armstrong, M Devon- pert, M Ellis, E Grice, G Homes, H Hunt, B Jones, N Jones, F Harrison. J Lea, D Gebsen, L Shaw,M We-ton, and M Jani in. Boys: T Veston, El Jones, W Shaw, P Peivoek, S Leach, E Weston, E Lawton. C Clough, and F Weidere. The several accompaniments were played by Misses Armstrong, Humes, Williams, Parr and Jukes, and Mrs. Kay. The costuin s of the juveniles were pretty and taste! il, and gave an appearance of pleasing richness and beauty to the eve that blended harmoniously in every detail.—At the conclusion of the programme, Mr. W. B. Kay pros posed, and the Rer. H. B. Firth seconded, a hearty vote of thanks to Miss Jukes for the entertainment which she had so generously provided. Mr. Dykes proposed and a similar vote was conveyed to Mrs. Kay for so ably presiding. The singing of " God save the Queen" I brought to a close what; proved a most enjoyable enter- ' taiument.</p>
        <p>na==zilmmtnll7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HALTON COURT LEET. Speech by Mr. Robert Davies.</head>
        <p>HALTON COURT LEET. Speech by Mr. Robert Davies.</p>
        <p>This ancient and interesting Court met on Saturday afternoon, at the Balton CAstle Hotel, in answer to summons i.suod by the iii2h Sreward of the Court and Manor of EL-Won (Mr. Robert Davies, solicitor, Warring- ton) and thou4ii it is now prevented by legisliture fr im c irr:ing on the dui that once belonged to it, the cus- tom of formally opening the court and swearing in the jury is still adiferred to ; as is also the carrying out of no ri s-,lution passed at one of the earlier gatherings "that the members dine together "; and this social side of the court's business generally proves highly satis- factory. . _ _</p>
        <p>_ When the court was opened by proclamation of the High Bailiff, Mr. J eseph Walker, there were present : Mr Robert Dxvies (nigh steward of the court) and Mr KTkc ,nner (Warrington),the Rev G D Wray (chaplain), Mr Tnomas Wrieht (foreman of the jury),,dessrs Joseph M inning, Joseph Se kes, J Jirmee Speakman, James Lowe, Joseph Walker, Samuel Hairisoe, C Beugley, T Potter,John Newport and John Wright (members of the jury), etc. The High Steward (Mr. R. Davies) expressed how well pleased he was to meet the members of that ancient court once again, and referred to the fact that the court had met *lmes" without intermiesion for a period of something like 600 years. It would appear that the first record from that court had been forwarded to the record officer in 1347—that was, in the 21st year of the reign of Edward IV. Since that period the court had been called and sat on some convenient Saturday following the feast of St. Michael the Archangel, right up to the present gathering. Mr. Davies then desoribed the nature and doings of the ceurt, which was in a sense a court of morals as well as a court of justice. It ri goletel the quarrels of people ; decided who seauld or should nc,t keep cards and dice or partake in any kind of gaming whatever. Referring to their last meeting Mr. Davies said, as far as he could recollect, he toought one of the jury (Mr. Cooper) asked why the township of Moere was not sumna stied on the jury and he had promised he would look into the qles- tion. He hal done s ,ani foun i the reason to b Vest in 1628, the copyholders in Moore lnught the manorial rights from King Charles I. and became holders •,f their lands in other words freeholders There were always some freeholders in Moore as well as copyholders, and they, of c urse, were ex- cepted from the grant. Their names in 1628 were Porter, Brookes' Piekering, of Thelwall; Rutter, Dutton, of Mis End, and Widow Harper. The coprholders chose Picker- ing, Harper and Byron to be the purchasers from the Kieg on herialf of the eopyholdere. Runcorn else en- franchised itself about the same time. Thus in spite of the changes of centuries many of the old Cheshire names clung to the neighbourhood still, and if any of their possessors should go to Kionclote and return a millionaire the rolls of that court lest and the tornbetones, and registers of Dlresbury, Aston Cft tpel, Gr-at Budwortb, and other places within Ike Honour and Fee of Halton, would enable him to have his pedigree prepared, going centuries beck, and it weuld be much more accurate awl genuine than any p‘digree for which the nouveaux riches paid their nuggets. Even within the last year er two he hari had applications—Due from a person in Ainerica— desirous of making out their connection with that neigh- bourhood. Concluding his remarks with some personal re ninescenses Mr. Davies said th it even cii-erf to So late as the period of his own birth there were some 143 offences for which an offender might receive capital punishment. Some of them were so trivial as the stealing of a hank of unbfrachei yarn freen the bleach- ing yards, or the theft of potatoes. Speaking of the rise of Widnesthe stated he remembered that locality when it was but a hamlet of some 25 houses. One of the ancient privileges that had to be accomplished on becom- ing a burgher of Widnes used to be the drinking of a yard of ale, a feat which was accomplished by means of a bamboo red of the requisite length. Another privi- i legs was that if in going along the road you saw three pigs sleeping on the roadside, you could waken the middle one and take your place between the other two for a nap. That was, however, a state of things now passed, and he attributed it to the pregress made by education. On resuming his seat Mr. Davies was warmly applauded. A sumptuous dinner prepared by Mr. Joseph Walker, the host, was then done full justice to. Mr. D ivies gave the toasts " The Queen, as Lady of the Manor, Duchess of Lancashire, and Baroness of Halton " ; " The Prince and Princess of Wales, and the rest of the Royal Family" ; "The Bishop of the Diocese, the Clergy, and Ministers of all denominations," and with the latter coupled the name of their chaplain (the Rev. G. D. Wray).—The toasts were all duly honoured.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. D. Wray, in responding, referred to the amouf.t of pleasure, being chaplain to ...uch an ancient and regpectable court gave him, and to the kindly rela- tions that existed betwesn himself and the ministers of all 6thelr denominations in Halton.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Wrieht (foreman) gave "Tie health of the High Steward," and said it had been his pleasure to attend that court for eight and twenty years He was perhaps the oldest member present, and as such he felt he was expressing the opinion of all when he signified the great pleasure they all felt at st eing Mr. D „vies once again,and they all hoped that Mr. Davies would be sparel to meet them for some time to clme. (Applause). He was sure her Majesty could not get a better representa- tive than their respected High Seward. (Hear, hear.) He coupled with the toast the name of Mr. Kirkconncl. rho toast was drunk with musical honours,the company singing " For be's a j,llv gaol fellow." Mr. Davies, and Mr. Kirkconnel bo'h responded, and expressed the pleasure of continuing the court, and of meeting them again next year. The following items were contributed and much ap. predated during the afternoon :—Violin solo, " II Tra- vatore,' Miss Watson ; song, " Tne old horse," Mr. Watson ; ep.ng, " My deaf uncle," Mr. Wright. On the propulition of Mr. John Wright (Daresbury), seconded by Mr. Joseph Walker (Halewood) a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the host and hostess Mr. and Mrs. ‘Valker for the txellent manner in which they had prepared and served up the dinner to the satisfac- tion of all concerned. Mr. Walker suitably responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN Plii TO SHAME.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN Plii TO SHAME.</p>
        <p>(BY THE SCORCHER.)</p>
        <p>• This week the " Linnets" song has changed to one in a minor key, the cause being the reverse experienced at Stockport. Anyone prowling round Runcorn looking for " footballers, might spend considerable time before he found one, and might even be induced to give up the search, under the impression that they had gone off to the Transvaal —" to be shot." Anyhow, if they have not gone there; that is where they have been universally consigned. From realing other nports about the ' match at Stockport one would be led to believe that the home team played a game of very high standard, and any team meeting Stockport for at least a week or two was warned to look out for a " trouncing." Let me, however, disabuse people's minsls of that idea. It is quite erroneous, Stock- port did not play any better than usual. Indeed, they did not play even an average game of football, I and the whole spectacle made up a very inferior I and disappointing display. Yet, how comes it that Stockport beat Runcorn ? " All ! there's the rub,' and the relating of " how" can only come from certain of the Runcorn players themselves. But, unfortunately they are too modest to tell it. In my opinion Hull Kings- ton Rovers were the principle factors in the defeat of Runcorn. That game was played on the previous Wednesday, and was most successfully got over. Toe "aftereffects," and the "after after effects" were, however, somehow or other overlooked, and what happened between the conclusion of the match at Hull, and the commencement of the one at Stock- port, materially contributed to the defeat of Ran- corn. It is to those circumstances alone that Stock- port have to attribute their victory—they C1111.7,ht the radtails unprepared. that's all—and by no means are they entitled to the reputation some would give them.—What price Oldham for instance ?—Anyone with half an eye could see that Runcorn were worth two Stockperte, as footballers, but all being more or less" unfit' they were unable to stand the rough handling they got from the Stockport men. But even then, after stockport had done their best —or rather worst—to wipe out the "linnets," what was left of them were playing a far better game than Stockport ever made any pretentions to. Tnese excu -es, however, avail nothing, and not withstanding all, the Runcorn team are theroughly ashamed of being caught so unprepared. They knew what they had to face at Stockport, and yet they had the audacity to face them with an ex- hausted team, and for their impudence got beaten 8 points to nil. If anyone wants to know what it is to be in a " cyclone," he has simply got to walk up to a Runcorn footballer,look him in the face and say" Stockport" ! The rest will follow, and he will soon know all about cyclones. What will happen when the Leigh team comes to Runcorn, next Saturday, is somewhat uncertain— just sufficiently to to add a" bit of spice "to the game. Last year's record shows a home and home victory, the scores being : Runcorn, 3-0; Leigh, 0-4 , added to which Leieli beat Runcorn in the cup-tie by a drop goal. What few players I have been able to see, say they are quite " fit " and are going to play to win ; but, still, if they don't people must remember Leigh is still Leigh.</p>
        <p>To MOTHER9.—Mrs Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty Tears by millions of mothers for their 2hildren while teething, with perfect euceess. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes " as bright as a button."ol all Chemists Is lid per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>iHOAIOCEA FOR THE BATTLE-FIELD.</head>
