      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>INF A NTR Y</head>
        <p>INF A NTR Y</p>
        <p>NEED HOMOCEA FOR ANOINTING THE FEET ON THE MARCH, AND FOR SORES CAUSED BY</p>
        <p>CHAFING ACCOUTREMENTS.</p>
        <p>CAVALRY NEED HOMOCEA FOR TROUBLES CAUSED BY ROUGH RIDING, DAMP SADDLES, ETC., AND</p>
        <p>ESPECIALLY FOR PILES.</p>
        <p>SEE THAT NO FRIEND</p>
        <p>GOES TO THE FRONT WITHOUT FIOMOCEA. WHAT ELSE CAN THEY RELY ON TO TOUCH THE SPOT "AND SOOTHE THE ACHING PART " ?</p>
        <p>HAL fuN.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCIL MEETING was held at the school room, on Tuesday night, there being present :—Me gra. Joseph M...ni,ing (coairman), Robett Hughes, Joseph A.stbury, J Ames Looker, Samuel Barrow, Walter Moores, aud A. Woodier (clerk).—Accounts amounting to £4 Os 6.1 were passed tor payment.—Mr. Moores reported that the ce:netery was in a most Fatisfactory condition, Lemg at the present time quite tidy.—On the motion of Mr. Hughes it was teed d to write asking Mr. Ashley whether ha would repair the broken down pillar by a certa:n date, and if he c, u:d not do so, that the Clerk look out for someone else to do the work.—Mr. John PtrAval, f Halton (whose little child met situ an acci- dent which resulted in a broken leg, the other day), wrote pointing out the dangerous state of a gate on land ne sr io the footpath through Halton Fields, and asked whether anything ciuld be done to remely the state of affairs.—Mr. Bonen Hughes statel that the attention of the Vicar had been called to the gate, and stops were already being taken to have the matter righted.—M,. Barrow thought whilst they were considering the "dan- gerous situations" the; should call attention to the fence at the brook.—Mr. Hughes intimated that he be- lieved it was the intetion ot the County Council to fid up and level the whole piece in time.—This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>'rHE PAROCHIAL COSMITTEE.—This committee met at the National &amp;Noel, Haltoa, last Tuesday evening,thero being present :—Messrs Thomas Wright (chairman), Joseph Meaning, J.,eepti Astbury, James Looker, Samuel Barrow, W Moores, Roeert Hughes, G F Ashton (clerk), and James Farrington (inspector).—The Clerk intimated in the matter of the Halton Sewerage he had received a reply from the Rancern Urban District Council signify- ing that the matter would be laid before their surveyor and a draft scheme submitted. He had also seen Mr. Wild,ne, an,' he informed him that several schemes had been prepared but were awaiting the sanction of the Manclaeeter Ship Canal Company at their meeting, after which they would be lard b •fore the Runcorn Urban Ceuncil, and finally passed on ts the Parochial Corn- mittee.—The Chairman : That means we shall have to wait ?—Tne Clerk : We cannot do anything else yet.— Tne absence of the surveyor was accounted for by the I Clerk, and the reason assignee was considered ample.— A letter was read from Mr. W. H. Bradbury, Halton Brook explaining that when he erected the shed ad- joining his place he was not aware it was first of all necessary to have provided a plan and secured the sanction of the committee. Plans were enclosed and the letter also stated that no sewerage at all was con- templated.—The Chairman explained that it would now be advisable to ask Mr. Bradbury to enter into a bend, so that seould the Council at any time wish to have the sued removed he would do so. It would simply entail a stamp duty of about is. 6i. to which he felt sure he would not object..—Mr. Looker observed he had men- tioned the matter to Mr. Bradbury, and he was quite willing to do anything reasonable. He proposed that the necessary bond be entered into with Mr. Bradbury.— Mr. Barrow seconded,and the proposition was carried.— The Inspector submitted four certificates of Analysis of effluents taken for the purposes of the Rivers' Pollution , Act.—The Chairman referring to the Halton water supply eaid there wee not the slightest doubt that it was a very bad one, and suggested that another analysis be taken by a private analyst. It might be that Dr. Carter Bell had his hands so full of work that the analysis they I had was the work of one of his younger aesistants. He would rather they paid a much larger slim and have the water privately analysed by a good man.and if it provrd poor they could petition the Local Government Board for a better supply or appeal for the " Vyonwy "supply.— Mr. Barrow did not think that tne water they used was any worse than when they first got it. When the water was supplied by a private company, the reservoir was cleaned out only once or twice in 20 years. They knew for a fact that it had been cleaned about 5 years ago, but he could not see how that improved it. The water flowed in at the bottom and out at the bottom.—Mr. James Farrington said there was not a possible doubt about the analysis being worse.—Mr. Barrow was willina to grant that, but contended that possib:y a bad sample of stagnant water had hem taken.—Mr. Farrington : We let the water run off the pipes first.—Mr. Barrow: But del you give a portion of the water to the Runcorn Urban Council.—Mr, Ferrington : IC was not necessary 'to divide the sample. It was not taken under the Food and Drugs Acts.—lt was ultimately decided on the motion of Mr. Looker, seconded by Mr Manning, that a fresh sample of water be taken from the mains ; that the Runcurn Urban District Council be asked to send a representative to see the sample taken, and that the same be stitmitted to an efficient analyst—not the public analyst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ROYAL WELSH CHOIR CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL WELSH CHOIR CONCERT.</p>
        <p>A concert was given on Monday evening in the Parr Hall in aid of the fund of the local St. John Ambulance Brigade, at which there was a good attendance. The first visit to the town of the Royal Welsh Ladies' Choir had doubtless aroused many pleasurable anticipations which it is date at once to say were largely realised. The ensemble as well as the solo singing of this bevy of fair ladies has at all times the merit of being refreshingly vivacious and pleasing. The difficulty at Monday's concert was that it was found impossible to get through the surfeit of goo 1 things sat down in the evening's musical menu, and special exigencies forbid our noticing in detail even those numerous items which were actually performed. It will be gathered that the audience was often enthusiastic in its demonstrations of approval of many of the choral numbers and such, in fact, was the case throughout the evening with the result that the latter part of the programme had to be seriously curtailed. The best concerted pieces were undoubtedly the setting of the familiar "Ash Grove "and the Chicago test piece" Spanish Gipsy," in both of which a richness in tone volume and a delicacy of expression was shown whicn it was a delightful surprise to find possible in an assembly of voices which must necessartly vary so little in compass and timbre. The least satisfactory item to our mind was the part-song " Fair land we greet thee" the uncertain tonality of the vocal cadenza recurring in each verse being the fault rather than any shortcoming in vocalisati n. Tne singing of individual members of the choir was in several instances of a high order of merit as notably Mi.s Annie Bell's rendering of Cowen's charming little song " The Swallows " and the artistic vocalisation ot the bravura pas- sages of " A fors e lin " (Verdi) by isliss Jennie Ffoulkes. Mr. Emlyn Davies's baritone songs were marred by an ever-present tendency to exaggerated expression. "The Sailors Cleave" (Sullivan) especially suffered in this respect the sotto voce of the earlier part of this fine song being greatly overdone and coupled with too slow a tempo it is not to be wondered at that the song dragged noticably. Nevertheless in less exigent, circumstances as in the martial encore song " Who carries the gun ?" Mr. Emlyn Davies did himself and the music full justice and won enthusiastic plaudits. Miss Backsheen Wood is a lady vocalist who has already won for herself something of a reputation in Warrington to which her playing on Monday cannot but give an added lustre To suc- ' cassfully surmount the technical difficulties of the finale to the Mendelssohnian concerts the " Ber- ceuse " (Neruda) and the Brahni-Joachim" Tanze " predicates the possession of no mean abilities and that Miss Wood did brilliantly get through her task was amply testified by the unstinted applause showered upon her at the close of each piece. Con- strained to respond to a recall Miss Backsheen Wood played one of the Wieniawski Mazurkas with con- spicuous crispulos and verve. Mks Maria Williams in a pianoforte solo and in the accompaniments to the choruses showed unmistakable evidence of taste and skill as a pianist, and Mde. Clara Novello Davies the talented conductress of the choir wielded the baton during the choral pieces with the tact of a true musician, playing also with marked ability the accompaniments to the songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>HUMANE SLAUGHTER</head>
        <p>THE HUMANE SLAUGHTER</p>
        <p>OF ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>Successful Demonstration at Warrington. There have been many complaints in the past of the inhuman practises adopted in the slaughter of animals and the need has been felt for a safer, more expeditious, lese risky and more humane method of killing animals. A much-needed invention for producing instantaneous and painless death has now made its appearance and is keown as Greener's humane cattle killer. Mr. Toole is the first, we believe, to introduce it into Warrington,and on Tnurslay morning two members of the Health Com- mittee wi,h officials took the opportunity of visiting Mr. Toole's horse-slaughtering premises for the purpose of seeing the invention put into operation. Amongst those present were Councillor Smethuret,Councillor Rogers,Mr Nerman (chief inspector),Nir Powell (assistant inspector) Mr. Hugh 0. Ferguson (veterinary eurgeon),Mr. Payton, Mr e folle, Mr 'eocile jun. and severe! representatives of the press. Two horses which were unquestionably fit subjects for thelknacker's yard were provided for the occasion and they were placed in front of the small group of spectators. As the animals stood docile before their slaughterer, neconscious of their approaching doom,one could not taut reflect on the tragedy of their end. The slaughterer performed his task with commeneeble des- pateh, and not only were the animals killed instantane- ously but with the least possible danger to the slaughe terer. The instrument used is a noiseless explosive apparatee, consisting of a short rifled barrel, chambered to receive a small caeridge, with steel pointed bullet. It is terminated by a bell-shaped chamber, which serves to deaden the sound, to protect the operator from the flash of the explosion, and to direct the bullet through the brain into the spinal cord. This avoids the necessity of what is knewn in butcher's parlance as " pithing." The animal is killed instantly without noise or smoke. The slaughterer stands in front of the horse's head during the operation. He presses the iletrument upon the fore- head with the left hand, and with a small mallet in tha right hand he strikes a projecting pin. The animal immediatel falls at hie feet, to all appearances dead. Not only is the invention used for destroyirg horses, but it is in use at moat of the slaughter houses in the king- dom; Those who witeessed the operation on Thursday were convinced of the expeditiousness of the despatch of the animals, and Inspector Norman said it was far and away better than the polo axe, which has been the chief instrument used previously.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON SUNDAY tSCHOOL ASSOCIATION. The quarterly meeting of the above association was held at t. Paul's on Monday evening. The pro- ces,lings began at seven o'clock in the Infant's Scho4l, with tea, prepared by Mrs. Tarbuck, Miss Westwell, and a numerous band of helpers. This sociable and enjoyable meal gave an opportunity for the clergy and teachers of the various Sunday echools in the town to meet together, compare notes Sze. The committi e meeting followed, with the Rector of Warrington in the chair, when it WAS de- cided to hold the next quarterly meeting at Holy Trinity. At eight o'clock all adjourned to a special evensong at St. Paul's. The Rev. J El G Holling- worth sang the service, and the Vicar, the Rev. Ernest R Tarbuck, read the lessons. The Rector of Warrington, the Rev. Canon Willis, preachei on the privileges and duties of the Sunday school teacher, pointing out in a most sympathetic manner the preciousness of each child's soul, and the great re- ward that awaits those who train them for good. A very large number of local clergy and Sunday ' school workers attended the service, which was a most hearty one.</p>
        <p>FuOTBALL.</p>
      </div>
