      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>HIS POLICE COURT JCTBILEE. At the Warrington Police 0 urc on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, William Plinston, a broker living at 12, Ellen-street, was charged with being drunk in charge of a horse and cart 11 Bewsev-str2et on the 9:h October.—P.C. Clough sail that at 7-35 on the date in question ho was on duty in Bewiey-street when his attention was called by a gentleman to the defendant who Was drtl,k in charge of a horse and cart near Bewsey-street railway bridge. He assisted in taking the horse and cart to the stahle an! reported defendant. He was informed that defendant was upset on account of one of his horzes dying Rainhill on Saturday. His son had sold the animal and taken all the money, and that caused him to get drunk —Doft ndant said that was why he got drunk. He admitted drink had been his downfall.—The Chief Constable slid that Plinston made his 50:h appearnnce. That was his jubilee. (Laughter.) He had been fined in April for a similar offence.—Alder- man Francomb asked if defendant would discontinue his bad habits if they let him off.—The Chief Constable said he took absolutely no notice either of the penalties of the court or its clemency.—Mr. Bircha.ll thought the best place for him would be an Inebriatas' Home.—Defendant was fined lOi., the Chairman remarking that it might be defendant's jubilee, but it was a bad one in his opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>GOING TO DROWN HERSELF IN THE BREWERY-</head>
        <p>GOING TO DROWN HERSELF IN THE BREWERY-</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at the Warrington Polin Court Annie Jones, a stranger, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Wilderspool-road at 2 15 that morning.— P.C. Cooper (20) said that at the time in question in consequence of information he received from a cabman he proceeded near the "Saracen's Head," Wilderspool- road, and there found the prisoner. She had almost undressed herself some of her clo'hing being on the footpath. On being questioned about her conduct she told him that he had just come in time as she was going to drown herself. With great difficulty he got her in a cab and brought her to Bridge Foot. He walked her the remainder of the way to the Police Station. She told him that she came from S:. Helens and had had a lot of drink. She had been in goal and knew Dale-A., Liverpool, well. Gaol did not have any effect on her.— The Chief Constable add she was going to drown herself, so she had got near the brewery. It was more in her line than water. (Laughter.),—Prisoner was committed to prison for seven days. EGGINGTON AGAIN.-1 SUBJECT FOR AN INESRIATES' flour.</p>
        <p>At the VVarringfen Poll'7e Court en Tues lay, be! re Mr. Eirchall and Councillor Shaw Green, Sophia Egging. ton, Samuel-street, Sankey Bridges, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Liverpool-road at 10 o'clock the previous night.—P.C. Woods said that at the time nareed be w3e cilled toSaMU'l-qreet. On proceed- ing there he saw the prisoner. She ,xtig very drunk and was shouting and making use of had language. The neighbourhood was in an uproar. Ile was called to the woman three or four times a week, regu- larly.—The Chairman observed that it was time the Inebriates Asylums were ready.—The Chief Constable said there were a number of people ready for them. That was a typical case. The prisoner made her fifth appearance within eight months. She bad been before the Court altogether ten times. She bad bad a month on two occasions, but nothing seemed to do her the least bit of good. Im- mediately she came out of prison she commenced drinking again—Pri.oner's husband was called and stated that his wife spent all the m3ney he gave her in drink.—Mr. Birchall.—The best place we c3uld send her to would be an Inebriates' Asylum but there is 110 asylum to send her to—Witness, continuing, suggested that she should be sent to a home.—Mr. Taylor P.C.M. said there was a good home at Liverpool where she could go.—Councill;r Shaw Green said that as long as drink was there she would indulge in it. It was as sad a case as he ever knew —Prisoner's husband : She really cannot help it.—The Chairman : I am afraid she can't.—Prisoner (to her husband) : Allow me so much a week and I will leave you. —The Chairman : You would spend it in drink.—The Chief Constable : The man has tried every possible device.—Prisoner said she would go to the home for six monthe.—The Chief Constable : If she does not consent to go for twelve months I must press for the full penalty. —Prisoner eventually consented to go to the home for twelve menth3, and the case was adjourned sine die to see if she kept to her promise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NEGLECTFUL FATHER.</head>
        <p>A NEGLECTFUL FATHER.</p>
        <p>At the above court on Saturday morning, before Colonel Ridgway, William Taylor, a fitter, late of Stock- ton Heath, was charged on a warrant with neglecting to maintain his wife and four children whereby they became chargeatle to the common funds of the Dutton Work- house.—Mr. De Prcz, relieving officer, who prosecuted on behalf of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, said that the defendant left his wife and four children at Stockton Heath some time ago and went to Chippenham in Wilt- shire. He sent contricutions towards the maintenance of his wife and family up to Whitsuntide, but then ceased to send and his wife and family became charge- able to the common funds of the Dutton Union. It was stated that the expenses (railway fares, &amp;c.) amounted to £3l7s 24, and the defendant was discharge I on promising to pay 53 per week until that sum was paid off, and to maintain his wife and four children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court. FRlDAv.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Councillor Smethurst. and Mr. J. Hepherd. CRUELTY TO A WITE.—SEPARATION ORDER GRANTED.— Benjamin Aukland was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife, Ellen Aukland, 128, Wilderspool-road.—Defendant did not appear.—Complainant said that her husband con- tinually illused her, and she was afraid of living with him. On Wednesday week he got a pole and threatened to take her life, On Monday night she turned him out. She had never had a day's peace since she was married to him about twenty- nine years ago.—Complaivant's daughter said she had seen defendant assault her mother many a time, He was cruel to her in every way. On Wednesday weetc he came in the worse for drink and went upstairs. Her mother went to stop him from getting in bed and he threatened to strike her with a pole Witness heard the noise and ran upstairs and prevented him from hitting hr r. They turned him out on Monday night.—The Clerk handed to the magistrates a letter received from the defendant consenting to a separation order and offering to pay 12s. 6d. a week. —A separation order was granted, defendant to contribute 12s. 6d. towards the main- tenance of his wife and family and pay the Costs. CASE DISMISSED.—Kate Cartlege, 22. Turner-street, was summoned for assaulting Mary Ann Taylor, 1, Pinshop-yard, on the 4th inst —Complainant stated that on the date in question she saw the defendant who was" beastly drunk." calling her husband bad names. When she saw her (zom- plainant), she rushed at her and hit 117 twice.—Defendant denied having hit Taylor, and the magistrates dismissed the</p>
        <p>case. A CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR.—Rciwin Highley. 27. Gonl- den-street, applied for an order for the exemotion of his child from vaccination. He said that he conscientiously be- lieved that vaccination would be preju Heel to the child's health.—Mr. Hepherd : Were you in Warrington during the small-pox epidemic —Applicant : No sir. —Counci llor Smethurst You would have a different idea if you had been.—The application was granted. MoNDA.Y—Elato:.e the Mayor (ch airman), Aldermen Prancornb and Blackhuret, Councillors Smethurst, and Shaw Green, and Messrs. S. Wright, T. Birch all, and J W. Hayward. A WANDERING LUNA.Tlo.—Frederick William Bowden, a stranger, was charged with wanderine i a Kerfoot-street at midnight.—P C. Re Idington said that t.n minutes after ' midnight that morning he saw the defendant in Kerfoot-st , acting in a very strange manner. He questioned bine, anti he said he had come from Weaste, near Manchester. and was going to Altrinchern Defendant did not lune' where he was bailie said he knew that Weaste WAS at the ead of the street. —The Chief Constable said defendant had told him some stran7.e stories which showed that he was not right in his mind. He asked the B -nets to make an order for the defen- dant to be taken to the Workhouse to be examined.—This was agreed to. A 'Bus DRIVER ASSLULTED.—Joseph Thomas, an engine driver, residing at 267, Liverpool-roach WAS charged with being drunk and disord srly in Li•-rpool-roed at 10.30 on Saturday night and also with assa siting Daniel Roberts, a 'bus driver at the same time and otaoe.—P.o Woods said that at the time in question he was on duty in Liverpool- road, when he saw a 'bus come up an I all the people get off except the defendant. Daniel Roberts, the d iver of the 'bus, asked him to get off and the defead ant, who was drink. said he would knock him off and struck Roberts several times. Witness got him off the 'bus. ant with difficali y took him to the Police Station —Daniel Roberts gave evi- dence in support of this statement, and defeadant was fined 103 and costs. DRUNK IN CEIARGE.—Ernest Whittle, 'bus driver, of St. James-street, was charged with ti lying been drunk, whilst in charge of a 'bus and two horses on the Bth October in the Old London-road.—P.C. Gibbs stated that he saw the defend- ant in charge of a 'bus and two horses drive up to the Saracen's Head public house, in the Old Lon ton-road, at ten minutes past nine on the night of the Bth October. The checker, named Healey. who was on the 'bus at the time, Old witness thet the driver WAS drunk. Witness followed the 'bus and told defendant to give up the reins to II teley. tie di so.—Joseph Healey gave corroborative evidence, and the defendant was fined 10., including costs. DRUNKENNESS.—The following were dealt with for drunkenness :—lsaac Hill Bromley, 47, Nienolson-street. fined 5s and costs ; Mary Quinn, no setled abode. fleet.' 55 ; and Kate Ann Jon's, ot 9. Ciarimes-yard, committed to prison for 14 days with hard labour. 11IsKING UP bk LOST TIME.—Tho Mai; Norris, a bus driver, residing at 17, Edgeworth-etreet, was charged with furiously driving three horses and a 'bus in Sankey-street, on the 7th October. He pleaded not guilty, —P.C. Monks said that at 10.15 p.m. oa the 7th inst he was on duty in Sankey-street when he noticed two horses attached to a 'bus going at a furious rate, he should think at about ten miles an hour. The streets were crowded at the time. He called out to defendant who was driving the horses, but he would not stop, so witness folio wel him to the Ma...lce-place. The 'bus was half an hour late.—Defendant WAS sober.—The Chief Constable said he was no doubt driving rapidly in order to snake up for lost time. It was a common practice. , —P.C. Hodgkiss gave corroborative evidence.— [he defend- ant said , hat when near the park getes his horses took fright and he lost control over them.—He was fined in the costs. 3s. A WARNING TO Boys —Walter Stowe (11), 3L, O'cl-roed, and John Duffy (13) 1, Prince-street, were surnmonst for throwing mud and gravel in St. James street on the 18th inst,—David Wallis, a checker in the employ of the War- rington Carriage Company, said that at 8.15 p.m. on the date mentioned (Sunday), in consequence of 'complaints he received from 'bus conductors about boys throwing at the 'buses, he got on a 'bus at Bridge-street. Whet; passing the end of St. Jarnee'-street he saw the defendants running towards the 'bus with emellsticks and straps in their hands. They threw mud and gravel at the 'bus, which was full of passengers. One lady was struck in the face with a small stone —Ernest Wells (15). cenductor of the 'bus, corrobore- ted.—The Chairman thought the boys deserved birching.— The Chief Constable said he had seen lads throw lighted crackers at horses.—After being ceutioned defendants were fined in the costs (6t.) FooTBALLING IN THE STREET 3. —William Miller, Arthur Miller, Harry Bate, and William FLI'I49:IB, youths residing in Long,ord-street, were summoned for playing football at the rear of Dudley-street on the Bth inst.—P.C. Smith said that at 3.20 on the date is amed he was on duty in Loneford-street in company with P.C. Howard. They were both in plain elothes. Hearing shouting at the ba'k of the street they pro- ceeded there and SAW the defendants playing football in an entry leading into Dudley-street. They obtained their names and addresses and reported them.—The Chief Con- stable said the police had receive! numerous complaints of footbelling in the streets on Sunday afternoons.—Defendents were fined In the costs which amounte t to 3s. 6d. A LLE..SED WIFE Dee ERTION. —Frank Michael Sanderson McGee, Aikin-street, WAS charged with deserting his wife, Saran Jane McGee.—He pleaded not guilty. —Mr. A. Browne appearea us behalf of the complainant. He said that she applied or a separation order against her husband. The parties were married at St. Paul's Churen on the sth August, 1893, and up to twelve months eive lived comfort- ably together. A dispute tnen arose about a niece of 'he defendants who was living with them. The niece wets rather impudeut to complainant but deteadant would not allow her to punish her. On Monday last the girl was im- pudent and co nplainent boxed her ears. When defendant came home, the girl told him about it. An altercation be- tweenteornplainant and her husband ensued, and in the end defendant told her to clear out, threatening what he would do to her if she did not. She was so frightened that she went away and ha! not been back since.—Defenlant said it was through his mother-ia-law'sl interference that the dispute took place.—The magistrates decided to adjourn the case a month to see if a settlement could not be arrived at. TUESDAY.—B4fore Mr. Birehell (is the cheer), and Councillor Shaw Green. THEFT OF BOoT9.—Alfreil flamer Spilling, 42, Cairo-street, was charged with stealing a pair of boots v alue 63. lid, from the shop of the Bankruptcy Boot Company on Saturday night —The Chief Constable said that at 733 on Saturday night prisoner entered the shop of the Bankruptcy Boot Company, Horsernerket-street, there beiag a number of cus- tomers in at the time. An assistant named Miss Hill noticed , that he was standing near to a pair of hoots in a box without a lid on. The manager, Mr. Roberts Was serving on the out- side of the counter an 1 when he went behin 1 the counter to get some laces the man left the shop hurriedly, carrying a boot under each arm underneath his coat. They went out- side but could not see the man. Information was subse- quently given to the police and prisoatee avag erresteti oil Monday night by P.C. Plumb and Detective neicock. He denied the offence. At nine o'clock on Saturday night, prisoner. It was ascertained pawned the boot at Mr. floueh- ton's shop. Mersey-street. obt.ining 4s. for them. That was his first offence and as he did not appear to be a thief he had had a sudden temptation which was acceler +tied by drink. He thought a small fine would meet the case,—Prisoner Was fined 205. DRUNK IN CHA.RGE. -TOOrria3 liughts and Patrick Cairns were charged with being drunk is charge of two horses and slurry In Bold-street, at 8-50 the previous night. —P.O Gibes said that he received a complaint from a gentlemen that prisoners were drunk in charge of two horses and a lurry in Bo'd-street. He proceeded there in company with PC. Rasp and found the prisoners outside the" Woolpack " Inn. They were both drunk, with the assistance of P.C. Hasp he brought them to the Police Station and took the horses and iurry tn Mr. Bennie, Liverpool road, to whom they belonged. e—Corroborative evidence having been given prisoners were each fined 10s, WEDNESDAY,—Before Alderman Feirclough (in the chair) and Messrs. Geddes and fireball. ALLEGED WIFE AssauLT.—James Lawreesoa, of Seott-st., was charged on a warrant with assaulting his wife, Amelia Lawrenson on the 14th October.—Prisoner was represented by Mr. A. Browne.—Complainant said that on the dete men- tioned her husband came home slightly under the influence of drink. She was poking thu fire and be took the poker from her and struck her with it. He then got hold of her by the throat and tried to choke her, and also struck her on the ear. Her son interfered and he hit him and ran him out of the house.--Thomas William Lewrenson gave evidence In support.—Mr Browne said he advised prisoner t 3 plead guilty to an aggravated assault on conditions that the magis- trates simply recorded a conviction and made a separation order.—The Chairman thought it would be better if the man signed the pledge and lived with his wife.—Prisoner said he could not live with her.—The Bench ultimately decided to adjourn the ease for a month so that the parties could draw up an agreement between them.</p>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions. WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. 9r.: Comber (in the chair), J.</p>
        <p>White and Councillor J.__Cro.sfield._</p>
        <p>.. FAILING TO DIP SLIEEP —John Higham, Rixton. WAS summoned for cau,ing sixty-seven sh2ep to oe moved from Manchester to Rixton on the 1,3t14 Septernber,and not having them dipped twice within 14 days from the removal. —P.C. Clarke said he saw the sheep taken to the defendant's farm. He read the regulations over to the defendant's son and he promised to have them dipped. He failed to do so however.—Defend- ant said the sheep did not belong to him. He wrote to the owner and he sail, he would send a man to dip them. He did not arrive till the 14 days had expired.—A fine of lOs was imposed. SUSPECTED POACEEF:RS. —John Bolton and John: Arstall of Hollins Green, were charged with being in unlawful posses- sion of game at Rixton on the 4th October.—P.C. Clarke said that at 5-30 on the date named he met the defendants. Arstall was carrying a gun. He searched them and found a hare in Bolton's coat pocket. Arstall said the hare belonged to fine of 5s and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>GA.MBLING AT BURTONWOOD.—Thornas Blakely, Henry Blakely, John Blakeley, William Duxbury, and Richard Williams, all of Burtonwood were summoned for playing pitch and toss at Burtunwood on the Bth inst.—P.O. Gerrie proved the case.—Richard Williams and John Blackley who had previously been befo-e the court, were tined ss, aud the others were ordered to pay 2s 6d. THEFT OF I WATCEIGUARD AT CrTERDLEY.—WiIIiam Price was charged with stealing from a dwelling house at Cuerdley some time between the 20th September and the 12th October. a silver watch guard valued at 25e, the property of George Tuson.—He pleaded guilty.—Frem the evidence given it appears that prisoner was employed by the prosecutor. When the watchguard was missed, he helped to search the plate for It. On the 23rd September he went to Mr. Nield's pawnshop at Widnes and pledged the guard. receiving 23 6d. He said his wife and family were starving. On being apprehended by P.O. Coupe he at first denied all knowledge of the offence, but subsequently admitted his gout and produced a pawn- ticket for the guard.—A fine of 10a and costs was imposed. MISCELLANEOUS —For riding bicycles on the footpath Henry Holding, Fearnhead ; Samuel Maines. John Henry Jennion, William Gaffrey.and William Taylor, of Croft, and Moses Jones of Fearnhead, were each fined is. and costs ; for being drunk and disorderly at Burtonwood on the 30th September. Thomas Harrimond, and Patrick Hammon were fined the former ss. and costs and the latter 5s ; Joseph Quinn, of Warrington,was fined ss. for being asleep in charge ora donkey and cart sat Sankey on the 4th mat; John Dalton, Winwick, was fined ss. and costs for neglecting to send his child to school regularly ; John McHugh, Warring- ton, was fined 53. and costs for being drunk and disorderly at Winwick on the Bth inst. EXEMPTION FRoM VACCINATIoN.—JOIm William Walters, .iverpool-road, Sankey, applied tcr an order to exempt his child from vaccination. He said he thought vaccination would be detrimental to the child's health.—Councillor Cros- field said he would sympathise with him if they were allowed to vaccinate with lymph taken from another child as he considered that was dangerous, but now they did it with calf lymph the danger was removed. He did net think he need fear the consequences under the present circurn- stances.—Applicant said he would sooner risk his child dying of small-pox than have it Vaccinatei.—The order was granted.</p>
