      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington win again. The " Wirepullers " stature on Saturday was an in- teresting oaf, for several reasons. Millom, the visitors, have made a great name for theruselye in Cumb-ndand, and last season capped all previous efforts by winning their way into the prtmer c nnoatiti on. During the years of their success they have enjoyed one or two fix- tures with the Warrington Club, and won each time. Last season they defeated the home club at Wilderspool, and the Warrington men have since looked forward to Saturday's encounter as the occasion on which they meant to be avenged. Millom brought a strong tbam and a fine day aith them, while Warrington also had norhing to complain about in the way of representation. There was a good crowd presen-,numbering about 5,000 and the teams lined up as follows: Warrington : Ashurst, full back ; Wood, Jones Isherwood and Harris, three-quarters ; Bate and Duck- worth, half-backs ; Taylor, Naylor, Morrison, Boardman Sudlow, Fairhurst, cleoltze, and Eden forwards. Millom: Inch. full back; Eagers, J Waitehead, Strike and Lathom, thre -quarters ; Northmore and G White- head, halt-backs; Buckett. Hoggo.rth, Scott, J R Moores, J Moores, Smicheram, Wilson and Bee•ham, forwards. Warrington lost the toss, and played against the sun, Jones ttarting operations with a kick which was well returned. Millom followed up with promptness and energy, Ashurst being lucky to avert a score in the first minute. The narrow escape roused the home lot up, and they male tracks for the other end, where t'vey eventually got a footing as the result of good play by Bate. Isherwood and Harris gained further ground by gond combination, but Milloin responded with a nice round of passing. Asnurst was called upon and cleared with a powerful punt. Eagers was tackled in his own half and Warrington set about fiading an opening. Duckworth made one awful piss wnich nearly let Warrington down, Ashurst atoning for the mistake by kicking the ball half the length of the field. Some nice punting by Inch and Jones followed, the advantage remaining with the home side who followed up well. Isherwood intercepted a pass in front of goal and made a splendid dash for the line, Inch only just checking him in time. Malone kicksd out and Ashurst tried a drop at goal which went near. The visitors now rallied and following a mistake by the home full back they m tde considerable headway by combined movements. Latham go: off down touch, Duckworth bringint him down and send- ing the bill cieo.r. Harris punted well and Strike mull. ing the catch Eden dashed ukon him and raced over with the ball amid cheering. Jones failed at a some what easy place. Fine play by Bate neutra- lised all Millorn's efforts to change the venue Eden almost scored again by speedy follow- ing up Inch having to kick the ball over the line. Millon carried the ensuing pack but Jones and Wood ran back again the latter nearly crossing at the corner. Jones dropp-d at goal unsuccessfully, and then a fine punt by J. Whitehead caught itshurst napping. Hoggarth and Eagers dribbled the ball past him and the latter piekineg up went over the line but lost possession most unfortu- nately for his side. Bate saved, and Warrington had a lucky escape. They showed their appreciation by at- tacking strongly, Ashurst nearly landing a goal from centre. VVarriagton scrimmaged on the line and a series of quick passes between Duckworth, Isherwood, and Harris enabled the wing man to get in at the corner. Jones again took the kick and this time landed a grand goal hone touch. Warrington maintained the pressure to the interval whoa the score was 8 points to nil in their favaur. - . .</p>
        <p>nuckeht te-tEarteci ani the opening stape were in mid- field. Millona obtained possession but jonel promptly grassed Northmore. The next movement was on the other side. Bate. Duck worth, Isherwood, and Harris, combined bea.utitully,"and the wing man outpaced the Milloaa backs and scored finely near touch. Jones made another good kick but the ball passed outside. Wa-- rington continued to attack httly Duckw ,rth twice pu Cn.l in a short pant over the line which nearly brought about Millom's downfall. Bate threw across to Wood who knocked on with a possible opening. Duck- worth then t ied to score himself, but slipped after he had passe I the Mlllom backs. Millom made several bursts to the home quarter but were easil repulsed. On one occasion Taylor picked up at centre and ran strongly for the tine, only being capturei when a score seamed certain. Naylor did get over, but laylor had thrown forw trd. Towards the c'ose Warrington took m tttera rather more easily, and Lathom twice made Lract efforts to scora. Barris was too quick for him, however, and the end arrived with Millom beaten pointless by one goal, three tries (11 points). -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Runcorn's First Defeat.</head>
        <p>Runcorn's First Defeat.</p>
        <p>To an observer of form, prior to Saturday it would have seemed almaet impossib:e for Stockport to have beaten Runcorn, yet this is what they dil, and in a manner teo which bespoke a warm time of it for future visitors tn the EJgeley Park Ground. Glorious weather prevailed and the " gate" numbered fully 8,000. The composition of the respective si, IPR was diff srent t what had been ad ,ertiFei. For the first time this season the Runcorn officials found it m ces•iery to make changes in the team. Langley and Tnornason were injured at Hull on Wednesday last aed were tmable to take the field, and the places of these two players were filled by those two capable forwards Woods and Darlington. whilst C. Butterworth officiated in the stead of Wilkinson at Ithreequarter. On the home side Worsley owing to injury I stood down,and Beath from half-back was his substitute. Burrows who could not get off work, had in his place Taylor. Puactual to time Oram commence I for Stockport against a slight breeze, the ball rolling into touch. Run- corn early on were seen to advantage, Houghton's long kicking being of much service to his side. Jones missed a high pass by Jolley, and getting relief by some smart half-back play the home men invaded the visitors' line, Oram kicking dead. Scrambling Vey was the order for about ten minutes, and then working well together. Jones, Warder, and J Butterworth carried the leather to the opposite end. where Saville touched down. A fecdness for long kicking spoiled Houghton's play to some extent, or otherwise he was playing a rattling game. Booth essayed a run no his own account, but was held by Faulkner when a score looked certain. k capital mark by Jolley relieved, but Cieugh and Williams led a rush back, the forme- beating J. Butterworth in a race for possession, but Houghton cleared. Jones next had hard lines in net scoring, ano'her high pass proving too much for him. There was little to choose up to new between the teams, but gradually Stockport assumed the upper hand. Taylor. one of the Runcorn forwards, fell on the ball and fractured a rib. He left the field, and took no further part in the game. Houghton kicked marvellowly, but eventually he was beaten by Williams, who went over the line with the ball and scored. Saville amid frantic cheering, added the major points. Beacom made but spasmodic efforts to score after this, while Stockport were playing grandly. Thrice the game was stopped owing to injuries to Runcorn players. At the interval Stockport were leading by a goal and a try to nothing. After the interval it was expected that Runcorn would make things hum, but they did not. Crocker, the old Swinton man, make three marks in as many minutes, and this neutralised the work of Jolley and Faulkner at half. Then there was another delay through an injury to Oram, and hardly had a recommencement been made when Gayter was injured. Jones endeavoured to relie•e the misfortunes of his side with a beautiful run, but he was pushed into touch. Butterworth and Warder were next seen to advantage in mid-field, where some nice</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 20, 1899.</p>
        <p>passing took place. Hodgson got away. and giving to Bevan, the latter scored at the corner. The place kick proved unsuccessful. Runcorn pressed, and were get- ting dangerous for the first time, when the whistle went. Fintl score: Stockport, one goal, two tries (8 pointi) ;</p>
        <p>easily repulsed them, and the Rovers were soon again peppering the visitors goal. Although the homesters had practically all the play up to the interlal they could not BCOre, weak shooting when close in being chiefly responsihle for this. Half-time : Na score. Resuming the Rovers early assumed the aggressive and after about4five minutes attacking Ellison scored their first goal from long range. Roused by this reverse the Albions took up the running and Water worth was called uoon from the right wing, but he proved equal to the occasion. The Rovers second go It was milieu through after an exciting scrimmage in the goal mouth. This seemed to dishearten the visitors and the Rovers had matters all their own way to the finish, but did not score again thus running out winners by two goals to nil.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN ALEIONS v. ST. HELENS Y.M.C.A.—This match was played at St. Helens on Saturday, before a fair gate. The hotnesters won the toss and elected to play with the hill in their favour. The Alblons early assumed the ag.eres4ve and a grand shot from the foot of Bramm)r missed the post by inches. Midfield play followed for a time neither side claiming any material advantage. A foul against the Albions took play into their goal-tnotith lost all efforts to score ware rendered null by the splendid defensive tactics of the visitors backs and custodian. Just before the interval the Albions were granted a penalty and this was taken by Owen. The ball was sent in at lightning speed but whether by luek or good ju igoment the ball struck the custodian's foot and rebounded half the length of the field. Half- time no score. Resuming the Y.M.C.A. rushed up the field but Eckersley relieved the pressure with a timely kick. A nice rue up the wing by the home right was followed by a fine centre from the outside man. This was met and promptly put between the posts by the inside left, the only goal of the match thus fa'ling to the Y.M.C.A.. The Albions tried hard to equalise after this but all their efforts were futile. Final : St. Helens Y.M.C.A. one, Earlestown Albien nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ROCHDALE HORNETS v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>ROCHDALE HORNETS v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At RochJale. in glorious weather, before 9,000 specta- tors. Th first half of the game was splendidly con- tested. Slifer, Williams, and Davies scored tries, and Thomas kick”cl a goat for Oldham, and Rigg kicked a goal for ta.i Ilornets. Odhan d,cl pnitty much as they plead in the Er?.cond half, and Williams and Martins scored tri.s. It!solt—Dilham, 1 goal and 5 tries ; Hor- nets 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Sr. HELENS v. WIGaN.</head>
        <p>Sr. HELENS v. WIGaN.</p>
        <p>At S. Illeo. Ii the first half Rennie made a mark, and fravno• trcked a goal for St;. EL‘leni. Liversage foilowe 1 WICh Lfool try, but no goal was kicked. In She sec ml half Wigan oconA strongly, and seemed liketly to NCON., but they did not do s). St. Hil-ms tnen had the best of matters. Result—St. Helens, 1 goal and 1 try ; Wizan nil. SALFORD v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>At Salford btifore 15,0J0 spec:acora. Larno started for the RingPr.], and after ten rninuces' play Thompson scored, Nelson not improving. Sticitney next scored, and at balf-time the Rangers led by 6 points to nil. Upon changing ends [leach started and play continued to be foight out very keenly, and just on time Dawson scored a brilliant try. Reault—Broughton Rangers, 3 tries ; Salford,</p>
        <p>TYLDEgLEY v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>At Tyldisley, before 4000 spectators: Woodward started for the home team, and the game at once be- mire exn tinc,,. Early on Grindley missed a certain try t n Lig a pass. Caldicott, for the yisitors,soon at yr , .aiid in the second half Winstanley dropped a granl goal. Caldicott played splendidly, and scorcd a third try. Reault—.Leigh, 11 points; Tyldesley nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>VVIDNE3 SWiN roST</head>
        <p>VVIDNE3 SWiN roST</p>
        <p>At Widnes,in beautiful weather,before 5000 spectators. Swinton brought a s:rong tsam, but Widnes were minus Donnelly and Kelly. In he first half Widnes had the advantage, Ribpan dropping a val. Aspey (Widnes) and Hampsou (Swinton) retired injared, but the Wid- nesian resumed in the second half. Lewis and J Valen- tina sccred a try each,and M)rgan and Goodman placed goals. Result—Swinton, 2 gaals 2 tries; VVidaes, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>CLDEIAM v. STOCKPORT.</head>
        <p>CLDEIAM v. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>The return match between these teams was played en Tuesday at Oldham. The home team was in good form and won by 16 points to 5. Arthur Lees, Williams, Marti 3, and Lawton scored the trios for the whiners, Frater kicking 2 goals. Williams sc3red the try for S:ockport, Stvill id iing the goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>BRADFORD v. BRAAILEY.</p>
        <p>A close, but rather rough game was witnessed on the Bradford ground, the lalme side winning by one dropped goil and one try to nil. Cooper ran in the try, the same player droppipg the goal. CAS fLEFORD v. LIVERSEDGE.</p>
        <p>The home side had much the b?tter of this game at CAstleford, and won by one goal and two tries to one dropped 'goat. Brady and Barton gained the tries, while Qarrett droppei the loiers' goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HULL v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>HULL v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Fully 14,600 peop'e viewed this game at Hull. No:hing was scored till the second half, when Williams obtained a try for the visiting side which Cooper con- ver,:ed, Huddersfield winning by 5 points to nil.</p>
        <p>LEEDS v. LEEDS PARISH CHURCH. This match, at Leeds, resulted in a win for Leeds by one goal and three tries to one goal and one try. Lum- ley, Jackson, and Parfitt score,' for the winners, and 111)seley and Hewitt for the los2rs,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EIUNSLE I' v. HOLBECK.</head>
        <p>EIUNSLE I' v. HOLBECK.</p>
        <p>A large crowd assembled on the Hunslet ground, the home fifteen winning by two goals (one dropped) and one try t') a try. Albert Go:dthorpe did all the scoring for the winners, McCarthy securing Ho!beck's try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WAKEFIELD TRINIFY MANNINGfIAM.</head>
        <p>WAKEFIELD TRINIFY MANNINGfIAM.</p>
        <p>Wakefield was the venue of this match, the home team gaining a creditable victory by one goal and two trios to nil. Malkin aril Howell ob:ained the tries, Met- calfe canverting one. BATLEY v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS.</p>
        <p>The Rangers were outplayed, at Batley, being defeated by tvr) goals and two tries to nil. Auty dropped a goal and Oath an' Davies obtained the tries, the latter also kicking a goal:_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EIALIFAX v. KINGSTON ROVERS.</head>
        <p>EIALIFAX v. KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>_ A keen struggle was witnessed at Halifax, where the home side just won by eleven points to ten. Ambler Wood. Smith. and Nicholls were among the scorers for their respeltive sides.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WARRINGTON " A " v. FLIXTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " v. FLIXTON.</p>
        <p>This friendly match was played at Flixtcn on Satur- day. The following team represented Warrington. 803C3W, full back; Hockenbull, Ratcliffe, Moores and Fish, threr-quartere ; Bate and Hallam. half-back 3 ; Deakin, Merryweather, Cunningham, Wilcox, Swift, Jtlev, Parker and Price, foz-wardt. Warrington won the t-mis and Jolley kicked off. During almost the whole of the first half play was confined in the home 25. Hockenhull scored a try for Warrington, Fish failing at goal. From a forwtrd rush Swift scored, the try not being improved upon. Owing to bad passing by the Warrington forwards, several opportunities to score were lost. Half-time : Warrington, two tries, Flix:on, nil. On resuming, play was of a very rough character, Hallam being ordered off the field. Fish got the ball in the 25. and scored a fine try, which Ratcliffe converted. Final: Warrington, 1 goal, three tries (11 points), Fax- On, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>English Cup. WARRINGTON v. Nvi-rwsca.— At Nantwich on Satur- day. Warrington bad previously met Middlewich, whom they d-feated by five goals to one. The Warring- ton team on Saturday was as follows :—Aspinall, goal ; Mather and Downie, backs ; Graham, Hackett, and Mc- Gray, half-backs; Riley, Jones, Babies, Murray, and Connor, forwards. In the first half Nantwich kisked down the s'ope, with the sun in their eyes. The play on both sides was loose, and somewhat uninteresting. The home team, however, was the first to attack, but Aspin- all proved safe for a time. Throughout the first portion of the game Warrington gave an indifferent display, and the only player who gave a good account of himself was Riley, who sent in some capital shots. Nantwicn played the bettr game and F c)rect twice, the first through Howard, after some capital passing by the right, wing, and the second thr3ugh Mort. In the second half Warrington improved in a remarkable manner, and had the b st of the exchanges until the finish. They scored once, and had hard luck in not registering a sec 3nd point. While Maybury was out of the goal tne, ball struck the cross-bar. The game rPsulted in a victory for Naatwich by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GRAMNIAR SCHOOL V. HIILIIE GRAMMAR ScitooL.—Played on the ground uf the former. In the first half Warrington had the best of the game but could not score, and the score at haif time was 1-0 in favour of Huhn, Grammar Scho3l. In the second half botls sidos scored, and the game endel hi a win for Hulnae Grammar Schcol. Final Grammar School WO goals; Warrington Gramnaar School, one goal.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS v. NEW BRIGHTON TOWER RE- SERVE.—Nursery Park was the scene of another grand tussle for supremacy on Saturday. The Saturday pre- vious Newton had administered the first check to the victorious career of Hudsons who for the last two or three years have practically walked away with the chlmpionship of the combination. The soapmen went away with a very high opinion of the Newton team and a registered vow to simply swamp the regre.,entatives of Nursery Park when they visited Liverpool, The Towerites arrived with a very strong team and Newton had also their full strength up. The first half was fiercely contested the Newtonians if any, having slightly the best of matters, but nearly half-an-hour had passed before they managed to net the ball. Half-time,Newton 1 ; Tower, nil. The second half was as fast and exciting as the first had been, and after a period of heavy pres- sure t he visitors contrived to make the scores equal. The homesters tried hard to gain the lead but all their efforts were futile, and the whistle blew with the score Now- ton-'e-Willows, 1 ; New Brighton Tower Reserve, 1.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN V. BLACKPOOL:—TIaest3 teams met for the second time this season oa the Raikes Hall ground. Blackpool, in gloriously fine weather. After Leadbetter had opened the scoring with a long, beautifully accurate shot, Blackpool had by far the greater share of the game, but good gnalkeeping on the part of Chew pre- vented any further addition. Just before half-time Leach, the Earlestown inside left, sent In a shot, which Fletcher mulled in a perfectly unaccountable manner, and before the custodian could recover himself the ball was netted. In the second moiety the visitors played a dashing game, ad time after time got into the Black- pool qaarters. They had hard lines, however, in the shape of an QfT-side goal, put through by Harrison. The game ended without further addition to the score, and the nsult was thereon a draw of one goal each. As an exhibition of football it was extremely disappointing. Neither side showed anything approaching cembination, and at times the tactics employed were too rough to be pleasant. In such a game it is impossible to say that anyone specially distinguished himself, Of the forwards perhaps Le Whetter, for Blackpool. and Harrison, for their opponents, showed to most advantage. Green, toi, in the Earlestown half-back line,did yeoman service EARLE9TOWN ROVERS V. WARRINGTON ALlTlONT.—War- ringtOn Albion, the new members of the Warrington and District League, were the visitors to the Mesnee Ground on Saturday. The day was an ideal one for football, and there Was a fair mat 'r of spectators present, when hav- ing lost the toss, the visitors s'arted the game against the hill. The Rovers w ,re the first to become dangerous and a corner was forced but this was put behind. After a spell of defensive work, the Alhions broke away, but they were not allowed to become dang row, as Crowther</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WINTErt'S TALE.</head>
        <p>A WINTErt'S TALE.</p>
        <p>You who work in factories koow what it is to come from the heated rooms of the mill into the cold and clammy fogs of a winter's evening. You get chilled to the bone, and often suffer right through the winter from a chronic cold and huskiness of the voice. Has it ever occurred to you to try Lavans Lung Linctus (you can buy it at the Co-op.). As a remedy for coughs and colds, and as a preventive of the dangers to health resulting from exposure to the night fogs LAvans Lung Linctus has no rival. Asthma and brsnchitis succumb before it, and viprous and lusty health reign in their stead. Try a bottle and test its efficiency for yourself. We will stand by the result. Lavans Lung Linctus is sold by all chemists and co-operative stons. Post free, is 2s 9d.. and 4s 6 1. from the proprietors, Green and Com- pany, Chemists, Bolton. Mr. D. A. Turnbull, of 249, Woitehall-street,Glasgow, says cannot express to you how highly I value this preparation ; it has b3ea of incalculable benefit to ma personally and to others in my family circle. To one, who, like myself, at this season of the year, is sub ject to colds in the head and throat, with all their accomoanying inconveniences, Lavans Lung Linctus is simply invaluable ani I can heartily recommend its use."</p>
