      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>OFF TO THE TRANSVAAL.</p>
        <p>Warrington . Reservists Called Out.</p>
        <p>During tTie week a number of army reservists have left the :own and priceeded to the several depots there to be drafted for service in the Transvaal. In some cases, the summons to join the colours was very brief, but without exception the calls were responded to. In several cases there have been pleasant little gatherings at which the friends have bidden the reservists "God speed," and in the case of one reservist—a postman—he was the recipient of a small souvenir from his comrades. This took place on Monday night in the Blue Lion Hotel, Market Place under the auspices of the Warringtoa Post- men's Federation, and nad been specially held for the 'purpose of saying good-bye to Herbert Roscoe, who has been called upon to de duty in the Transvaal. There were about 40 postmen presenr. Mr. John Skelton was unanimously elected to the chair, alai kn a short speech Ihe spoke of the good feeling which existed towards their friend,who was about to leave them to do his duty to their beloved Queen and country. He hoped he would be spared to rejoin the postmen's ranks. He (the chairman) in the name of the postal staff of Warrington, then pre- sented to Roscoe a beautiful silver-mounted pipe and case, with pouch well filled with tobacco, observing that he hoped he would think of those dear to him at home, and that he would return safe and sound. (Applause.) Roscoe was so § upset by the presentation and the refer- ences to him, that he asked to be allowed half an hour in which to try to " pull himself together," as he was too full to speak. The Chairman consented, and called upon all present to drink to the health of the Queen and country, whom their friend and comrade was about to serve. All rose and drank the " Health of the Queen," and sang " G)d save the Queen." Then followed a song by Ms. J. R-yriolds, an I the toast" Trio Army and Navy," and a song by Mr. J. Leicester, and the toast" rho Reserve &amp;meets." Songs were also rendered during the evening by Messrs. Toker, Hewitt, A ukland, Bibby, Walsh, Goodfellow, Mr. T. Boardman presiding at trie piano. The Secretary of the Postmen's Federation (Mr. W. Readett) put in an appearance during the evening, and being called upon to speak alluded to the respect entertained for comrade Roscoe, and said that he in the name of the postmen, wished hint "God speed." (Applause) Mr. Roscoe then rose to respond. He said he could not find words to express his feelings, but he hoped they would take the will for tee deed. the meeting broke up at 11 p.m. On ruesday morning Roscoe, who journeyed to London by the 9.50 train, was escorted to the station by a large number of postmen, amongst those present being the postmaster (Mr. Viney) and the chief clerk (Mr. Broad- hurst) who expressed the hope that Roscoe would be spared and return to Werrington. Police-Coast ablss Hurst and Howard, of the Warriegton Folic Force, who are reserves of the Grenadier Guards, have also left to join their regiment at Wellington Barracks.—Police.Constable Jo wett, of the Lancashire County Constabulary, who has been stationed at Woolsroa for the past three months, left Woelston on Monday mid-night to join his regiment, the Scots Greys, who on Tuesday left this country for the Transvaal. 'Mora doing so he was entertained by his friends to a dinner at the Rope and Anchor lan. About 30 sat down to an excellent repast, served by the land- lord, Mr. Henry Daiutith. A short toast list was gone through. Mr. William Houghton presided, and he was supported by several well-known gentlemen. The first toast was," The Queen and the Royal Family," submit- ted by the Chairman. It was drunk wire) musicall honours. Mr. James Newton proposed " fee Army, trio Navy, the Volunteers, and the colonial Tr ,OpS." The toast of "The Parting Guest" was also sureeeteee P.C. Jowett suitably responded, and the rest of tee e :ening was spent in a convivial manner: Songs were given by Messrs Finch, Fryer, Turley , Deintith, and N,esston, the latter gentleman's songs being in the Leneasnire dialect. We understand that the constable's wife and family will be supported during his absence by the County Constabu- lary, and that his eituation will be reserved for him till his return.</p>
        <p>Another Army reserve man in Warrington who has been called out is a young man named William lerb3rt Barton, who has been employed at the Bewsey Wirerope Works. A large number of his friends saw. him off on Tuesday from Bank Quay Station, to join his regiment, the 14% Field Battery Royal Artillery at Aldershot. On Monday evening about 70 of his fellow workmen met at the Imperial Hotel, Bewsey-road, to bid him farewell and God-speed. Mr. S. Archer presided, and an excellent entertainment was provided. Mr. Perkins, the landlord, made a felicitous speech, the sentiments of which were cordially approved by the company. An enjoyable even- ing was brought to a close with the singing of " Rule Britannia," and" God save the Queen."</p>
        <p>At ten o'clock on Tuesday morning Charles Smith,who resided at 12, Wellfield-street, and who was a p3rter em- ployed at Bank Quay Railway Station, received a tele- gram from the military authorities requesting him to join his reiment at once at Chatham. He left by the two o'clock train, and his departure was witnessei by a large number of relatives, friends and others who happened to be on the station at the time. He bore the ordeal of the separation from his frends remarkably well, and as the train was steaming off he put his head out of the carriage window, and in a loud voice called out" Majteoa ! Majuba !" Needless to say a hearty cheer was raised, a hope being expressed that he would return safely to Warrington. An effort is being made in the town for the relief of the Transvaal refugees, and a special performance was given on Friday afternoon at the Royal Court Theatre, of " The Span of Life." The entire proceeds (by kind- ness of Mr. J. A. Atkin and Mr. J. Potter and members of the company) are to be devoted to the Transvaal Refugees Relief Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WALPOLE AND THE WHIGS.</head>
        <p>WALPOLE AND THE WHIGS.</p>
        <p>Lecture at the Co-operative Hall, Mr. F. E. Sserm, M A, B C L, barrister-at-law, of Liverpool,delivered the second of a course of six lectures on the political history of England in the Eighteenth Century, in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, War- rington, on Wednesday evening. The subject of tl: lecture was " Walpale and the Whigs." At the outs( t ho reviewed the period under discussion at the previous lecture, and pointed out that the resolution of 1683 was an important chronological land mark in English history The importance of that revolution did not consist in the substitution of one line of monarchs for another line of monarchs but consisted in the enunciation and clear recegnition for all time of the great fact that in the future the form of governmeat in this country was not the king and the country but be nation and the king. The great principal was laid down that the king ruled in this country not by virtue of an arbitrary prerogative or by " right divine " but ruled only by the free asaent of his subjects, and if that assent were taken away, the whole base on which the kindly prerogative depended was at the same time taken away too. In the preceding lecture he also attempted to show the nature of th great continental struggle in which this country was embroiled in checking the growing power of France,and the successful steps taken in shat direction by William 111. and Marlborough. Dealing with the Act ot Settle- ment, by virtue of which the Hanoverian dynasty ruled, the Lecturer pointed out that they did so provided they were not tainted by Raman Catholicism, and paid a tribute to the honesty of James 11. in refusing to tern- porise with his religious principles for the sake of retain- ing the English throne. It was his attachment to the Roman Catholic religion which cosc him his throne. Proceeding to deal with the general relations of the two great political parties during the reigns of George I. and George li., he said it was very necessary to understand what was the policy of the Whigs and that question was identical with the question, what was the policy of Walpole ? and then in contra-distinction they must ask, what was the policy of the Tines? The whole history of the Tory party during Walpole's career was an at- tempt on their part.and not always a successful attempt totget rid of their former predispositions of the authority by" Right divine," It was the fear of the Stuarts that formed the real basis of Whig supremacy Walpole stood pre-eminent among his contemporaries, and the question arose, ho er was it that this man who possessed on the surface so very few of Use attractive gifts of the statesman, was able to establish in this country a degree of political influence and maintain it unimpaired for a period which hardly any other states- man had approached. Tht answer was Co be found in the fact that devoid as he was of the qualities of seates- manship, he possessed in a surprising degree a gift which many a statesman had been without, and that was an intuitive perception of what it really was the nation wanted, and the almost equal acute perception of how far it could be conceded. If they asked them- selves what was the one thing needed to place English commerce on a secure basis they would see that the elementary necessity was peace ; continuous peace. Walpole was undoubtedly the greatest peace minister this country had ever known. Lord Sunderland's tenure of office was referred b, and the Lecturer dealt at some length iwith the attempt of Sunderland to prevent the creation of new peers, and thus prevent the number of members of the House of Lords from increasing. The Smith Sea troubles which ultimately brought Walpole back to power were touched upon. Walpole's displomacy which was so successful in preserving peace was carefully exam- amined and commended. As an example of Walpole'a ability as a financier, the ExcDe Bill of 1733 was re- ferred to and the lecturer characterised it as one of the most remarkable proposals ever made by an English finance minister. Hie mode of dealing with the Colonies was an enlightened one. The same idea had been developed in the hands of greater financiers and had led</p>
        <p>to the free trade movement in which the whole econornia history of this country was bound up. Passing rad* &amp;ice was made to Walpole's removal of religious diP abilities, aud his foreign policy was fall? described. Thd guiding principle of Walpole his foreign policy was tho detachment of Englonla from bontinental disputes. He wished to keeP England ciear of all foreign embarrassments, ill the hope that lie might be able to exorcise mediating infltmace and daach :some of the allies from France ona Spain. His domestic policy made pc:lce an imperatist necessity. He was in politics a cynic. His idea et patriots was that he could raise fifty of them within four and twenty hours. He said he had raised many of them within one night. It was but refusing to gratift au unreasonable or an insolent demand that patriots - were made. With reference to the charges of corruption brought against Wi.lpole, the lecturer said because 116 was a corrupt statesman they must not therefore eorl' elude that he was a statesman who called for reprobe tion. They could not dismiss him with the remark tlllO he was corrupt and say he was a bad man. He WO, neither in front of or behind contemp Tary politics+ morality. He was not one of the figures to fire tbe, imagination in the way that Chatham Old, but he In many gOOO qaalities ; he had courage and resource API as a minister be was strong-willed, insistent and cleat" headed.</p>
      </div>
