      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SAD DROWNINb FATALITY AT</head>
        <p>SAD DROWNINb FATALITY AT</p>
        <p>SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inqueet at the Chapel House Inn, Great Sankey, on Wed' nesday evening, on the body of Joseph Ward (66), who resided at 110, Evelyn-street, Sankey Bridge, which was found in the St. Helens and Sankel Canal on Sunday afternoon.—Mr. G. Thompson waS foreman of the jury. The first witness was Louisa Ward, wife of Thornei Ward, residing at 110, Evelyn-street, who stated tbs; deceased was her father-in-law. He was 66 years of age and lived with tnecu. He was a labourer nut was out of work. 0 the 2nd inst. he got up at 7.30 and altar having h,s breakfast went out and said b 6 was going to look for work. He returned IP three o'clo3k in the afternoon, and after having souir thing to eat ho again went out. She did not see WO again till sh- slw him dead on Monday. Sae had nog the slightest suspicion that deceased would take his life. His being out 4:;. w irk did not seem to upset him. and he never complained about it. Hs was blind in one eye through an accident, but that did not seem to trouble him at all.</p>
        <p>Martin McCormick, labourer, rending at 51, Fog' street, said tint on th:, 2nd inst. he was going hang along the bank of the St. Helens canal between eig1:10 awl nine o'clock when he met the deceased. He WO smoking his pipe and was sober. He was going in tbe direction of homo.—lt was not a foggy night. The Foreman; IC 973.3 a ritaer stormy night.' think.</p>
        <p>Witness continuing, said that ht did not notice Or thing strange about him.</p>
        <p>Alexandrii Algie, ironworker, 7, Refuge Square, WO' rington, said that at 4 o'clock on Sunday afternoon hi was walking along the bank of the St. Helens aria, Sankey Canal wheu he saw the body of the deceaseu, floating in the waeer betwuen Buttermilk Bridge alio Messrs. Clare and Ridgway's wh trf. He assisted in OW lug it out of the water.</p>
        <p>P.S. Devlin, statbued at Sankey, sail that he assisted the last witness to get the bxly out of the water and re. removed it to the Cnapel House Inn. He examined ltt but found no marks of violence on the body. in the pockets he found 4Ad in money,a pipe and a small chisel. The body had the appearance of being in the water, twelve or thirteen days. He was informed that decealeo was blind in the left eye. A juryman : It is evident that he slipped in the water accidentally.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said they would have no suspicion Chit decease had taken his life. He had borne his misfor• tunes bravely and did not cm3plain as others would have done. Ho had been seen sober and smoking bit pipe and the question wa3 whether they seemed incline, to go so far ad to say that his death was accidental: There was, however, no direct evidence to show how LIP got into the water.</p>
        <p>- The jury returned a verdict of "Found Drownednthe being no circumstances to show how deceased got int° the water.</p>
        <p>DEATH or MR. H. Y. CATER.</p>
        <p>The death is announced as occurring mi—Thursds9 at Adirondacks. New York State, of Mr. Hamiltoo Y. Castner, managing director of the Aluminiin. Company at Oldbury and of tbe Castner-Kellner Company at Weston Point, near Runcorn. UP Castner, an American citizen and a thorougm chemist, came over to England about 12 years aga with the object of working in this country.. patented process for the manufacture of the Micah" metal sodium. Here he became acquainted with some gentlemen interested in the production a aluminium by a process in which the metal aodile. was required; Mr. Castner devoted himself to tbi. manufacture with such success that the compels, were able to reduce the price of aluminium frofa pounds to shillings. Mr. Castner formerly lived 80 Edgbaston, and subsequently remeved to London. Some months ago his health began to fail, and be journeyed to Florida with a view to regaining bif strength. it was while in New York, on his WO back to England, that the deceased became wets_ef and he died somewhat suddenly. Mr. Castner NV5.7 between forty and forty-five years of age. THE TRAINING COL T.EGE ANTI THE CLERGY DAUGHTERS' SCHOOL, At the Manchester Diocesan Conference on Wed. nesday, the Dean of Manchester made a strong appeal on behalf of the Clergy Daughters' Schal and the Warrington Training College. He snub there was a general misapprehension that thes° institutions were simply Warriagton institution&amp; They were, however, supported by the three dioceses of Chester, Manchester, and Liverpool, all were established, the College for the training ° teachers, and the School for the education of tb6 daughters of the clergy, it was hoped these. daughters of clergy would become Nations+ School mistresses, but in only two or three cases had that been so. Both institutions were flourishing condition, and were undoubtedly VW° ° the most valuable of the institutions in the three dioceses. The school had passed under the g ance of bliss Kennedy, a sister of Mr. Jactire, Kennedy, a lady who was one of the best schn°,ln, mistresses engaged in secondary education. reports of its work were extremely satisfactorr whilst those of the Training College could scarcelt be improved. Both institutions, however, needel expansion in order to meet requirements. It WI been suggested that the school should be removeqh from Warrington. Warrington had an atmosphell —(laughter)—and the buildings were not adapte' to the purposes for which they were used. 'net desired to provide the necessary acc3mmodation the College under one roof, and to take another more than the statutory number for Warringto.! College. For that purpose they desired acquire the premises of the Clergy Daughter!, School for the purpose of the College, and "" take the School somewhere else. The committee was considering bow best to carry out a plan OIL al! lines here indicated. Personally, he had a larger wish. He considered that the most stater manlike plan would be, if possible, to sell t.he premises of both institutions, and bring the Trani' ing College either to Liverpool or Manchester,whel it could be in proximity with Owens College. an, take the Clergy Daughters' School into the count"; For the purposes at present contemplated, howevel some £14,000 was required,and he earnestly appeal for support.</p>
