        <p>•••••• ••• • ..... •••• ••• ..................... ••• ••• • •••</p>
        <p>CONDITIONS. I..—The first coupon Is free, and no competitor can send in snore thAn one free coupon. Unclose Id. starno for every other coupon used. One Wiling Postal Order must be ~alloset If all the c moons on aboveLshew, ,are used Any number nc coup uts 'an be seat in if paid for at the rate of lOne Shilling forleach 20. .—Cross all Postal Orders and keep the numbers. 3.—No competitor can take snore than one share of toe £lo prize. 4.—The coupons must be forwarded to the dx miner offloe Warrington, the envelope to hal eniorset Football Coupon?' so 6ss to be deli‘,ered by the first post nn Thursday morning. If the envelope bears Wednesday's postmark. it will be received up to 5 p.m. on Thursday. Coupons delivered by hand must be received not later than 9 a.m. on Thursday. 5.—Competitors demanding a scrutiny must enclose fee of Ten Shillings, give number of Postal Order (if any), ,and num- ber of coupons sent. In the event of the claim proving correct the fe will be returned. 6.—ln the event of two or more competitors tying, prize money will be divided. 7.—A1l attemots in this competition must be made on coupons cut from this paper. B.—When not less than 40 attempts are made, the coupons of unsuccessful competitors for the coupon prize will be carried forward and added to the number sent in, in the next competition,so that sooner or later the coupon prize must be won by regular competitors 9. —The results will appear in the first issue following the result of the matches.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION. The following were the results of the matches played on October 14th :—Sunderland beat Stoke, Aston Villa beat Notts. County, Bverton beat Notts. Forest. Luton beat Wal- sall, Derby County beat Burniev ; Sheppey United v Grave send, not played; Chatham drew with Bristol City, Warring- ton beat Millom, Swansea beat Devonnort A., Exeter beat BristoL The victory of the "favourite " in each case male the task of giving correct results a very easy one. The fol- lowing comt.etitors gave nine correct results, and therefore divide the £25 prize TWO LOCAL WINNERS SHARE IN THE £25 PRIZE. Mr W Ilutter, 20, Naylor-street, Warrington ; Mr J Lamb, 15, Ellesmere-street, Runcorn ' • Mr J Young. High-street., West Melton, near Rotherham ; Mr 0 iller, Spilsby, Lines ; Mr E Luther. 49, Vicarage-street, Walsall ; Mr W Hamiltesa 60, Sebright-road. High Barnet ; Mr W flatton. 8, Darnley- road, Gravesend ; Mr H Twining, The Thrupp, near Stroud, Glos ; Mr. C W Stanley Horns Road Stroud Glos ; Mr Maidatene, Erinseombe. near Stroud (three shares), Mr E Wadsworth, 51, Violet-street, Derby ; Mr S T Allceck. Weingrores, Denby, Derbyshire ; Mr J W Roelgers, 16, 51111-green, Staveley; 4r W Ttike, n. Silver-street, Bide- ford. D Non .; Mr G Gillard, 14, The Green, Crediton ; Mr F W Woolcock, 21, George-street, Devenpert ; Mr W J G Drower, 57, Almack-road, London; Mr F Gillard. 14, St. Lawreive Green, Crediton ; Mr P Hen- son, Newpott Paenell. Bucks. ( hree shares) ; Mr C J Harris, 8, Manistone-terrace (West), Plymouth; Mr A Martin. 8. Daniel-place, Penzance. Mr K T Sweetland Genista, Victoria road, Daemouth.; Mr W G Dean, 4, Mild- may-street, Plymouth; Mr it D Nelson, R.N.E. College, Davenport (three shares); Mr F D Blackwell, 59, Stoney Stanton-road, Coventry; Mr Rathay„ 18, Burlington-'ad, Bayswater ; Mr R A Truscett, Markt t-place, Holston, Corn- wall ; Mr W B T Wood. 8, Parade, Truro, Cornwall; Mr J Street. Wolverton (two shares) ; Mr .1 P Hodgson, Rose-terrace. Keswick , 1r W G Davis. 143, Ruck- Edge-avenue, Harlesden, London; Mr P Critchley, 36 Bolton-road. Chorley, Lancashire ; Mr E Funnel! c/o Mr M Johnson. Oarshaiton. Surrey; Mr G Mason, Hursley. near Wineirt-ster, Haute; Mr A G Cook, 8. Clarence-terrace, Regent's Park, N.W. ; Mr H Procter, 5, Spring Vale. Haslingden. LaRCI. (three sh sres) ; Mr C Davidson, 169, Linthorpe-road, Middlesbor ugh.;• Mr H B Pittam, 7. Fore-street, London, B.C. (two shares) ; Mr G H Harris, Hill Side Villas, Mount Durand, Guernsey ; Mr</p>
        <p>W Spray, Fiskerten, Lincoln (three shares) ; Mr A Astles, 32. Grosvenor-street, Downing-street. Manchester (three shares) ; Mr P Adamson, Bridge-street, Northampton (two shares) ; Mr G Gibson, He4tber, Ashby-de-la-Zouch (three shares) ; Mr G El Wain, Whitchell, Bilistown, near Leicnter; Mr Jiton, Oxford-street, Coalvil'e. If no other competitor is found to have given nine correct results before Thursday r eat, the above-named competitors will receive Ss. id. for each share due to them.</p>
        <p>THE COHSOLATION PRIZE. No less than 486 competitors gave eight currect results, and are therefore entitled to share the £lO Consolation Prize. As this amount, if divided, would give each competitor less than Fivepence each, we feel sure that in order to prevent , the prize being split up into so many :TAR meats. the winners , would prefer•to compete for the prize again amongst them- selves. Therefore we propose that each competitor who gave eight correct results shall send in one extra attempt on plain p :per in the competition which appears in this issue. The at- tempt can be enclosed in the same envelope as the ordinary coupons for the competition, but should be marked "Consola- tion" in the left-hand corner. All you need do is to copy the names of the teams on to your plain sheet of paper in the same order as given in the coupon in this issue, give the number of the club you select to win, and attach name and address. These coupons will be examined separately, and a prize of £2 given to the competitor sending in the best forecast, and 20 half-cover igns to the 20 next best—£l2 in all. Only the -486 competitors who gave eight correct results can compete. If any competitor is not quite sure if he gave eight correct results or not. he can send in an attempt, but if his name is not in the list, it will, o, course, beedestroyed. If any com- petitor prefers to have his share of the prize, he can do so by sending a postcard to that effect.</p>
        <p>TIjE ACCRECATE PRIZE. The winners of the £25 prize have had three points each placed to their credit for the aggregate prize, and the 486 winners of the £l3 prize one point each, The £3 coupon prize has been won by Mr. H. H. Pittam, 72, Fore-street, London, SCRUTINY.-ANOTHER WARRINGTON WINNER.</p>
        <p>The name of Mr. P. Green. 11, Gilbert•qreet, off Greenall street, Warrington, should have been included in the list o last week's winners of the £lO prize. On scrutiny Mr Green has been found to have named nine winners twice.</p>
        <p>ILater on Mr. Tunstall presented his petition, andsoon after Mr. F. R. Roberts, on his behalf, sought an inter view with the Committee, and at that intrview suggested the following amended terms, viz., the pay- ment of a capital sum, based upon the Post Office Annuity Tables, which would purchase an annuity of two thirds of his nett inc )me during the last five years. The Corpurationto take over such •of his staff as they thought proper, and to compensate those not taken over by the payment of three years' salary. It is worthy of note that the particular tables selected for capitalizing the amount of his annuity were not those giving the smallest sum. The Committee replied, repeating their previous terms. Mr. Roberts rejoined that these were the same terms as previously I.W:eel, and the interview terminated. On the report of the Local Government Board upon the Bill coming to hand, and being distinctly in favour of the scheme of the Corpora- tion, Mr. Roberts in a hurry asked for another inter- view, and on that occasion readily agreed to the follow- ing : that the pension be irds the nett income as suogested, and that the Corporation find work for the whole of his staff to the 25th March 1900 or pay them their salaries to that date. This latter was a suggestion of the committee, and was definitely stated as an act of grace for the purpose of enabling any or all of them in the meantime to look round and obtain other situations. It Mi. Tunstall did net convey this information to his clerks the blame does not rest with the committee. 13e could make no possible mistake as to the commit-t.e's intention, for it was pointed out to him over and over again that the Corporation would not require so large a staff, and in particular Mr. Smith was continuously referred to as the one least likely to be required. This is th- history of the events and circumstances upon which I made my statement, and it is utterly impossible for me to form any conclusion as to how Mr. Tunstall could say that I as Chairman of the Committee agreed with him that his clerks should he taken over or that they are HOW in the service of the Corporation, as service is commonly understood. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The specific objdcts the Corporation had in view wete greater convenience and reduction of cost. As to the former there is no questim—as to the latter we know where it will come in, and we don't ask Mr. Tunstall find the means for further saying. There was no boast- ing of any such saving, no exaltation ef the new officials bat a Vain statement was made that the sub-committee had been appointed to prepare a report.—They will shortly determine what is to be done in rearranginc, the duties, and, as above stated, if Mr. Tunstall's clerks find other situations, their salaries will be paid until the 25th March next.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tunstall may imagine he knows more than the rest of us, butl am advised his duties are well under- stood, and have been thoroughly weighed. He seems to think there is no one capable of undertaking the rev - tration work, but as he took it up whilst a strange to it, as did also Mr. Smith, what they hay done I have no doubt, Mr. Fairhurst, Mr. Wood cock, and their stiff can also do. Mr. Tunstall had good friends on tee Parliamentary Committee, and the best was made of his case by them ,and in the opinion of the Finance Committee he Inty be very thankful he CA7IIO to terms with them rather than let it go to the Local Government Board. I fancy it would have eone differently with him there, for it must not be forgotten that the Local Governmrnt Board had noted an, re- psrted that he did not personally perform the duties of the office. This would have made an important differ- ence in assessing the compensation, for the Treasury is empowered to make such deductions as advisable from the statutory compensatioti allowed (which in hi case would have been about £182), when the dutlesof the appointment have not been such as to require theholder to give the whole of his time. Your readers willguess that when it was rearzed by the Treasury that Mr. Tunstall had given so little time the com- pensation would in all probability have been equally small. From this statement it will b. seen:how generously the Committee has setPd, and now it seems to me to rest with Mr. Tunstall to do the same. He ought not to attempt to shift his own harden and I trust his better nature will assert itself. The Co- poration are not parties to any " shabby transaction„ but if Mr. Tunstall fails to do hi, plain duty that charge must lie at his door. The conduct of these negotiation. has been no easy task. It was entered upon by th. Committee with a generous predisposition, for therm were professional and friendly good feelings prompting</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Utters to the Motor.</head>
        <p>Utters to the Motor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. blotters intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>THIC ASSI3TA.NT OVERSEERiHIP.</p>
        <p>Sir,--Your issue of Saturday contains a letter from Mr. J. L. Tunstall,in which he seeks to discredit certain statements made at the recent Council Meeting by Mr. Councillor F. W. Monks and myself. It is impossible to allow these statements to pass unnoticed and though I may have to go into detail, at some length, I think the public should have the opportunity of judging which version is correct. I need not refer to the strictures upon Mr. Monks' statement further than to say, that what he said with reference to Mr. Smith being the stumbling block is correct. Replying to questions from members of the Council 89 to Mr. Tunstall's pension, and the position of his clerks, I said amongst other things that the committee in no way recognised Mr. Tunstall's staff, on the ground that they were his private clerks, but that when the time came (viz., 25th of March next) the Corporation would make overtures to such of them as they found it desir- able to retain. Mr. Tunstall, in his letter, says in effect that my state- ment is not true, and that the Corporation not only agreed to take his clerks over, but that they are now actually in the employ of the Corporation, and that if any of them are dismissed the onus will not fall upon</p>
        <p>him. I desire firstly to make the statement that I was not the author of this movement to amalgamate the offices. It was neither suggested by nor originated with me, but as it came within the Financial Clauses, it fell to my lot to work up the details, and to be the chief mouthpiece of the Committee during the carrying of it out.—The necessity for a change began in my opinion so long ago as May, 1897, when the Local Government Board, dis- satisfied upon the report of their auditor, wrote the Board of Guardians advising a change. Further, all re- cent legislation clearly suggested amalgamating such offices, and later frill the Royal Commission on ratin distinctly urged it in its vport. Mr. Tunstali can never ne charged with neglecting his interests, for he has been able to influence the Cor poration from time to time, and so get his position strengthened in the various local provisional orders, lout everything pointed to this Bill giving the oppor- tunity for doing what Parliamentprejudged necessary. I was no:, present,and therefore cannot know what passed between Mr. Fairhurst and Mr. Tunstall, but Mr. Tun- gsten's published version is very different to that in the possession of the Committee, and as the Committee has no motive for varying its terms I still prefer to believe our view. Mr.. Tunstall presented the terms on which he would retire, consisting first, of a pension of two-thirds of his nett salary, a provision that his staff be taken over- and not dismissahle eirc,pt for misconduct or incompet- ency, and that Mr. Smith be appointed Assistant Over, seer and li,gistration Officer, and that Mr. Smith be also permitted to retain an outside appointment, viz., the position he holds as Assistant Overseer of Great Sankey. These show they were fairly onerous, but this was not all. A sub-committee was appointed consisting of Alder- man J. Fairclough, Councillor F W Monks and myself, and though we,thought two-thirds of his salary an ex- tremely liberal alto wance,we did not raise any objection thereto. It was the terms with reference to his clerks that gave us all the trouble. Mr. Tunstall said the staff must be taken over, but the sub-committee could not entertain it, and the matter Watil adjourned for him to further consider big proposals. Subsequently the sub- committee and Mr. Tunstall met again, when after some discussion Mr. Tonstall, though slightly modifying his terms as to his clerks' service, fined° it a coedition that they must be taken over, and the negotiations thereupon broke off. Mr. Tunstall was however, at this interview distinctly informed that the Committee would proceed with the Bill, and leave to the Local Government Board the assessment of his compensation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>3 14 15 16 17 18</head>
        <p>3 14 15 16 17 18</p>
        <p>3 114 115 116 1 17 118 1 19 120</p>
        <p>lects to win in the</p>
