      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER O. 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER O. 1899.</p>
        <p>liberal treatment, and he has got it. We were more than once strongly urged to gi re up, bat a sense of public duty compelled us to stick to the clauses. We have also heard of personal motives being imputed, which were as ungenerous as untrue, but I truA that now the question is settld our 2ctioc will be seen in a better light.—l am, yours truly, J. SHAW GREEN.</p>
        <p>Sit,—ln your issue of Satuidav last,Mr. J, L. Tunstall takes exception to the explanation given by me at the last meeting of the Council. I have now to (*Arco the report as bt ing c arrect,and to add that the whole of the negotiations between Mr. Tunstall and the sub-zomcnittee have been on the one que.tion of Mr. Tunstail's staff. The ruh-eanamittee agrefd at once to Mr. Tunstall's claim for of the nett emoluments of the office, but declined to entertain or acknJwledge any liability in respect of Mr. Smith and the other clerks in the office.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tunstall bad two interviews with the sub-com- mittee, but subsequent negotiations were carried on by Mr. F. R. Roberts. It was however not until the Town Clerk had received froth the Local Government Board their report, approving the principle of the Clauses in the Bill 4ffecting Mr. Dingell, thtt his repre,eatative waived all stipulations as to the clerks and finally acc-pted the terms originally offered on behalf of the Corporation. Knowing the desire of the sub-committee to treat Mr. Tongan not only fairly but generoully,l do not consider he was justified in writing as be did, and feel sure your readers will concur with me in,this opinion of his letter. —Yours truly, F. W. MONKS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>3, Winn:meal/it-street, October 12;b, 1899.</head>
        <p>3, Winn:meal/it-street, October 12;b, 1899.</p>
        <p>THE REV. H. G. ROBERTS' SERMON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A letter under the above heading, appeared in your contemporary (the "Guardian").on Wednesday last. Kindly permit me space in your eclumus, to state how completely 'differ from the views expressed therein by the writar who signed himself "Steward and Trustee." As a member of the congregation,' fully appreciated the remarks ot our worthy pastor, for although lam far from being a Jingo, or, even a supporter of the present Government' yet I, with many other Bold-3teeet atten- dants, am fully in accord wih their action in the pre- 'sent crisis, and thoroughly endorse the rev. gentleman's utterances thereon, believing as I d), in British subjects everywhere enjoying the same privileges. It is not my intention however to discuss the Trans- ' veal question in this letter. But allow me to say that if this Pillar of our Church had considered his positioo,and the peace and harmony of Bold-street Wesieyane, be would not have discussed his pastor's remarks through the columns of the press. "Seward and Trustee "says that we require earnest preaching and work to fill our chapel. 1 venture to say, that no more earnest preacher and worker than the Rev. H. G. Roberts ha 3 been in this circuit for years ; the fact of large congregations when be occupies the pulpit is proof sufficient oi this. The writer's allusion to the Rev. H. G. Roberts' remarks upon St. Paul, and Tne3logy's indebtedness, to him (sir. Roberts) is too sarcastic, and unchristian, t 3 be worthy of further notice. Steward and Trustee windi up his epistle by rebuking the Rev, gentle- man for being so " unmanly " as to attack President Kruger from the pulpit, where he is sate from reply. And yet he hides himself behind a nom-de- plume, and rushes into print to attack his pastor. Wonderful consistency. Surely he might have discussed the matter over with Ar. Roberts, winla manly enough to :lava met him, and who would have given him credit for his convietio ns. Tnis would have been the wiser policy, and would not have sown the seeds of dissension in our midst.—Yours tee., Wilderspool Causeway.</p>
        <p>E. PLINSTON.</p>
        <p>"TRY KLNGDOM COME."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the one and, so far as I know, the only form of prayer, whicci the Lord ever taught to his disciples the petition, quoted above, occurs. And during the nineteen centuries which have intervened since that prayer was delivered. Christian men in all lands and in all ages hive used it in their devotions. But for some reason or other known only to the supreme disposer of events the Kiagdom, that is tee reign of peace and righteousness on the earth foretold by the Hebrew Seers has not yet coma. I know, of course, that many zealous and well-meaning people say that the Church is toe Kingdom to which the Lerd referred. But, with all beceming deference, I would ask what is the Church? It it is the agglomeration of religious sects which now assume that name then indeed I must de- cline to recognise the coenbined sects as the Kingdom. And why ? Because it must be obvious to the most casual observer that the sects do not exhibit much of the peace and righteousness welch shall distinguish the heavenly reign. Most gladly, how- ever, do I admit that, scattered through all the various denominations, and even outside of them altogettur, , there are men and women wbo, seekine after righteous- ness, experience the inward peace which the world can neither give nor take away. But these are at best but " a little flock." first fruits as it were of the happy and glorious time when the Lord "Shall judge the world with righteousness and the people with his truth." Nor will the kingdom ever be intro limed or established by hurran methods. In all past ages men of superier minds have appeared and striven to raise their lelow beines to a higher level, and to impart to them a clearer knowledge ot the duties and respousibilitiee of life. But the light thus displayed 13Ang dim and fitfuloind limited in sphere, soli' pissed away to be followed by other schemes equally futile. The natural condition of men is such that it is absolutely hopeless to expect that they will ever be able to reform themselves. A radical and entire change of mini and disposition must be accom- plished in them before they can desire or become sub- jects of the heavenly kingdom. And, of course, that change can only be wrought by a Power infinitely greater than themselves. But it is often ,aid that the preaching of the gospel will bring about toe Millennium, and that all that is now needed to hasten its advent is more money to ' endow more preachers. But bow can we believe this , with the light of history before our eyes ! We have had nineteen centuries experience of that preaching at a cost of money beyond calculation, but where are its effects to be seen to-day.? The world is still 'full of evil, the strong dominate over the weak, the poor are oppressed and despised, and strong drink is sipping the very foundations of society. And all this wickedness fleurishes in the countries which build state churchfs and richly endow state priests while, at the same time. they are striving with each other for supremacy in the diabolical arts of destroying human life. Surely in the face of all this it would be absurd t hope that the gospel that is now preached will ever do much to ameliorate the lot of mankind. And yet, notwithstanding my strong clnviction that the world will never be converted by human instru men- talities, I nevertheless believe that a full and fearless preaching of Christ's gospel, as taught and exemplified by himself would act powerfully for good. If preachers feelingly and persistently told their bearers that the oppression of the poor and the weak was a heinous sin in the sight of God, churches and chapels might be thinned a little, hut we should have fewer cries for" a living ware." and less sorrow and distress in the homes of our workers. And further, if the men who pose before the world as messengers of the Prince of Peace were imbued with His spirit they could do some- thing to prevent war. The glory of Christ's gospel is that it is a gospel of love, mercy and forgiveness, and a faithful and consistent preaching of it should make the professors of Christianity ashamed t 3 be seen in league with the shedders of human blood.—Yours truly, lONA. 14th October, 1899.</p>
        <p>1* THE—DB-A.F.—A gentlema7u who cured' himself of Deafness and Noises ,n the Head after fourteen years suffer- ing will gladly send full particulars of the remedy, on receipt of stamped addressed envelope.—F. CLIFTON, Ambarley House, Strand, London, W.O.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The firet of the weekly meetings of the PS A. Society was held on Sunday afternoon in the Co- operative Hall, Earlemowu, when there was a large attendance. Mr. J. Jackson presided, and an ex- cellent address was given by Mr. Webster. Mrs. Frances gave one or two recitations and the vocal- ist was Miss Watkin. Miss Profit presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>BICYCLE ACCIDENT AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>BICYCLE ACCIDENT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday at noon when Mr. B. Lane of War- grave-road was cycling down Fairclough-street in the direction of home, he collided with another cyclist who was turning into Fdirclough-street. The re-iult was that the latter was pitched head first into the hedge, and Lane's bicycle was badly damaged. Neither of the riders received any in- jury. A NARROW SHAvE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning a young fellow named Miff; employed as a joiner at the Viaduct Works, Earles- town, had a narrow escape from severe injury, if not from death. He was working on an ovorhead travtlling crane. when he overbalanced, and fell from the crane. In his fall he fortunately managed to secure a rope hanging from the crane, and was soan dangling between the crane and the ground. Hal it not been for the rope he would have fallen heid first on a heap of bricks, a distance of 30 or 40 feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>POLICE CASES.</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Eirlestown Polica Station on Monday morning before Mr. J. W. Emmett. Thomas Knight and Niartin Rafferty were charged with being drunk on Saturday and were each fined 2s. 6d. and cots —Mary Broderick for being drunk on Saturday night was fined 24. 61. including costs.—William Calvert for being drunk early that morning was sent to gaol for seven days, in default of paying a tine of 2s. 61 and costs.</p>
        <p>No. 1 CONVENTION, S.W. LANCASHIRR GOOD</p>
        <p>TF,MPL sits.</p>
        <p>The monthly session was held at the Lecture Hall, Havdock, on Saturday. Bro. A. Francis pre- sided over a good attendance of representatives and visitors from Ashton,Sutton. St. lielens,Earlesbown. Wigan and Warrington. The monthly report was given by Bro. E. Newman, C Sec., who referred to most of the lodges and Temples in the area doing active work in the temperance cause. The reports of lodge representatives were good, showing a num- ber of visits made to the various towns. The next session to be held at Atherton in November. Pro- gramme for that iLeeting; a paper by Bro.J. Turner of St. Helens. After tea a public meeting was held. Bro. Plant of Havdock, presided and gave an address. Bro. Vint of Ashton, also spoke, and a musical prozramme was rendered by members and friends of the order. FIRE AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at one o'clock a fire broke out in a large wooden building, situate behind Mercer- street, Newton, and occupied by Mr. H. Houghton, coal dealer, as stables and cart sheds. The wool composing the building was dry, and had recently received a coating of tar, there also being a tar bar- rel in the stable, together with a quantity of hay. In a few minutes the fitmes had enveloped the structure, and before two o'clock, it was burnt to the ground. A telegraph pole standing at one corner of the building was set on fire, and the sparks were also carried to the thatched roof of an outbuilding adjoining a cotta e belonging to Lord Newton and occupied by Mrs. liolgate, setting fire to the thatch. The hose was played on the roof and very soon the fire was extinguished. The dam- age is estimated at £6O, and is not covered by in- surance. It is alleged that the fire originated through some boys igniting paper behind the build- ing, which set fire] o some hay, and soon tbewbole place was ablaze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NEWTON-LB-WILLOWS BOWLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>NEWTON-LB-WILLOWS BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The closing bawling handicap of the season was brought to a conclusion on Saturday last. Alto- gether there were 63 entries arid as the prize list was extensive, eh player obtained a prize. At the close those who had taken part in the handicap adjourned to the Pied Bull Hotel, where an excel- lent knife and fork tea was partaken of, between 60 and 70 being present. After the cloth bad been removed, the proceedings were presided over by Mr. Robert Stone J.P., supported by the Rev. J. Ryder, Meslrs. Hindley,Crouchley, Sankey, riodmer, &amp;c. The Chairman, after the usual loyal toasts had been duly hov.oßred, took an opportunity of pre- senting to Mr. Henry Helcroft, a beautiful marble clock, as his (the chairman's) prize, which was towled for a week or two ago. The prizes for the general handicap were also distributed by the Chairman, the first four winners being,l,J William- son; 2, W Gee; 3,J Galloway; 4, T Fairclough. The toast of the Chairman which was proposed by the Rev. J. Ryder, was duly honoured. The toasts "Treasurer and Secretary "were heartily honoured, and suitably responded to by Mr. Hindley and Mr. Gee respectively. The harmony of the evening was contributed to by Messrs W Huddleton, R Carson, S Swann. and W Liptrott. Mr E Leyland presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN INDEPENDENT LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>The members of the local branch of the I.L.P. opened their winter session on Tuesday evening by a lecture in the Co-op-rative Hall, Wargrave-read garlestown on "Sociali-m and Individual Liberty,' by Mr. Phillip Snowdon: Mr. W. Speakman pre- sided but there was only a moderate attendance. The Chairman said that night they started a new session, and they would endeavour during the elm- ing winter to provide in that room every week, a lecture something similar to what would be given that night, or a paper would be read by 901113 of their members. He spoke of the progress made by the I.L P. and said that the number of their members which had bee n returned on the municipal bodies of the country was a matter of sincere gratification. It was quite a new movement and had not been known long in this countiy, but when compared with the progress made in other countries, they omen to be very proud; He was pleased to find that the Trade Unionists were be- coming alive to the importance of having labour representation in the House of Commons as that was the proper place in which they could fight their battles. The Lecturer then proceeded with his discourse, which was of much interest to those present, dealing as it did, with the relation of capital to collectivism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting was held at the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave-road,on Monday. Bro Cutter P.C.T., presiding. Sim Woods. and Bros Willis. and Wilson appointed to arrange for anniversary concert. Bro Wilson asked the lodge to sanction the formation of a mouth organ band to interest the younger mem- btrs and on the motion of Bro Woods it was resolved to sanction the formation of a mouth organ band Ito consist of members of the lodge only and Bro I Wilson was authorised to collect subscriptions in aid of it. It was resolved to present a mouth organ to each member of the band. Sisters War- burton, Smith, Ord, Thomas and !Grant and Bra's Warburton, Machlin, and Leather- barrow were appointed to arrange anniver- sary tea. The Hope of Sutton Lodge who were visitinathen gave the following programme; song, Bro Myers; song, Sister Jones; reading, Bro Price; song," The song that reached my heart," Sister Jones; duet, Sister Mrs and Miss Jones,which was encored ; song." In the Army," Bro Pillinger ; and for an encore "Rocked in the Cradle of the Deep," song, "Somebody's Child," Bro Price. A hearty ' vote of thanks was then given to the visitors for their entertainment and the Lodge was closed by Bro Cutter at 945.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>The Boer Repulse at Mafeking.—A Dashing Encounter.</p>
        <p>CAPETOWN, THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Reliable new has came through trom Maleking. It gives further details of tha receut B-ter &amp;feat. Teer2is no longer any cl,tubt but that the Boors made an attack and were c mipleztely repulsed. Colonet Hore led the sortie and the dash against the enemy. Colonel Eirts brought his Maxima into play upon the enemy, and they ciid heavy execu,:ion. Colonel liore's movement is described as a dashing one. The news has been received here with enthusiasm. A message has also come through this morning from Kimberley. The tone there was cheerful when the despatch was sent away. The nazia ware eager, and only waiting an opportunity to get at the enetn7 and deliver their blow. A" Cape Times" despatch from Kimberley states that, accorling to trustworthy information from Mafe- king, an armlureci train reconnoitred on Saturday last to the north, and engaged 500 Boers, who suffered heavily. The Fitzclarence column foiled the Boers, inflicting severe loss upon them. The British casualities were two killed and fourteen wounded, two severely. Fighting near Tintwa Pass. LADYSMITH, WEDNESDAY,</p>
        <p>The cavalry patrol below Tintwa Pass had a brush with the enemy last night. The Boers opened fire with their artillery but retreated on the advance of the Fifth Lincers. Cavalry scouts found that the Boers bad ad- vanced to a small berg seven miles from the foot of the Tint wa Pass. They came oa a commando estimated to be 700 or SOO strong, with a field battery. The Boers opened fire and sent several shots. Their aim,bowever, was wild, and no one was injured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Battle Imminent.</head>
        <p>Battle Imminent.</p>
        <p>To-day the Boers are advancing fram both sides, east and west. The advance guards are getting sharply engaged, our patrols covering a wide area to prevent flanking. They are subjected to a hot fire in the per- sistelt 51ctrrni lung. Sir George White expects the enemy's advance will draw on a general engagement, and is prepared to offer battle, but the main force will not leave Ladysmith. The prospect of a definite blow being struck very soon arouses much satisfaction in camp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Crewe Apprentices to the Rescue.</head>
        <p>Crewe Apprentices to the Rescue.</p>
        <p>One of the rai-Iviay reservists, Sapper Evans, called out at Crewe, ;vie the sopport of his widow mother, and his going away deprived her of her means of livelihood. This caused both much anxiety. The premium apprentices of Crews railway works, however, got to hear of it, and rejoiced the heart of the gallant soldier by assuring him that during his absence at the war they would take care of his mother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>Departure of Sir Redvers Buller.—Destruction of the Armoured Train.</p>
        <p>Sir Redvers Buller and his principal staff officers left London for Southampton on Saturday amidst scenes of great enthusiaam, the Princ3 of Wales and the Head- quarters Staff seeing him away from Waterloo, and the Mayor attending officially at the growing southern port. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The latest accounts of the wreck of the armoured train on the west of the Transvaal indicate that no one was killed, and only two were wounded, while the defenders were taken prisoners,</p>
        <p>The first Shots of the war on the Natal side were fired on Friday night, a patrol being shot at by a dozen Boers, probably local farmers. Happily none of the shots took any effect. A Boer pirtr on Saturday crossed the Buffalo River at De Jaager's Drift, just north of the Zulu frontier, and about twelve miles north-east of Dundee.</p>
        <p>Newcastle Occupied. Newcastle has been occupied by the Boers. Police Super- intendent Macdonald refused to leave his post, and was last seen walking along towards the Boers. A mounted policc-sergeant and five men with their hotses and arms have been captured by the Boers near Buffalo River. They were pounced upon quite unawares. Oae of the party had the good fortune to escape.</p>
        <p>Kimberley Invested by _ the Boers.</p>
        <p>CAPE TowN, SUNDAY..</p>
        <p>The Free Staters have destroyed the railways and tele- graphs at Norval's Pont, on the Bloemfontein line in Cape Colony, near Coleab-rg. At Modder Rivor, too they have interrupted rail and telegraphic communica- tion.</p>
        <p>Commandant Cr' nje invested Kimberley, which, how- ever, is strongly fortifisd and held. The British authori- ties have no fears for Kimberley. The water upply, it is true, is fifteen miles distant ; but there is an abun- danc9 of usable water from the mines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Fighting near Kimberley. Five Boers Killed and Several Wounded.</head>
        <p>Fighting near Kimberley. Five Boers Killed and Several Wounded.</p>
        <p>OAPETOWN, OCT. 17TH.</p>
        <p>An armoured train under the command of Lieutenant Webster, Lancashire Regiment, was sent out to recon- noitre. When it reached Spytfontein the Boer artillery began shelling it. They fired thirteen shots, but their shooting was wretched, and not a single shot struck the train, which thereupon approached nearer and opened tire with the maxims upon the burghers, who returned a heavy rifle fire. Their shooting, however, was again wild, and only three or four bullets struck the train. It was afterwards learnt that five Boers and two horses had been killed and several Boers wounded. Not one of the British was touched.</p>
        <p>Bridge Blown up by Imperial Troops. A teiegram to the " Cape Times" from Orange River station announeAs that no Imperial troop have blown up Hopstown Bridge. This is not the railway bridge, but another bridge situated twelve miles west of Orange River station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>German Ammunition for Boers.—</head>
        <p>German Ammunition for Boers.—</p>
        <p>Steamer followed by British Cruiser. PORT Saw, OCT. 17. _ _</p>
        <p>The Dautche Ost-Afrika Line steamer Kiser, from Hamburg. is disembarking at the entrance to the Suez Canal 4,000 cases ot ammunition for the Transvaal, in order to avoid seizure in the Red Sea by British cruisers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Fighting near Ladysmith.</head>
        <p>Fighting near Ladysmith.</p>
        <p>• LADYSMITH, WEDNESDAY. British troops came into contact with the enemy in the n.ighbourhood of Acton Homes and Beater's Station, about 16 milts out. The first shots were fired about ten this morning, and our cavalry patrols have been engaged with the Boers since midday. Some casualties are reported.</p>
        <p>THE WAR VOTE. £10,000,000 to be spent.</p>
        <p>_ A supplementary War Office estimate 'for ten millions sterling was irsued on Wednesday, showing that 35,000 additional men are required for the current financial year owing to the military situation in South Africa. This number, it is explained, r(presents the probable maximum CXC.I39 beyond the establishments for the year in consequence of the proclamation calling up a portion of the Army Reserve and the transfer temporarily of a number of troops from the Indian to the British estab- lishment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>THEFT BY AN EARLESTOWN BOY.</head>
        <p>THEFT BY AN EARLESTOWN BOY.</p>
        <p>James Henry Ashurst, Athol-street, Earlestown, aged 11 years was charged at the Warrington Petty Sessions on years, with stealing from a dwell- ing house at Havdock sometime between 9 a.m. on the 11th and 730 p.m. on the 12th inst., a watch value £5, the property of samuel Manchester.— Samuel Manchester, labourer, residing at 377, Clipsley-road, Haydock, said the watch produced was his property. He saw it safe in the house on the 11th inst., and missed it the following day. Ile gave information to the police.—Sarah Ann Man- chester (14),daughter of the prosecutor.said she saw the watch on the mantlepiece in the kitchen on Wednesday morning. the 11th inst. Shortly after- wards she was cleaning the front and heard foot- steps in the kitchen. She went in and saw the prisoner. He asked her for a piece of bread and butter and she gave him some. He then asked for an old pair of boots, but she did not give them to him.—P.S. Walker said that he apprehended the prisoner on the 12th inst at his father's hott,e, Athol-street, Earlestown. He searched the house, and in the pocket of an old pair of trousers which were in a bedroom he found the watch. He charged him with the theft and he re- plied" Yes. I stole the watch, I took it home and gave it to my brother to mind."—Henry Ashurst, the boy's father, said his eon was uncontrollable. He had previously stolen money from various places. He sugges!ed that he be sent away to some Institutior.—The magistrates decided to send prisener to a reformatory, where he will remain till he is 16 years of age. • NEW AUTUMN MILLINERY; Choice Selection of FURS. The latest in Jackets. and Waterproofs. Blouses, Dress Material, /cc. now showing—W. T. WILLIAMS, Sankey-street, and Legh-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>S ATCHELL'S CLUB HATS. THE BEST THE WORLD CAN PRODUCE. PRICES 7S. 61). AND 10S. 6D.</p>
        <p>481 LLORD-STREET' IVERPOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. BEST BOOTS FoR ALL, UNDER THE CLOCK.—Ado UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>OUR COMMANDERS.</head>
        <p>OUR COMMANDERS.</p>
        <p>" M.A.P " publisheA the folio wing character sketches of some 01 our commanders in tho Trans- vaal.</p>
        <p>General Sir Redvers Buller.</p>
        <p>Sir RedversDer's fitness for the job now en- trustsd to him cannot be questioned. He is the best soldier of his standing available for the present crisis. Lord Wolseley's time for field service is pretty well over, so is that of Lord Roberts. The Sirdar can afford to wait for his next great chance, and,seriously speaking,he has not Buller'- qualifica- tions for this particular business. Buller knows his trade by heart, is up to every move, can handle every tool with the perfected skill of the master craftsman. A British general called to high com- mand has more to do than march acid countermarch to bring his enemy to battle and meet him. He has to manipulate the threads of the somewhat cumbrous military machinery that holds him tight, and may often hamper and hinder him from a distance. He is never quite free of War Office control. for he is at the end of the wire, and may always expect inter- ference. Not the least of Buller's merits is that he knows our War Office by heart, for he has been closely associated with it for years as a 'military staff officer in the highest grades. He Ras a pro- found contempt for it, too. His evidence given be- fore the committee for decentralising War Office work, and giving more initiative to the generals commanding districts and stations at home and abroad, was quite amusing in its outspoken ridicule of War Office methods. Buller is no courtier, but he has always been popu- lar with the Royal Family. There is a strong bond of personal attachment between him and the Duke of Cambridge. He was long the trusted friend and right hand of the late Commander-in Chief, to such an extent that he was practically the head of the army for years. It was then, no doubt, that Sir H. Campbell-Bannerman, as Secretary for War, con- ceived that high opinion of him which so nearly ' made him the Duke's successor. Buller could ,-Ity anything to the Duke. A year after the latter's re- tirement, he was sitting in Buller's room (the Duke could not keep away from the War Office at first ; the habit had become ingrained) bemoaning his hard lot, when Buller turned on him and said: "Come, sir, admit that you are ever so much better since you went out of harness ?" "Upon my soul you're right !" admitted the Duke, with a loud laugh: "I believe it has added years to my life !" Buller is seen at his best in his own house, whether at his country place or, till lately, in Bruton-street and Government House, Aldershot. There's no sign there of his somewhat brusque ab- rupt surface manner, which many were inclined to call rude. He is the kindliest and most hospitable of men, and likes nothing better than to welcome his friends, and particularly an old friend, to his bout He has no greater joy than to make a night of it, talking everything out, past and present and to come, till a very late hour in the snug privacy of his study, when he lets out freely, not over pipe or cigar, for he is-no smoker, and of late he is very abstemious in fluids. As a case in point, showing his fondness for sitting up, be was to be seen and heard" cracking: in the Beefsteak Club till half past one last SaturAay morning, the very day of his departure. He is, as a matter of fact, satisfied with very little sleep, and has often been heard to declare that he hates going to bed. A strong constitution and great physical energy are inestimable natural gifts to the campaigner. Sir George White. Sir George White, weo commands the Natal forces, is an Irish officer, who did all his regimental service and won his way to distinction in a High- land regiment. Like Buller, he wears that coveted "bit of bronze," the Victoria Crisis. He won it in Afghanistan, in the hard-fought battle of Charasiah, twenty years ago. White told tie Goidons that he counted on them to carry a hill with the bayonet. Up they went, two companies in front, two more in support, and the Gazette tells what followed, in recording White's claim to the Cross. " Advancing with two companies of his regiment," it says," and climbing from one steep ledge to another, he came upon a body of the enemy strongly posted, and out- numbering his force by eighteen to one. His wen being much exhausted, and immediate action being necessary, Major White took a rifle, and going on by himself, shot dead the leader of the enemy." When White fired, the Afghan was rushing upon him sword in hand, trying to bring his men on to the charge. If they had come on, mere weight of numbers might have hurled the handful of High- landers down the hill. But the fall of the fanatic chief produced something like a panic among his people, and the Highlanders, cheering wildly for their heroic leader, rushed on with level bayonets. All was over in a few moments. The ride was won, and the deadly rifles of the victors were busy shoot- ing down the flying foe. If White had not been given the Victoria Cross on this occasion, he would have won it at the battle of Candahar, when,charg- ing again at the head of the Gordons, he captured one of the enemy's guns. For this also he was named for the Cross. Sir George White is a tall soldierly looking man, with a handsome face and a very charming manner that has won him hosts of friends. He has had always in his favour a re- markably strong constitution, and is still, to use the popular phrase, "as hard as nails." About a year ago, when another distinguished general was temporarily invalided through a bad fall, a friend of both remarked to White that he doubted if the injured officer would ever again be fit to mount a horse, for he had broken his leg in six places. "Why should not he get quite well," said White cheerily, "he has only broken his leg in six places. I once broke one of mine in seven, and I don't think I am any the worse for it." General Gatacre.</p>
        <p>General Gatacre, who commands the Third Divi- sion in South Africa, is one of our youngest generals lie first won distinction in Chitral, and then had the good fortune to be sent out to the Upper Nile to command the British brigade that fought at the Atbara. This was a step to the command of the British division at Omdurman. Gatacre has the re- putation of being rather a hard man. lie holds that plenty of work is the best way of keeping soldiers in good condition, and in the Soudan he sometimes seemed to go out of his way to invent work for his brigade or division. He is a " first- rate fighting man." At the Atbara he charged in the first rank, and was himself busy pulling down the thorn edge of the zereba, when a Dervish rifle was aimed at him at close quarters. He went on tugging away at the mass of thorns, simply saying to the man next him : " :Shoot that fellow," and the Dervish was dropped before he could make up his mind to fire. When the zereha hedge was passed, and the hand to hand tight began Gatacre was in the thick of it, and ran one of the enemy through with his sword. Some very good soldiers still doubt if he was in his right place in this bcau sabreur performance, and say that a brigadier, even during the assault, ought not to be out in front fighting like a company officer, but in the midst of his brigade where he can bring up support to a weak point or check any temporary disorder. As a divisional commander the brave young general will be safe from temptations to such displays of personal prowness. In this same Atbara tight, Gatacre showed in one point distinct originality. It is generally laid down that it is usele, s for soloiers to fire without halting while actually moving forward. But Gatacre trained his brigade to fire while on the move, and during the advance on the zereba there were Yolles from the foremost ranks. "Do you suppose that many Dervishes were hit?" Gatacre was asked by a critic of this novel departure. "I am sure that we hit very few," he replied, " but I am quite as sure that we prevented them from hitting a good many of us. If we had halted to fire, we should have lost time. If we moved on without firing, they could have taken many a cool shot at us. But our fire must at least have made them jumpy and spoilt their aim a bit. So I think we did the right thing." Lieut.-Col. Baden-Powell.</p>
        <p>Lieut-420 . Baden Powell, who commands at Mafe- king, is a cavalry officer with o plod record as a soldier. Be is a strong, wiry-looking man, with a face that tells of restless energy and keen deter- mination. His best work was done as a leader of iry.gular troops in the Matabele war of 1896 His official position was that of chief of the staff to General Fir Frederick Carrington, but he was a kind of soldier-of-all-work, now dealing with papers and returns in his office at Buluwayo, now riding beside his chief as principal staff ( fficer, now com- manding a detached column, now scouting with a handful of native trackers, or going off alone on a dangerous errand to spy out the places of the Matabele impis in the Matoppo strongholds. As a scout he is unrivalled among white men. He has taken the trouble to learn the niceties of the art from white frontiers- men and black trackers, and he speaks of it as the most exciting ano engrossing of sports. The Matti- bele used to call him by a name in their own lan-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>OUR NEW SERIAL.</p>
        <p>OUR NEW SERIAL.</p>
        <p>We have pleasure in announcing we have arranged for publication in our columns a brilliant New Story, entitled</p>
        <p>44 NOBLER THAN REVENGE,"</p>
        <p>by that Popular Writer ESME STUART,</p>
        <p>Author of</p>
        <p>" A Faire Daniozel," " An Inscrutable Mystery," "A Woman of Forty,"" Married to Order," " Sara's</p>
        <p>Mother."" In the Dark," etc.</p>
        <p>4Gp NT- IN OBLER THAN REVENGE."</p>
        <p>" NOBLER THAN REVENGE."</p>
        <p>i• 6 xi- -11 OBLER THAN REVEisTGE."</p>
        <p>Our new story is one of Vie most thril:ing this charming novelist has yet written. It abounds in strong character drawing, vivid scene painting, and dramatic incident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>SOMETHING ABOUT THE STORY.</head>
        <p>SOMETHING ABOUT THE STORY.</p>
        <p>On his way to visit his con sin, Sir Harry Beauwortb, Alec Beauworth, heir to the title, is set upon by two men and knocked senseless. Sit Harry Beanworth has a bad reputation. Bob Rabbitts, son of the evil-looking landlord of the " Dog and Duck," revives Alec, and de- termines, as he has the chance, to enter Sir Harry's services for a time to see that Alec comes to no harm. Sir Harry has one daughter, Ruby, a beautiful and un- sophisticated girl, who has never been allowed to see the world, and whom her father intends to marry to an Australian. James Felton, who with his father. Tom Felton; appears to have a powerful interest over him. These Feltons are the men who tried to kill Alec, though he never saw their faces, and it is obvious that they acted at the instig %Mon ef the baronet, who had some secret reason for wishiag to be rid of the heir. Alec, when he sees Ruby, falls in love with her, and not knowing his danger determines, if possible, to stay and win her.</p>
        <p>Though he has no intention of allowing Ruby to marry Alec, Sir Harry Beauworth, plays upon Alec's affections in order to induce him to make a will in Ruby's favour. Alen consents, and hereafter his destruction is practically decided upon by Sir Harry and the Felton'a, so as to pave the way for a division of all the money and the estates which" in the ordinary course, would have fallen to Alec. The baronet is in really personiting the real baronet who died many years ago in the Bush and the Feltons, knowing this secret, blackmail Sir Harry Beauwor, h and make him an ally in their nefarious schemes. With the assistance of a movable floor in his bedrooat Alec is preci- pitated into a dungeon and is left there to starve. His thrilling attempts to escape and the subsequent adven- tures watch befall hint and the heroine Ruby impart a Monte Cristo flavour to this romance which draws to a. spirited and satisfactory conclusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-10-20_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ra IN THAN REVENGE," WILL 00MIENCII ON FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3RD.</head>
        <p>ra IN THAN REVENGE," WILL 00MIENCII ON FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3RD.</p>
